        <p>WIDNES 31UNICIPAL ELECTION, 1893. TO THE BURGESSES OF SUMS CROSS WARD.</p>
        <p>LADIES AND GF:NTLFMEN, A year ego you honoured me with your confidence by electing me as ene of the representatives of this Ward. In accordance w:th the laws and us ges of munit Ipal Government my term of offise now exi ires, and in response to the influential requisition of numerous burgesses of the Ward. I have consented to offer myself for re-election. In doing so I venture to ask you to believe that I have laboured to the utmost of my ability, and with the best intentions, for the good of all Fections of the community. With this object in view, I intend to persevere in the future as in Use past. Amongst the labours of the year. I have taken special in- terest in the proposed new Technical Schools and Fres Lib- rary, which, we are expecting will be erected in the near future. The finances of the Borough have also received much of my attention, and in this department a considerable an- nual saving has been effected. I ale aVe have be n, and shall continue to be Interested llt all public questions, especially those affecting the intetests of the working c'asses—better education, -better homes in which to dwell, and clean and welt-lighted streets, being, ill my opinion, necessary condi- tisns to life :tad health. In conclusion, if you think me worthy of a res‘owal of your, confidence, support me with your vote and interest on the first November. lam, Ladies and Gentlemen, Yours faithfully, SAMUEL OWENS.</p>
        <p>MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS, 1893. ST. JOHN'S WARD. TO TDB BURGESSES OF TDB WARD. LADIES AND GENTLEMEN, My term of office having now expired I agtin offer my services to you. I have, to the best of my ability, done my duty as a Coun- cillor for the Ward, and fulfilled my promises to you, as evideaced by the state of your Streets and Sanitation at the time of any e`ec'ion, and t`,eir present state. I therefore ask you for your continued confidence and sup- port, and hope you will return me on the day of Election by a large majority. I beg to remain, Indies and Gentlemen, Yours obediently, WM. JAS. SHAW.</p>
        <p>.89, DaHam-lane, October 1401, 1893.</p>
