      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0004" n="art0004">
        <head>AIUNICIPAL ELECTION, 1893. ORFORD WARD. TO THE BURGESSES OF THE WARD.</head>
        <p>AIUNICIPAL ELECTION, 1893. ORFORD WARD. TO THE BURGESSES OF THE WARD.</p>
        <p>LIMES AND GENTLEMEN, , lam requested by a Lug, and influential numb.r of ratepayers (of both politics) in your Ward, to allow myself to become a Candisllte to re present and guard your interests in the Town Council, and I have ac ceded to the request. I have spent.the whole of my life In your town, and have very considerable interests at stake in the Borough. I am a determined advocate of economy in the spending of Public Moneys. and am of an opinion that every step possible should be takel to reduce the exorbitant Bates that we at present have to pay, By so doing we considerably increase the possiblity sf New Works and Industries so inging up in our midst rendered so probable by the early opening of the Ship Caual, The coining year will be a very importaa t one: financially for Wars ington. Already a scheme is prepared involvlug the expenditure of some th'rty or forty thousand r MIS ds. This, scheme requires very careful watching , as I consider tl e present time a very inopportune one for spending so much money, and any part of it that can well be postponed ought to be. .</p>
        <p>There are many other Important public matters about which I could with to express my opinions and I hope to have the pleasure of doing so In public meting at an early data, if you will b 3 kind enough to honor me with your pre- sence there'd.</p>
        <p>I promise you. if electe,, that everytling that may be brought forward for the goo I and well-being of this, our Town, and of Orford Ward in pArtieular, shall tame my cordial and emphatic support. Believe me to be, Ladies and.Gentlemeri,</p>
        <p>Faithfully yours. WM. P. SKELTON. Iforsemarket-st., Warrington, 19th October, 1893.</p>
        <p>I‘IUN ICIPAL ELECTION, 1893.</p>
        <p>TO TIIE ELECTORS OF THE FAIRFIELD WARD.</p>
        <p>LADIES AND G ENTLEM EN , -</p>
        <p>At the request of numerous friends and ratepayers. of different political opinions, I have consented to place my services at your disposal, as one of the reaesentatives of the Ward in the Town Council. •</p>
        <p>Asa resident in the ward, an emp'over of labour, and a large ratepayer, lam more likely to understand the require- ments of this ward in wrticular, and the town in general, than a non-resident in the borough. lam aware that there are several Important matters affecting this ward especially. which require Immediate attention. I refer to ..he sewerage of Padgate Lane district, tc the evils arising from the present system of sewer ventilation, and to the wretched condition of the pavemt nt of Scowl Brow, and the adjacent thorough- fares. As en owner of property it will be my endeavour to keep down the rates, wh ch at the present time‘re very excessive, and enablt the working otasses to have decent 'and respect- able homes at moderate and fair rents.</p>
        <p>To these and other matters pertaining to the Ward, should 3ondo me the. honour of returning me as one of y, ur repre- s,qttatives in the Town Councl, I will devote my very best attention, without fear or. favour, irrespective of party. I aid, Ladles and Gentlemen, Faithfully y,,urs, JOSEPH MOUNFIELD. 60, Manchester-road, October 20th, 1803.</p>
        <p>(election can ressto.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0005" n="art0005">
        <head>WARRINGTON MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS, 1393</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS, 1393</p>
        <p>TO THE BURGESSES OF THE HOWLEY WARD.</p>
        <p>LADIFS AND a ENTLF.MEN,</p>
        <p>At the desire of the Conservative Committee. I lave consented to become a candidate for your suffrages at the forthcoming Municipal Elections. But, before doing so, I informed them that, although my political sympathies are with the Conservative party, I should always act, especially In municipal matters, in an independout spirit. If you are willing to accept me on these conditions, it will give me pleasure to do the best I can to promote the welfare of the ward to which I belong, and of the town with which all my interests are Identified. I believe that, with the openiag of the Ship Canal, a new era of progress and prosperity awaits " the good old town." But whether we are to reap the full advantages of our posi- tion will larg •ly de, end upon the composition of the Town camel!. In order to attract new industries, an Iso to pro- vide more labour and better pay, low rates ere essential I am, therefore, in favour of retrenchment wherever it is at- tainable without the sacrifice of efficiency. But it is possible to be " penny wise and pound foolish," and there are many lodal Improvements which are urgently needed, and which I should heartily support, subject always to a due regard for the pockets of the ratepaprs. We need broad streets, more open spaces, improved municipal accom- modation, and a central abattoir; and I am strongly in fav- our of the removal of the Town Hall wall, and the gradual demolition of all insanitary property. Complaints about the van nuisance, the unpaved and unswept streets, and the intercepting d -po' s, have been far too frequent ; and the recurrence of an epidemic such as that from which we have so recently been 'offering ought to be prevented by the ' strictest attention to everything that concerns the health of the town. We ought to be able to make good gall, either at a low rate or a handsome i rout; and, at any time of acute distress. I think it desirable to enlistthe services of the unemployed in works of public utility. In a idition to this, I wish to see mereattention veldt° the artistic, Intellectual, and social necessities of the town. Our Art Gallery ought to contain some of the best works of our local artists ; and no effort should be spared to popularize the Museum, the Library, and the Technical Institute. Any- thing that will tend to brighten the lives of the toilers of our population, and beautity the place In which they live, will have a warm claim upon my sympathy. I a ish to see the borough rich, and populous, and busy. But even more than this do I desire to see its streets wide and wholesome, its river pure, its atmosphere healthy, and its constantly in- creasing area the abode of a prosperous. public-spirited, and enlightened people.</p>
        <p>lam. La,llea and Gentlemen, - Youra faithfully, ARTHUR 13ENNETT.</p>
        <p>Market Gate Chambers, Wavrington, October 18th,1893.</p>
      </div>
