        <p>Copy of unsolisited Teatimonial, selected out of a number received. Hawthorne Villa, Llnthorpe, Michilesbro'. Oct. 29, 1892. Mr- M. M. Lawson, Liverpool. DEAR SIR,-1 have used some of the "Lawson's Black" on my drawing and dining room fire grates. which my husband brought me to try, with the object of saving the servants the tedious work of blackleading. and I think it only right to comply with his desire to inform you that the trial has been most satisfactory in every respect; not only is the saving In time reduced more than three-fourths, and the appearance of the grates touch Improved as compared to blacklead, but the brush and holder you heve introduced en- ables those who use it to do so without in any way blacking or soiling their hands. I may also add that so far the appear- ance has not been affected try the heat of the fires.—Yours respectfully, JENNIE DOVE. Ask your Teaman, Grocer, or Chandler for It, And See that You Get It.</p>
