      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <p>.PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>A, Sennlmmo Lima num.—Your last shilling when its changed. THE physician is the man who tells you you •seed change, and then takes all you have. . EVEN the most temperate wsslierwennln has "three sheets in the wind" at times. AN ORIGINAL—A child was asked what mud was, and she said, "Duet is mud .with the juice squeezed out." THE male wasp never stings, bat so long as he and his sister are twins and dresa alike, this bit of knowledge availeth not. "LINN Doc.."—You'll shoot me "like a dog," you say? Where did you learn to shoot that way?" OLD TIBLIT3 IN A RAGE.—" That fellow ought to be kicked to death by a Jackr.ss, and I'd like to be the one to do it."</p>
        <p>NOT A Mile LOVER. —He : " I hear. you attended the Handel and tjaydn peeformancea. Were you present at the " Creation "?" She (indignantly: "I suppose you will next want to know if I sailed in Noah's Ark." FAMILY Purne.—" Tommy Figg," said the teacher, "you wrote this excuse yourself." "Yes," admitted Tommy. "You see, paw writes -scoh a poor hand 'at I felt 'shamed for you to see it." PAUL RS SOMETIMES NECESSARY. —ll3 re. De Fashion : "Where's the morning paper?" Mr. De F. "What on earth do you want with the morn ing paper?" Mrs. De Fashion: "I wish to see if the opera we heard last night was good or bad." Poeur.Aß SCI KNCE--He (of Boston) : "Professor Skihig h is going to lecture on sun spots to-morrow." She (of Chicago) : "Well, if I thought he could tell of a real, sure enough cure for them, I'd go to bear him. I freckle so easily." "WELL, James, how do you like the placer?' "Oh, first rate. Master is a periect gentleman. Why the way he throws his now clothes all about the place is wonderful 1" A CIIII.LING WELCOME.— Ugly-looking way farer : " Whar is the man o' the house?? Far- mer's Wife (with rare presence of mind) : "He's at the back of the woo ished burying a tramp." BRITISH WORKMAN (is per usual) : "Garn awaiy wi' yer ! I tell yer yez don't know our guv'ner. 'E wouldn't gi' yet 'alt a pint of ale, no—not 'e, not U 'e'd got a purse full on it !" WIFE (LO hust,and,-coming in under an umbrella, dripping): "Goodness, Harry, why did you come through all that rain?" Harry (pleasantly) : "Because, love, I couldn't come over it SINTINCED for bite, d'yer sajr? Arrah, thin, the judge had his eyes about him he might ha' seen that Dinnis was that delicate that he'd liver live to servo out a loife sintiuce, even if it .was only for three yr rs." A REGULAR CHATTERBOX. " What a talkative woman Mrs. Vatageur is" "Yes. Why, not long ago we were out driving on a cold day, and even her teeth ceattered." A CHAMELEON. "My wife's a changeable woman. She looked black when I asked her to marry me; she looked white when I aetually dkl marry her, and now she looks blue U I bring another man home to dinner." ANGELINA : "Are there any sharks found on the English coast, Eddie?" Edwin: "Often—lots.' Angelina ; "Good gracious! What kind Edwin: "Lodging-house keepers." HONKER: "Ever since I can remember, Miss Flypp, I have searched for the beautiful, the true, and the good." Miss Fiypp " Oh, Mr. Hunker, this is so sudden. But you may speak to papa." THE WAITER : "Beg pardon. sir; but--ahem! —the gents here usually remember my services." The Guest (scooping up all the change): "Do they? They ought to be more charitable and for. ' MRS. HOSTESS: "Won't you play something, Miss Tempo? Miss Tempo (caustically) : " No, thank you; I don't wish to interrupt the conversa- tion." Miss Hostess: "Oh, dear, Tempo, it wouldn't—not for a moment."</p>
        <p>BOOK AGENT (entering): "Madam, I have a work of art to ehow you. It is a book." Lady of the house (reopening the door): "And I have a . .</p>
        <p>. - work of art to show you. It is's landscape." SEE: "I an never forgive you. How did you dare to kiss me before the whole compiny ?" He: "Let my love be my excuse. I didn't knaw you would object so to being kissed." She: I don't. It was doing it before the company that angered As A NATURAL RESULT—Mrs. Sccondtime (pee- vishly) : My first husband used to let me have my own way in everything. Mrs. S. : And what were the consequences? de died. MR. VATTER (approaching lady in black) " Mrs. Jones, will you honour ma with.the first?" Mrs. Jones (with a slow smile) : " With pleasure, Mr. Vatter. But you must promise to dance very, very slowly, for I am still wearing mourning for dear Mr. Jones."</p>
        <p>" WHAT iS that big iron thing full of holes?" asked Laura. "Locomotive boiler." said Tom. Laura lookcd thoughtful. After a rnomeut's silence she asked. " Why do they boil locomotives?" lum looked amazed. "To make' em tender,' he said, slowly.</p>
        <p>IRATE CUSTOUER "Yon said this c!oth was a fast colour, yet it faded out in two werke after it was mare up." Dealer : " Well, I don t think you ought to expect it to fade any faster than that." POSiIBLY TIIE EXPLI.NATION.—He "Do you think I—aw—aliall have a good beard ?" Barber (after cose inspection): "I'm afraid not, sir." He: "Ah, weaily. My fawther has aw very fine beard, you know." Barber : "Maybe you take after your mamma, sir r NOT EEL,: FAULT.—" Mary," said her 'mother,</p>
        <p>"you'll have to make that Mr. Gelush leave earlier." "It's not my fault, mamma." "Not your fault? Didn't I hear you last night at the front door say, 'Stop, Edward, half a dozen times? If he wanted to go, why did you want to stop him?" TREE WERE CLOSE TOGETUER. —Mr. Jinks : "Did you notice that when Alice and her young man were in the parlour last night they said hardly a word all the evening?' Mr. Jinks " No ; but I saw them when he said 'good-night' at the door, and I observed that they were very close-mouthed then." "I MADE an awful bad 'break' the other day, for which I shall be sorry as long as Mrs. 11---- lives. I called at their residence. She :showed me a handsome pair of riding boots she had just bought, and asked me what I thought of them." "'Oh, they are immense said I, innocently." AT an assembly a gentleman entered into con- versation with a young nobleman who was near him. Being a stranger, he made several inquiries respecting the company, which were answered with great politeness. At length he said, "Who is that fat sow at the other end of the room?" "That, sir, is the Countess of D and I have the honour to be one of her little pigs." Ton llnusErr happening to be at dinner at my Lord Mayor's, in the latter part of Queen Anne's reign, after two or three healths, the Ministry was toasted; but when it came to Tom's turn to drink, he diverted it for some time, by telling a story to the person who sat next him. The Chief Magis- trate of the city, not seeing his toast go round, called out, Gentlemen, where sticks the Minis- try?" "At nothing," says Tom, and so drank off his glass. PREPARING FOR IT.—Mr. Billus (calling down the stairway); "Media, have the children gone to school ?" Mrs. BMus : " Yes," "This la the girl's day out, isn't it?" " Yes. She's gone." "This is not the minister's day to call, is it?" "No." "Any of the neighbours likely to drop in during the next hour or so ?" "I think not." " You're</p>
        <p>alone, are you ?" "Yes. What do you—" "Then put some cotton in your ears, Maria. lain going to shave myself with that new razor you gave me the other day. ' A CONCEITED POET once asked Porson to read a new poem which he had just written, and tell him candidly what he thought of it. "Sir," sail the scholar, "your verse will be real when Virgil is forgotten, hut not till then.!” COUGHS are ungrateful things. Yol fill one out in the cold ; you take it up, nurse it, make everything of it, dress it up warm, give it all sorts of balsams and other food it likes, and carry it round in your bosom as if it were a miniature lap- dog. And by-and-bye, its little bark grows sharp and savage, and—confound the thing !—you find it is a wolf's whelp that you have got there, and he is gnawing in the breast where he has been n,-at- ling so long.</p>
