      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>"CAPITLIL" CHAT.</head>
        <p>"CAPITLIL" CHAT.</p>
        <p>Death has removed a gallant and distinguished officer from the army in the person of Lieutenant- General John Neptune Sargene,C 8., who expired at his residence,Mount Mascal, Ilexley, on Friday last. The deceased officer had a brilliant career during the Crimean and China wars, in each of which he writ his name large in-history. Kingslake has recorded his services in the former campaign, while in the latter, when he was Commandant of the Taku fort:; and Allied Commissions, be received the C.B. and a brevet Lieutenant-Colonelcy. General Sargent was the only officer who commanded a volunteer regi- ment while on the active list of the army, as when a half-pay colonel be was allowed to accept the command of the Inns of Court Volunteers, the fa- mous "Devil's Own," which be held for two years. After promotion to the frank of major-general he was appointed to he command of the troops in China and the Straits Settlements, an appointment which was considered ti betoken in due course fur- ther honours and rewards.</p>
        <p>Unfortunately for General Sargent. the Governor of Hong ;Jong at that time (1883-1885) was Sir George Bowen, with whose methods of administra- tion the general was not in accord. hence a wordy warfare, whose ultimate re,tilt was the cold-shoull dering ot General Sargent by the authorities at the Horse Guards, who denied him further employment, and thereby caused him to be retired from non-ser- vice some two or three years ago. The General was not a man to sit down under the slur up° 'his honour implied in the treatment he had received, and for some years he has been engaged in a pro- longed and acrimonious correspondence with the authorities, more especially. the Commander-in- Chief, who for some time pasr,however,has teceived General Sargent's letters in silence.</p>
        <p>The death of the Maharajah Dlittleep Singh at Paris is announced. When only five year old he succeeded his father, the famous Runjeet Singh, to a throne in the Punjaub. Be did not occupy this long. He was brought to England and educated, , and for the loss of his kingdom he was consoled with £40,000 a year. But his ideas were Oriental; he Rot into debt, and when he discovered that the British Government would not increase his salary he went abroad with the object. of seeking assist- ance from Russia awl others to win back his king- dom by force of arms. His mission was greeted with laughter. He returned, made his peace with the Government, and married again. lie was fond of matrimony.</p>
        <p>When the 31aharajah Dhuleep Singh rented Thet- ford, Elcedon Manor, Norfolk, he was perhapi the largest preserver of game in the country. The number of pheasants he reared was something enor- mous. Lahat winter he spent in Algiers with his young wife. His carriage was always followed by a troop of gamins, for he was in t he habit of throw- ing them largesse— coppers mainly—in quite the orthodox Eastern fashion.</p>
        <p>As a military commander and tactician a more than ordinary interest attaches ,to King Albert of Saxony, who, on Sunday, celebrated the jubilee of his connection with the German army, which he Uttered when he was a lad ot I. In Germany, Ring Albert is looked upon by many as the most competent military leader of the Fatherland, and it has even been suggested that he should assume the supreme command. He was cne of the first field =retails whom the old Raiser Wilhelm appointed at the close of the Franco Prussian campaign, in which King Albert brilliantly distinguished him- self.</p>
        <p>lii, is the uncle of the Queen of Its.ly through his aged sister the Duchess of Genoa, the romance of whose career offers one of the most curious chapters of modern court life. King Albert is in a position to sympathise with the Duchess, as he himself in his youth, suffered not a little in his affections from the exigencjes of his rank. It is no secret that his marriage with the Princess Caroline of Vasa was purely a matter of convenience. There has been no family, and on his death the crown passes to his brother, Prince George, brother-in-law to the late Xing of Portugal.</p>
        <p>By those who have seen Prince Bismark sincs his return home, his recovery, I learn, is regaided as remarkable. lie has quite lost the stoop, which during his illness took off so largely from his com- manding presence ; but he has lost flesh, and his face in growing thinner •is considerabl: wrinkled. Dr. Schweninger was right when he rek,koned on the marvellous constitutioq of his illustriou. patients, who, indeed, in strength of body as much as in mind, is still deserving of his old nickname, " The Iron Chancellor."</p>
        <p>For some reason Lord Itlwton's model lo.iging- ing house at Vauxhall, though it offers many ad- vantages to the claws for which it is intentled,is reported to have been nerer full but on one occa- sion. The numbers up to August last seldom rose above 420 or 42,0,being some forty short of a Possible total. On the other hand, the London County Ci.uncire lodging-how-e in Pdlker-et., Drury-lane, turns away manyapplicants every night. Possibly the fact that its charge for 4 bed is Only iicepence a 3 ngvint.t sixpence at Rowton house has more to do with these facts than the well-to-do can easily imngine. It is suggested us an additional reason that wen do not like the turnstiles at the entrance of the Vauxhall establishment. They revolse, it is true; very easily, but, after all, turnstiles are turn- stiles, and Lord Itowton's poor clients appear to puler the open doors of the Parker-st. house.</p>
        <p>At last we know the real cause ot the Victoria disaster. P is revealed to us by Z.i..kiel Tao eze, in the new edition for 1694 of his Almanac, and it is clear that the authorizies of the Court-martial were entirely at sea, if the expression may he permitted in this connection. The Victoria was lost, we ar. told, because the Admiralty residately sets its face against the study bytour naval officers of the truths of astrology. She was fotelootned, for she was launched at a most inauspicious moment', when the evil planet Saturn was in the meridian and evilly configurated with the Sun and 31.irs. Even the " meiliseval astrologers" knew that. to put a vessel in commission under such circumstances would result in disaster. What mediv3val astrologers knew is presumably known to Zadkiel, and a note to the Admirality, stating when and where the danger was to be apprehended, would surely have hen worth thousands of those vague., mysterious hints v. hich are made to do duty in the Almanac every year as "predictions verified."</p>
        <p>There was an unusually large show of the Royal Horticultural Society on Tuesday afternoon. It chiefly consisted of fruit and vegetables. A new kind of Arctic purple kale suggested that this lordly vegetable might come to be used for decorative purposes. Another thing to be learned was that the Batavian endive is being very largely cultivated as a winter salad. A chrysanthemum with over 2,000 buds and blossoms on it,was a remarkable ex- ample of skilful floriculture, but by no means beau- ful in itself. The orchids—cattleyas and .cypri- pedinms—weru exceedingly fine.</p>
        <p>. . An arra3 of eatables of all kinds was on view this afternoon at the Portman Rooms. The show which was opened by Bardness Burdett-Coutts, was bewildeting in its variety. Oa one hand was a table set with an appetising array of Cold dishes , cased with aspfe jelly or iced cream, on the other al prosaic stall filled with bottles of sterilised milk. , There were huge cakes of marzipan, decorated with flowers and inscriptions dear to ;he German heart. There were glazed oranges reposing in a basket- shaped mould of solid ice, There was a wheel-bar- row made of sugar and a pyramid of lobsters four feet high, and there were many things which looked strange and beautiful, but hardly fit for human nature's daily food. More practical than some of these, there were army un.l navy cooks at work, and a stove at which any visitor who hap- pened to bring uncooked eatables in his pocket, could have them c.mked free of charge. Better still, in one corner was a kettle boiling away men ily without flame or fire, electricity being the heating power. Here we have, in all probability, the cookery of the future. But bow is it that With all this evidence of skill ordinary cookery is till so bad?</p>
        <p>RUPTURE CURED.—S. J. SHER:HAN, Hernia Specialist, 64. Cnanmry-iane. London. sends Lis book on rimture post ree. Branch Chambers, 26, King-it., M anchester.—Adrt.</p>
        <p>. . W. H. M. Christie is the preseot Astronomer-Royal. He lives at Greenwich Observat(ory, where he has a pri- vate house and grounds. The Prince of Monaco receives £50,000 for the right t 4 use the garning-t.ibles at Monte Carlo. Coughs and coals are only signs a a general weakness. You would not take cold easily if your system were nourished propsrly. Scott's Emulsion of i3od Liver Oil, with hyp;- pliosphites. is the most nourishing fat •ocal known to science. Yon will nei her take cold easily nor have scold long ii 3on take Scott's Emulsion. Physicians, the world over, endorse It. Chemists sell it. 2s. 6.1. end 4s, 6d.—(4)</p>
      </div>
