      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <p>TITE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OMBER 28, 1893.</p>
        <p>From the fund which Was collected as an offering to Princess Marie when she went to Roumania,a certain sum is to te devoted to the founding of a school for the training of domestic servants at Jessy. The school will comprise four. classes7-for washing, cooking, house- keeping, i&amp;c., and through each of these, every pupil must pass„</p>
        <p>Queen Christina of Spain is the only Royal personage who has appeared as a balloonist. Some time ago ex- periments were being made near Madrid for milicary purposes, when the Royal carriage drove up. The Queen inspected the balloons and announced her intention of go- ing up in one, which she did, amid the enthusiastic cheering of the soldiers. Two Italian women living at or near Fenn Mtn, New York, have petitioned Governor Flower t 3 let them wear men's clothes in order to better assist their husbands in farm work. What's to prevent them wearing what they please on their own premises They've as much right to curtail their petticoats in the exercise or their industry as the ballerina in the erercise of her pro- fession.</p>
        <p>The fashion of long and elaborate meals has almost posed away among the richest classes. The Queen paid a visit to Baron Ferdinand de Rothschild at Waddesdon Manor, and the following comparatively simple menu is that ot the lunch given by this representative of the richest family in, tho world 0 the Sovereign of Great Britain :—Windsor soup, Norwegian trout, quails'ou toast filleted beef, roast ducks, asparagus, and</p>
        <p>- Pear Marmalade.</p>
        <p>T) make 18 glasses of this delicious marmalade use el4l&amp;t pounds of sugar, an equal weight of Seckel pears, lair a pound of crystalised ginger, and four lemons. Boil the lemons in clear water until the peel can be pierced whys broom splint. Take them from the water, remove the seeds, and cut the lemons very fine. Peel the pears and cut them into thin slices. Put all the ingredients into the preserving kettle and cc:l)k gently for two hours, stirring often. At the end of this time pour the marmalade into jelly glasses. When cold, cover the same as jelly.</p>
