      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER</p>
        <p>MY DEAREST NELLIE, It would seem that the greatest revolution in dress fashions for the autumn season is in the matter of jackets, the newest being made to fit the figure closely. Those that hail from Paris have large revers, curiously shaped, and I have seen several which have taken my fancy. One had a most uncommon feature in the ornamentation, viz., , a hand•painted design of leaves in a charming :violet tone, and this was outlined with jet paillettes flop the front, while the revers were of violet cloth, worked in jet and steel. It was bordered with fur as a matter of course, for everything is thus treated --dresses, mantles, and even bonnets. The em- broidery was certainly most original. And the fur =ink.</p>
        <p>The prettiest hats were of black beaver and of black velvet, and they were trimmed with bows of -ribbon and ostrich tips; the description sounds ordinary, but I assure you the hats are not se. Steel ornaments are worn on all hate, and the lodg steel buckle is the favourite ornament of the season. One effective hat had an oxydised crown; the rest of it wits green, with green shot feathers, and a few yellow bows to relieve the monotony. Another hat was in petunia velvet, with full crown. It was trimmed with back tips and jet ornaments, and wr.s also fitted with black velvet strings. To make a dress suitable for winter I think velvet would look better than braid as a trim- ming for a brown drese. The velvet should be a little darker than the material, and could be inttoduced on the skirt as a single band about 2} inches wide, sewn at the top of a rather deep hem; bodice with seam round waist, and four- inch baeque sewn on in rather scanty well-pressed box-pleats, fronts double-breasted, with wide revers of velvet; full sleeves. and gauntlet cuff of velvet. Another suggestion is to outline each seam of the gored skirt witk a band of narrow ribbon velvet, finished with a small rosette at the hem, and to have a round-waisted bodice with velvet belt and a plain pelerine pointed back and front, and edged with velvet. By plain, I mean not gathered or pleated, but sloped carefully to fit the shoulders. The miff should be trimmed with four or five narrow strips of velvet running upwards from waist to elbow. A caLital idea is a loose, sleeveless coat of a roseate magenta satin, with the fronts cut away, and displaying scarves of the satin, which can either be arranged to cross the figure or to hang loosely down the front with long ends. Black lace outlines this coat, and a deep frill of the same forma the sleeves, this is really an ideal theatre bodice, and is especially adapted to the matron-- the matron, be it understood, who boasts good arms. Another charmiog bodice which would be becoming to almost any figure, is entirely formed of diagonally placed rows of black velvet and white lace fixed over a pink silk lining, with full pink ilk puffs on the sleeves, and a frilled silk basque ; and yet another effective specimen of pink swab, has the yoke formed of infinitesimal tucks caught at different intervals to set in different ways, with double cares over the shoulders out- lined with black velvet.</p>
        <p>A dainty tca gown can be made of soft woollen material, gradually shaded from creamy white to dark chestnut brown. Turough the square open- kg of the bodice a chemisette is visible; it has a ruche in white batiste ; so 119.ve the long sleeves, which are divided into puffing., with bands of guipure. This trimming appears in different widths and patterns round the bodice and bands of the short upper sleeves, and also along the revers, I:moque, and imilor collar of the loose jacket fronts, conspicuous for its central kerchief point ; the largest flounce is noticed toyer the hem of the skirt. Through two slashes of the bodice are displayed white puffing., which meet in a point at the waist, where they arc outlined with a narrow ribbon, fastened at the side into a bow.</p>
        <p>In mantles there are many novelties. The popular mein!) antique is of black, edged with feather trimming, and is beautifully trimmed with an appliqu,t of velvet and jet, one of the capes being made entirely of jet and embrodered, the other of moire and embroidery, while the collar ia of velvet, and feather trimming borders the fronts. The velvet trimming is really most beautifully contrived, being patterns cut out and appliquéd on to the moire with the handsomest of jet. The bonnet which is illustrated with this is of black velvet, with a horse-shoe crown edged with cream- colour insertion; it is trimmed in the front with erect ends of this cream lace, transfixed with jet pins, and adorned with just a couple of black ostrich feather tips.</p>
        <p>Children's dresses have attracted a more than ordinary amount of attention this season. A prettily embroidered frock is in fine French cam- brio, with three frills round the skirt, headed with graduated narrow tucks. Embroidered yoke and full, con(ined with bands and frills at the wrist, knots of ribbon on the shoulder. Another pretty dress has a white moulton coat, the skirt, sleeves, and triple capes, of which are trimmed with white satin appliqué. The hat is.of the same material, with plumes. _ _</p>
        <p>Yours lovingly, . JENNIE</p>
        <p>The Czarina of Russia, lull tile I)w:ilea, v. .v... 4.1. ur4 the lucky owners,. Of tliP handsemezt Fa bl esiii t world. Both sets cost £12.500. . . _</p>
        <p>The height of vulgar cbsplaF has been reached by the wife ef an American millionaire who wears eyeglaSs sus- pended. upon a string of ilainonda hung round her neck, and alt&gt;wcd to fall to Vie hem of her gown. Miss Ada Itelisn, the great American actress, spends her life off the stage in stuiious solitude, preferring quiet and peace ot mind to the thing life of a society beauty. She lives in a charming at in New York, ernd despite her ripid rise to fame and fortune is elfl hs modest, as simple, as retired as ever.</p>
