      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>Rolled Beef.</head>
        <p>Rolled Beef.</p>
        <p>Rored beef is a nice dish arvi cagily prepared. About s pound of steik, in one piece, is required, this must be ittsned with a rolling pin and a seasoning made as Follows spread ova. the meat: Four ounces of broad crumbs, an ounce of chopped suet, a teaspoonful of chopped parsley, a very little grated lemon peel, and a slice of onion minced ; an egg or some milk must be used to moisten the mixture, the beef is then rolled up and tied with a piece of tape and stewed gently in thick brown gravy- for two hours in a moderately hot oven. The following day the rill would come in cold for break- fast, and the remains minced could be utilised for stuff- ing tomatoes.</p>
        <p>, Breakfast Dishes. . ; 1 Little dishes sultablq for breakfast are so numerous I that it should bean easy meal to cater for. Fish cakes or kedgeree are both appetising dishes. The latter is I made thus :—Bidl half a teacupful of rice until tender. , drain it well, and add amounce of butter, pepper, salt, two hard boiled eggs;chopped up, mad the remains of almost army cooked fish, mix thoroughly, make quite hot in saucepan, and heap neatly on a well-heated dish. Buttered roe, mixed nith a little potted meat and ;tented on buttered toast, with a sprinkle of brown bread crumbs on thttop, is a tasty little dish, as is also sardine toast made by pounding same sardine paste with the yolk .of a hard boiled egg, then spreading the mistare on toast and heating in the oven. German fritters are easily ma e._ Some slices cut from a chicken, !tam, and tongue aus e are necessary. These are dipped in a light bat- Sti t-r, a fried until a golden brown, and sent to table the instant they are finished. A piece of pressed beef, in pound or two of spiced brawn, a meat or game pl'e, arid German sausage's all help to vary the typical British breakfast of bacon and eggs or eggs and bacon.— "Woman."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>RUNCORN CAPTAIN IN SOUTH ' AFRICA. INTERESTING PRESENTATION.</head>
        <p>A RUNCORN CAPTAIN IN SOUTH ' AFRICA. INTERESTING PRESENTATION.</p>
        <p>A Runeornian at present resident in Cape Town, South Africa, forwards us a copy of the "Cape Times," wnich contains the following particulais of the voyage of the Royal Mail Stearder, Ilawarden Castle, and of the presentation of an address to the Captain, Mr. harry Rigby, of Greenway-road, Run- corn. Captain Rigby was compelled to spend nearly the whole of the summer at his home in con- sequence of hating to undergo a surgical operation, and this was his first !Kopp since his convalescence. The Ilawarden left Southampton on Setatembi-r 9 a: 6.55 p.m.; arrived at blideira on September 13 at 11.30 a.m.; left Madeira on Sep'ember 13 at 5.25 p.m., and arrived in Table Hay (Cape Town, South Africa) on Sep- tember 27, after a net passage of 17 days. 3 hours. She had on board a full general cargo and 274 passengers, besides 304 bags and 62 boxes of mails. In the Channel she experienced moderate breeze and fine weather, thence to Madeira fresh breeze and high head sea and dull, cloudy weather ; moder ite bre, ze and fine weather to Cape Verde, and thence to Cspe Town moderate trades and dull Wf ather.</p>
        <p>The evening before reaching Table Bay a very pleas- ing ceremony was celebrated in the saloon of this fine steamship, when Mr. J. R. Cuthbert, on behalf of the passengers, presented an address to CaTt Lin Rigby, con- veying to kim the hearty thanks of all for his extreme courtt sy and kindness throughout the voyage. Captain Rigby said that he was deeply gratelul for such kindly espr.,ssed wising of the pa isengers, and he could truly say that during his lengthened service in the South Afri- can trade—now some twenty years—he had never sailed will, more congenial and sociable passengers, and hoped they would all have the opportunity of again sailing in such a fine steamer as the Hawai (dna Castle. which he now had the good fortune to command, He aga'n thanked the passengers for their good wishes on bell :If of himself, iflicers, and all those connected in the work- ing ot the ship. He was only too pleased to feel that hat had been done for their enjoyment and comfort had la en so heartily appreciated by all on board. The following is the text of the address : September 26, 1893. To El. Rigby, Esq., Commander, R.M.S. Hawarden Castle. _ _ .</p>
        <p>Sir,—We, the under signed passengers on board your ship, now abut terminating the voyage tram London to Cape Town, hereby beg to tender you our hearty thanks for your extreme courtesy and attention, at all inns so cheerfully Lestowtd upon one and all of us. We rot 0 at through your genial dispositkii and kindness of maoner, 11w gvneral harmony amongst us has been ,iticce,sfully maintained, thus rendering it the pleaFant• est of voyages, which will long rotnain fresh in our ineinories. Nr)tiwitlistanding all the freedom afforded us, the discipline of the sliip has been generally cominc nt ed upon, evecialiy by the older travellers, as beirl.: of the highest attaimnent, making us Nei perfectly safe under yoor comniaoil and staff at experienced and obligiur ftic•rs. Wishing you a life of prosperity and continued success. On behalf of the passengers, the address bore the f hwin signvores : J. It. Ctithbeit. J. F. !Urchins, C. E. Solomon, F.H.G.S., J. J. Mielmas, J. Curtis, F. Fitz- F. Southern, and W. D. Beauchamp.</p>
      </div>
