      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>MYSTERY OF BLACK-TOR LIGHTHOUSE.</head>
        <p>THE MYSTERY OF BLACK-TOR LIGHTHOUSE.</p>
        <p>BY LAXRA JEAN LIBBEY,</p>
        <p>Anther of "lone, the Pride of the Mill," &amp;e., &amp;e.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>SYNOPSIS OF CHAPTERS 1 TO 6.</head>
        <p>SYNOPSIS OF CHAPTERS 1 TO 6.</p>
        <p>Sixteen years prior to the present period of the story, a wreck took place near to the Black Tor Lighthouse. The keeper, Mark Sefton, succeeded in saving a mother and child, whom he brought to the lighthouse, and his , wife, Nellie, tended them. The mother, a beautiful young woman, only returned to conaciousness to con- sign the care of the babe, Uldene, to Nellie's charge, and died. Her pleading for Nellie to love and rear it was followed by an attempt to unfold to Nellie her story, but death o, erlook her, and her last words were "This babeinust never love, for she must never marry." Nellie placed the .babe beside her own golden-haired chill, Verlie. They aro brought up together as sisters by the lighthouse keeper and his wife. Dr. Thorndyke 'remarked upon the linen the child's palm, which were uncommon and portended a strange destiny. She was christened Uldene. from the name on the marriage ring of her dead mother. Verlie grew to be a girl of quiet, retiring disposition, whilst Uldene was gay, reetless, and piquant, full of faults, wilful and wild. The girls pay a visit to Mrs. Chester, of Washington, a Senator's wife and an old friend of the Settees. Tliere they meet her son, Rutledge Chester, who had just returned from Euiope. Uldene • learns to love Rutledge, while he Las a passion for Verne, whose life be had saved by captmiug her runaway horse, The Ohesters give a masked ball ; Verlie is dressed as a bade, Uldene as lichen of Troy. Verlie is followed about by a plumed knight, and Milne, suspecting him to be Rutledge, persuades Verlie to retire with her for awhile to change costumes. On their re-appearance Uldene, in the blide's coStume, is joined by Rutledge, (the plumed knight), and they go together to a rettircil bower, where, thinking she is Verlie, he confesses his love and, asks her to be his wife. Uldene is silent, and feeling the confession too sudden, he asks her to wear a white rose when unmasking as a token of acceptance of him; ski a blood red one to indicate refusal. Uldene hastens to Volk and makes her change cos- ' tumes again, placing a red rose ii, Verlie's dress bosom. Verlie, having lie eye to her sister's treachery, allows it to remain. Rutledge is miserable over his presumed rejection ; but at length writes her to see him in the conservatory in the evening ere he speeds away to Europe. Ulan° and Verlie go out that day, and take an isolated path outside the city until they get into a churchyard. Verlie catches her foot in a bramble and falls heavily on a tomb. Oppressed with jealousy and knowing of the appointment with Rutledge that night, Uldene is seized with a sudden temptation and leaves Verlie lying insensible in the ohuichyard. Suow was falling. Verlie is missed the next morning.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER VU. A FATAL MISTAKE.</p>
        <p>The long, terrible night wore away t huit —night with its dark shadows and wailing winds. !Aden° crept to the _window again, and looked shudderingly out, her Mart beating with horrible fear.</p>
        <p>Yes, the snow was deep on the pavement and in the road. It had drifted high against the marble steps of the houses that lined the avenue, and she knew it must have drifted in a great white heap over the upturned face and the rigid form lying among the tombstones in the old grave-yard. As Uldene passed the mirror, she was startled at the white, wild face that locked out into her own. What should she do to drive away the startled fear, the vague dread, the deadly pallor.</p>
        <p>Oh if the wind outeide would but stop moaning —if the skeletod brancUes of the snow-laden trees would cease rustling ! Every rustling sound seemed to bear this imesage from the lonely graveyard over the hills—" You lured me away from home— to die.''</p>
        <p>Would those horrible words never cease ringing in her ears? _ •</p>
        <p>" Let me forget that white, upturned face or I shall go mad 1" she erica out to heteelf. There was no more time for thought. The breakfast hell sounded for the last time. Turning from the mirror, she walked with unsteady stops duwu to the breakfaetlroom. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The senator had already breakfasted and gone. Mrs. Chester and her son still lingered over their coffee. Loth looked up as the door opened. " Good-rnorhing, my dear," said Mrs. Chester, smiling. "I really believed you and your sister intended to sleep all day." A terrible whiteness overspread Uldenc's guilty face. " Has Verne not been to breakfast ?" she asked, in feigned surprise. " She 13 not in our room. I —I have not seen her since last night. I retired first, and dropped into a dreamless sluisber the moment my head touched the pillow. I must omfess that I feared, when I awoke, that Verlie must have tallen asleep in RA arm chair, and remained there all night, for her pillow was un- (Ho urbed."</p>
        <p>Both mother and son look up anxiously. " shall have to scold your sister a little for such irregular labile," he said, touching a small silver hanti:.ell at her ethow.</p>
        <p>In response to the summons, a crett y young maid came to the door of t he breakfust-room.</p>
        <p>" You will go to Miss l'erlie's room and tell her we are awaiting her at the breakfast-table," said Mrs. Chester. "If you do not find hcr there, go to the drawing-room, through the conservatory; in fact, throng!' the house, until you do find her." Nanon flaw hurriedly away on her mission, but soon returned with the intelligence : "liiss Verlie was not to be found in the house. I took precaution to look into the wardrobe," con- tinued anon, "and then I discovered her hat and cloak were missing. She must have gone out on the street."</p>
        <p>" That is strange I Out on the street on such a morning as this ' remarked Mrs. Chester. Uldcno made no codment ; she could not have tittered a word if her life had depended on it. She had 'with her a companion she was never to lose, again—a haunting fear—a guilty terror that would never more leave her. Uldene had not taken her usual plec,3 at the breakfast-table; she took a seat at the table with her face turned from the sunshine—she could not endure the light of the white snow outside. At every unusual stir—every peal of the bell— every quick footatcp—her heart eecined to die within her. She would fancy it was Verlie coming in to confront her, crying out how fair, false, treacherous ULlene had left li.tr alone to die. And if Vedic ebould thus come Mrs. Chester would turn from her (lildene) in ;terror too great for words, and Rutledge—at), the deed, blazing scorn and bitter anger in his eyes—the eyes of the man for whose sake she had sinned so fatally— % ould strike her dead. -</p>
        <p>He would dtspise her, and when that moment came she would die, she told hercelf. When noon arrived, and Verne had not yet returned, Mrs. Cheater commenced to grow anxious about her whereabouts, and when the aftetnoon deepmed into the dusk of the early winter afternoon her anxiety grew into terror.</p>
        <p>The senator had gone out of town early that morning, and Rutledge had lunched at the club and it would be rather uncertain, he told Lie mother, whether he would be home in time to dine with the family or not. Mrs. Chester could otand the suspense no lopger and seat a messenger for her eon at once. "I want to see you in ieferetace to Verlie," so ran his mother's brief note. "Come without delay.' The young man's handsome, grave face flushed and paled as he thrust the delicate, pink-tinted affair into &amp;is breast pocket. l'l3l course there could be but one moaning to</p>
        <p>it;" he told himself, as he seated himself in _ his</p>
        <p>sleigh, tu ked the buffalo robe around him, picked Up the lines and gave the mettlesome grey horse a slight cut with his whip that sent him fairly .spinning down the snow-crusted road homeward. Of course Verlie had recalled him. She had repented not having kept the appointment in the conservatory, and had confessed the story of her son's love to his mother, and that tender-hearted little woman had taken it upon herself to become mediator between them, recalling her son without delay ; for he had announced at the breakfast- table that morning his intention of going abroad for a few months, declaring he should start immo. diately. ! It was with a very light heart be drew rein before his home. Ile glanced 'up at Vie windows; no dimpled, bashful, girlish face, framed in golden hair, peeped coyly down at him from the lace. draped windows. Flinging the reins to the groom, he sprung up the marble steps three steps at a , time.</p>
        <p>A servant answered the imperative summons. As he stepped into the marble corridor the draw. lug-room door opened and he saw his mother, white as a ghost .and deeply agitated, standing on the threshold.</p>
        <p>"Come in here, my son," she exclaimed, draw- ing him into the room; and while he was removing his gloves she had told him of Verlie's protracted absence, and of the grave fears she entertained of her oeing lost. Boston was not the best city in the world for a stranger to find their way in.</p>
        <p>Rutledge Chester's face grew whiter and whiter AS he listened. Like a flash a solution of Ver.lie's mys•erious apsence came to him. He sprang from his chair and began pacing up and down the floor with such rapid strides, his face working convulsively, and his mother grew alarmed.</p>
        <p>"Rutledge, my son!" she cried out, in dismay. But he held up his hand with a gestu.o of silence</p>
        <p>" Hush, mother !" he cried, bitterly. "I can- not hear it."</p>
        <p>" Verlio is not lost," ho went on, huskily. " When she left the house this morning, she left it for good, with the intention of never entering these doors again." " Rutledge !" exclaimed his mother, in wonder and dismay. "What can you mean by such a remat k ?''</p>
        <p>" Ilear me out, mother," he went on, huskily, as he threw himself down in a chair and covered his face wi h his hand; "and the cause will explain itself as I pi oceed." In a straightforward, manly way he told her of his love for beautiful little golden-haired Verdi; and how the knowledge had first come to him, when he saved her from death that memorable day in the park. He told her, too, how he had detected Verlie's costume on the night of the mask ball, and in the course of the ev nine, in the dim conservatory, had laid his heart and hand at her feet, begging her, if she favoured his suit, to wear a white rose in her bodice aft( r unmasking, aud if it was her wish to reject and dismiss him, a red rose so worn should be the signal of it.</p>
        <p>" Verlie wore the red rose at the appointed hour," he continued, huskily, " and I knew then my suit was in vain. She did not love me. "I could not rest without making another attempt to win her,' he went on, desperately. "So I wrote her a note yesterday afternoon, begging her to meet me in the conservatory at eight that evening, to give mo one more chance to plead my cause. I was there promptly at eight. She was not there. I waited an hour. Still she came not. She wished me to understand by her silence that her answer was final. Don't you sec how it is, mother? Rather than pain me by another refusal, she has gone quietly away. She knew quite well I would tell you why."</p>
        <p>Mrs. Chester's distress as she listened knew no bounds. This revelation gave her such unbounded surprise. For once she was completely startled out of her lady-like calm. " Forget her, Rutledge, my dear son," she cried, throwing her arms around his neck and drawing his handsome, manly face down to her own. "Forget her, my boy. This lowly-born girl was no mate for the heir of the Cheaters at best. She would never have been suited to you, anyhow. it is all for the best."</p>
        <p>Ho started buck, as though those white welted, wrinkled hands had struck him a sudden blow. _</p>
        <p>" Da not sneak hard of her, mother," he. cried, huskily. " Remember, I love her better than life itself, and a cutting word spoken of kr strikes through my heart like the thrust of a dagger. She is fitted to wed a king on his throne. It she could not love me, it was not her fault, poor child!"</p>
        <p>"No,"- ad rn t ted his mother, drawing a hard breath.</p>
        <p>Vet she was puzz'ed to know how any young girl in her eeeses could help loving her tall, hand - e haunlity, manly son. 'I have an idea that se has gone directly home,' In.l went on ; " but to make assurance doubly sure, I shall make iuquirie3 at once." Oh, fatal mistake, that was the tuning pint of threa</p>
        <p>At the wharf he was told a young girl Had boarded the outgoing Pteatner that morning, with a ticket direct for Pens:wrote, Florida.</p>
        <p>" Of couo it wa3 he thought, turning away. "1 Lnew Elie must have gone etraight home."</p>
        <p>CITAPTEP. A DUEL AND AN ABDUCTION.</p>
        <p>Uldene felgned the greatest astonishment when Mrs. (nester broke this startling news to her, ac- counting for her sister's sudden disappearance, and that Rutledge had ttece4 her to the wharf where she had taken passage on an outgoing steamer for home.</p>
        <p>"I am not surprieed that this is a great revela- tion to ynn," Mts. Chester went on, looking pity- ingly inte tle, girl's death-white face. "It will no doubt be quite as much of a surplice for your father and mother. ilotledge is in the library now, writing them a full explanation of the affair. It will probably reach there en the same day %Torii° arrives home."</p>
        <p>Uldt no could have cried out in terror as sto heard those words ; and the thought flashed upon her that she twist prevent that letter from reaching Its destination at any cost. No one yet ever took the first fatal step in deception and sin without being dragged still further down the slippery, steep path, and at Iset, plunge.) headlong into the dark abyss yawning to receive her. It was SO with Udell°. She had taken the first fatal Step in a hcrtible deception and she told her- self it was to late to turn back now, • " I should like to add a short posteript to Rut- ledge's letter," She said, in a voice that sounded like nothing human, "and, if he does not mind, I —I will mail it."</p>
        <p>"Certainly," assented Mrs. Chester. "Do so by all means. It will greatly allay your father's anxiety if you alEo inclose a few lines." A few moments later a servant appeared with Rutledge's letter, enveloped and directed, but un- sealed.</p>
        <p>Early the next morning Uldene left the honse for the ostensible purpose of mailing the letter. When quite out of sight of the Chester mansion she turned in an opposite direction from the post-office, hurrying quickly along through the crowded thorJughfare until she found herself on the wharf. She glanced furtively about her. No one was observing her; and drawing the letter from her pocket, she tore it into minute pieces, telling herself that, in case of war, a general would open and destroy the dispatches of the enemy if he could. It was the only coursa left to her in thin case.</p>
        <p>With a quick movement Uldene threw the frag- ments of the letter into the river. To her horror they would not sink. The waves drifted the white flakes tog(ither toward the tea, and out of her sight. She was in a paroxysm of terror. What if they should sail on and on, swept on by the hand of an avenging fate, until they reached the solitary island ? hit if the waves dashed them high on the sands of Illack•Tor L'ght-House, and they should fall into the hands of Mark Sefton?</p>
        <p>" The waves will not betray me," she cried, under her breath. "They have had other fated secrets to ket p." Turning away with a shudder, she cluickly re- traced her steps home h ard. " I have separated Verlie and Rutledge itiester," I hat was the thought that deadened her conscience, " and ii the time to colic }di heart will turn to me. It mu-t. Love will win love. I should have gone mad or died if Verlie had won him. I love him so."</p>
        <p>Two days later, looking over the evening paper, Uldene's eyes fell upon the following item :</p>
        <p>"Fot:ND DICAD IN THE STET.—A young, fair• !Liked woman was found. quite dead, buried in the snow-drifts, early last Tuesday morning. The body was removed to the morgue, and as it remained un- claimed. urireostnivti. tilt to I,,,,terthty. it wits interred</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <p>by the proper authorities." ho paper felt horn Uldene's grasp, and the world seemed to grow dark around her. never doubted but what it was Verlie—the fair- haired sister whom she had lured so inhumanly to an untimely end. Even in this 11,:ur no keen repentance touched her heart, which was harder than adamant aid colder than marble, as she pictured what poor Verlio's waking moments must have been, or the pain and torture she must Lava suffered when she found herself deserted, alone, helpless, in the dark grave-yard, with the snow falling around her. How she must have stretched out her white arms to the dark sky, praying for the help that did not come! At first she must have hoped Uldene had gone for assistance; but as the hours rolled on the hop died out, and she realised that she had been deserted in her crippled helplessness and left alone to die.</p>
        <p>Uldene had swept her rival from her path at biller cost. Let tier triumph in the knowledge until an avenging Clod metes out retribution for her crime; for retribution follows every sin committed, sooner or later.</p>
        <p>Let us go hack, dear reader, to the waking mokients of Verlio Sefton. We shall see, as we- proceed, that it wits not Verlie who had been found dead in the streets. Pete had spared her, and for a far different end. And at the moment Rutledge Chester was writing that letter—which never found its way to the lonely lighthouEe—Verlie was going through the strangest and most startling experiences that ever fell to the lot of a young girl. The swoon into which she had fallen—caused 117 the intense pain of her sprained ankle—lasted an hour or more. The cool flakes of falling snoW drifting down on her upturned face speedily revived her.</p>
        <p>" Uldcrie l " she cried, faintly, " where anl I--I—''</p>
        <p>Like a flash, memory returned to her, and she- rennewbered. ILT first thought, as she lifted her- self upon her elbow and saw that she was alone, was that Ulr;ene had gone to sum,non help ; but as the hours dragged slowly on and no help came to, her, terror seizA her.</p>
        <p>"Bad harm befs,llen her beautiful sister?' vro hfr agonised thought. "Ah I what could detain Ulilene ?''</p>
        <p>The thought had no sooner crossed her brain, than suddenly a hori ib'c sound tel upon her ears the sudden, almost itztußancous report of two• pistol bhots that tang out sharply ol the night air. The cry of tenor froze on the girl's lips. The sound of voices leached her from the thick grove. at the back of the mitts of the old church, scarcelY ten r:Ali distant, nod Without reflection, and almost heedless of ti,c excrueia.ting pain of her sprained. ankle, rlie c,(pt towald the dick group of trees that overlooked the point from whence the bound had issued.</p>
        <p>And there anddst the snowdrifts, the horrible, weird scene, illuminated by the broad glare of two eye lanterns, the gil beheld a sight that burned itself indelibly upon her memory while' life lasted. A group of five men stood beneath the trees. One leaned against a broken tombstone; two wem conversing eagerly in undertones ,and two fen:R.l each other fifteen paces apart, with pistols in their• hands. I,le the ten t Hied girl could fully compre- hend the scene, the conference ended, the seconds. resumed their places, and a voice cried Out : " Are you lady for the second shot?" The two combatants nodded assent. Then, like the shock of doom, the horrible corn•• mend fell on the Etiiiie,:s of the midnight air : I- "One, two, three I Ready I Eire 1" xne rhisn ant ringing report trom too VC.° smoking pistols mingled with the command, and one of the principals threw up his arms and fell without a moan —without a cry—face downwards on the snow that was already crimsoned with his life blood that had spurted from the death-wound in his breast.</p>
        <p>"Dead !'' cried bath seconds—" shot through the heart</p>
        <p>" Well, he would have it," responded the prin- cipal, coolly and cynically, as he turnail on his heel. Ile had scarcely proceeded a dozen paceB' ere he star ted lmek with a los cry : " A woman -- a spy—by all that's wonderful I" he cried, agitaat• Directly in the path before him, her fam wnite as deAth anti frozen with horror, stood a youug- girl, clutching for support against a tree. In great mut prise he advanced a step or two, and asked, gruffly : " Who are you, girl? What business have yt,' here?" - He clutched her arm firmly with his strong right haud, but Verlie Sefton shook her arm free to though a serpent had stung her. " Murderer I " she gasped, recoiling from hiln• "Oh, how dared you take a human life? lb yffil think God will forgive you on the gallows for the crime I witnessed?" He was a mall probably twenty-eighk 'years Or age, singularly hands sine, with the dark beauty cf a Greek god—a handsome face, but hardened 10 iniquity. ' Ile sprang forward again, and clutched her arm BO firmly in his gi asp that she could not wrench it, from him. "So you witnessed the duel, did you?' he repeated grimly. " That is bad for you—for yo" Shall not go free until yon have registered a solemn oath never to reveal what you have seen and heard here to•night." The girl's dauntless blue eyes never quailed beneath the gaze of the dark ones bent upon her CO steadily. "And you shall tell, too, what brought yoa' here, girl." "I shill appeal to the gentlemen present to pro° tect me from you," said Venue, raising her eye t to' his companions. In that moment of time, While her attention wa9 attracted elsewhere, they had made away with the' body of the fallen duelist ; and the girl noticed. with a thrill of horror, that they had drawn black. masks over their laces, and were debating together in angry %shivers. A scornful, sneering smile cut in upon her words, and her captor answered, with a scornful' laugh : "These gentlemen will not interfere ; my wora' is law to them. If you knew what they were dis- cussing now, you would tremble. It is a question, of your life—or death. You have heard and &amp;ova' too much. But I say that you shall depart, pro- viding that you will take the oath required of yoa —and freak: it at your peril." "I would never promise what you ask !" cried Verne, defiantly. "On the contrary, I shall take pains to bring you to jurtice—and at the earliett opportunity, too." "Are you mad, to brave me thus, girl ?" cried, frowning darkly. "You are in my power, remember." - "I am vet !" she retorted, bravely ; "as Yol shall soon see. Already friends are coming to tuY rescue. I fell hero scarcely an hour ago wbilB walking with my sister, and sprained my ankle- ' She has flown away for assistance. They will scot] I return."</p>
        <p>"Forewarned is forearmed," returned the man' ahrtiptiy. " such a caso it is useless to stand here parleying with you. I shall take you to where you shall have plenty of time to consider my pr°' position at your leisure." The lights from the lanterns were shut off as if ty magic, and left them in total darknens. dexterous movement a thick cloak was tlfrocil over her, and despite her frantic struggles arli' stifled screams, Verlie found herself in the grasp 0' a pair of arms as strong as steel, and shc m 139 borne swiftly over the snow-crusted ground, then down a long of spiral el one steps, seemed to lead down, down into the very bowels a' the earth.. A narrow, subterranean, serpentine passage' Iv's traversed, and far off coital be heard the indistinct hum of gruff voices, The voices sounded nearer and nearer. .11"fra was the sharp, metallic click of a massive, ri.slY key turned in a lock, and a ponderous door via' thrown back upon its hinges. There was shuffling of many feet, followed by the fry exclamation of a score or more of voices, crying 'a a breath : "In Heaven's name, what have you theTc* captain?" And Verlie's captor answered, with a sardonc laugh : " An unwelcome guest." (To be continuer!.)</p>
        <p>,utuoritios." horn Uldcne's grasp, and the -.row dark around her. She it was Verne—the fair lurod so inhumanly to -epcntanco touched adamant and ' what poor 'n, or the •ni she 'irk • „ • , • r</p>
