        <head>PAINFUL DAYS.</head>
        <p>PAINFUL DAYS.</p>
        <p>Will arrive for all those who are predisposed to rheuma- tism or'rheumatic gout during the winter months, being the time of the year when these diseases develop with Fenewed violence. To Avoid a great deal of suffering, it is advisable to take precautionary measures in time. and to use remedies that will not only prevert these attacks, but effectually and permanentl) eradicate these evils from the systsm. Rheumatism and rheumatic g3at can only be cured by a constitutional trea'ment, and a remedy which prevents the accumulation of uric acid in the blood, and which will remove this acid if already formed, restoritig the digestive organs and kid- aaYa to a normal and healthy action. Einbrocations, Plasters, ointments, and ether localapplications may sometimes afford temporary relief from acute pain, but p.ever remove the disease. It is generally consllere d !hat Warner's Safe Cure is the only remedy which effec- tually and permanently cures these complaints, and , .T.ere is scarcely a town, village, or hamlet in the United cgdons in which some person does not proclaim its r acY. The following testimonial from Major McMur- a3', Springfield House, Killuney, Armagh, June 1890, conclusively proves that Warner's Safe Cure, not only cures, but permanently so. He writes :" I consider it la 7 duty to make public the followine statement. I was !party). t) rheumatic gout from 1865 until April 1888 ; 12ing laid up with it regularly every six months, some- limes for weeks and sometimes for months. In April, 888, I commenced to take Warner's Safe Cu.e. I found relief after the fourth bottle. I took twenty-one bottles. Since I atoPoed taking the medicine, in July, 1888. I h avP not had the sliehtest indication of rheumatic gout. _,August 1893,—1 have pleasure in confirming ±pnve statement, made three years ago, and adding, my via enemy has not visited me since."</p>
