      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <p>WARRINOTI N IVIUNICIiPAI,</p>
        <p>ELECTION S.</p>
        <p>LIST OF CANDIDATES. The following is a list of the candidates, anti:m(lM tn which are the remainder of the nominations which were handed to the Town Clerk on Tuesday:— Town Hall Ward Mr. J. H. Fleming (1...), mie Joseph Shillcock (C) Whiteeross „ Mr. H. Roberti (L.), Mr. B. T. Smith (C). Bewsey Joseph Shaw-Green (L.), Mr. John Baird (C). Orford , „ Mr. William gierpnint (L.), Mr. W P: Skelton (C ) St. John's „ Mr. W. Furness (L.), Mr. W. J. Shaw (C). Fa\ rfteld „ Mr. Joseph Mounfield (L.); Mr. H. Longton (C). Howley „ Arthur Browne (L.), Mr. Arthur Bennett (0). St. Austin's „ Mr. James Hepherd (C). Latchford . Mr. S. B. Stringer (L.),Mr. Joseph Ashton (C). NOMINATIONS. TOWN HALL WARD.. Mr. John Henderson Fleming, (L), Winmarleigh Lodge Springfield•street, tanner, is proposed by Alder- man Lodge, seconded by Mr. George Clay, and as- sented to by Messrs.. T.. H. Poole, S. J. Beaman, Thomas Jones, Alfred Corney, Matthew Knowles, Jeffrey Ben- nion,William Walker, and Thomas Lowe. Mr. Fleming is further proposed by Councillor Green, and seconded by Mr. William Winstanlev. Mr. Shillcoek is further proposed by Mr. Ephraim Auk- land, and seconded by Mr. Charles Holland. WHITECROSS WARD. Mr. Henry Roberts (L), Rose Cf,ttage, Common-lane, Latchford, builders' merchant,is proposed by Mr. Fred Monks, seconded by Mr. J. W. Hayward. and assented t Irc Messrs. Samuel Faulkner, Michael Payne, William ffenry Jones, George Edge, Thomas Dale. John Field- haute, Charles Morris, and Thomas Raybould. In a Second paper the candidate is proposed by Mr. James Evans, seconded by Mr. Arthur Birch, and-the assenting burgesses are Messrs. George Sambrook. Thomas Haw- thorn, George Evans, Thomas Gregson, J. A. Hey- wood, William Hewitt, George Faulkner, and Andrew Burns. Mr. R. T. Smith (C), hoop roller. 25; Levely-lane, is in another paper nominated by Mr. William Needham, seconded by Mr. Robert Mellor, and the assenting bur- gesses are Messrs. Levi Brown, Samuel Towers,.Henry Isherwood, James Morley, Samuel Mason, ThomasJenks Isaac Houghton, and James Jolley. Mr.. Smith is fur- ther proposed by Mr. Walter Hainsworth and seconded by Mr. W. J. Potter. REWSZY WARD. Mr. JosPph Shaw-Green IL).. public accountant. 13; Froghall-lane and 18, King-st., is proposed by Mr. Theta Morris, ascended by Mr. John Griffiths, and'assent.d to by Messrs. Edwin Plinston, James McCarthy, Jahez. Boardman. John Houlding, Michael Chambers, Thomas Trow, William Dick; and Samuel Jones. Mr. Gregn, in another nomination is proposed by Councillor Floodi seconded by Mr. Joseph Owen. Mr. Green is also nominated by Mr. William Hewitt, and seconded by Mr. JOhn Waring. Mr. Baird is further proposed by Mr.. Arthur Jones, seconded by Mr. John-Hough.</p>
        <p>ORFORD WARD: Mr. W. P. Skelton (C), Liverpool-road, Great Sankey, general merchant,is further proposed by Mr. Jbb Ramp' ton, seconded by Mr. John Titchard, and assented to by Messrs. George Boardman, James Grounds, Thomas Lawton, George Knott, William Cross, J. R. Blease, . Jog Grounds, and Stephen Siddall. ST. lOUS El WARD, Mr. Furness is further proposed by Mr. George Thomas Brown, seconded by Or. Robert Henshall. Mr. W. J. Shaw (C), 89, Dallam-lane, plumber and painter, is further proposed by Mr. James Woods seconded by Mr. Thomas Ince,and assented to by Messrs, James Welsby,Tlenry Ball, James Taylor„Jarnes Lockett,. William Danbury, John Norbury, William Gaskell, John Hughes, and William Holbrook. FAIRFIELD WARD. Mr. Joseph Mounfield (L) is further proposed by Mr. John Griffiths, seconded by Mr. John Clayton. In another paper Mr.Longton (C) is proposed by the Rector, seconded by Dr. Jack, and supported by Messrs.. J. Gibson, J. Buckard, T. Hedgcock, It. Butterworth, J. T. Vernon, J. Smite, R. Brown, A. Mortimer, and J,' Tilley. Mr. Longton is further proposed by Mr. G. T. Brundrett, seconded by Mr. T. J. Hulse, and assented to by Messrs. John Plumpton, Thomas Riley, W. A. Storey, George Davison, James Berry, J. W. Kenyon,T. B. Rush- ton, John Swanton, and Richard Wilkins. HOWLEY WARD Mr. Arthur Browne (L) solicitor, 27, Wilson Patten- street, is proposed by Mr. C. J. Holmes, seconded by Mr. Thomas Grime, and supported by Messrs. Thomas Din- widdie, John Williams, Robert Curran' J. C. Ross, Joseph, Mounfield, Joseph Albinson, J. S. Hill, and John Gandy. He is further nominated by Mr. Samuel Wright, seconded, by Mr. James Shaw, and supported by Messrs. William Vose, George Shaw, Michael Flannery, Frank Broad- hurst, William Savory, Joseph Dennett„ Robert Jefferson, and Jane Litherland. Mr. Brown is further proposed by Mr. J. Longland, seconded by Mr. Danis/ Houghton,and supported by Messrs. F. Leigh, C. Rrindtey, W. Clegg, G. Howard, G. Maguire, P. Mannion, Timothy Sauce,. and John McCall. In another paper he is proposed and seconded by Messrs. G. Plinston, and H. Malkin res- pectiv4y. ST. AUSTIN'S WARD. Mr. James Hepherd (q), 21, Palmyra-Square, maltster, is also proposed by Mr. John White, seconded by Alder- man Wright, and assented to by Messrs. H. N. Houghton Thomas Halton, Joseph Lawless, William J. Forshaw, H. Jolley, Arthur Shawcross, John Litton. and J. P. Taylor.—Mr. William Roberts (L), 19, Arpley-St., shoe manufacturer, is proposed by Mr. J. Rymer Young, seconded by Mr. William Croskill, and supported by Messrs. John Evans, James Critchley, Thomas Crampbert Joseph Podmore, Thomas Daley, Samuel - Dunbobbin, John Pattison, Herbert Richards.—Mr. John Rymer Young (L), 9, Egypt-st., Warrington, Ghyteist, is pro- posed by Mr. John Westgarth, seconded by the Rev. John Yonge, and supported by Messrs. F. Brown, G. Titchett, B. White, S. Taylor, J. Toft, G. Holford, R. Mercer, and W. ROWS., • LATCHFORD WARD. Mr. Samuel Bennett Stringer (L), 15, Wilderspoea Causeway, gentleman, is proposed by the Rev. J. W. Varley, seconded by Mr. Wm. Speakman, and suppnrted by Messrs. J, Tilley, T. Barnes. B:Few, G. Huxley, W. T. Shag, R. Cook, J. Stringer, and W. Hayes. PULPIT REFERENCE BY THE REV. J. YONGE. The Rev. J. Yonge preaching at the Wycliffe Clsurch, on Sunday, from the text " Run ye to and fro througb,. the streets of Jerusalem, and see now, and know, and seek in the broad places thereof, if ye can find a man, if there be any that executeth judgment, that seeketh the truth ; and I will pardon it, Jeremiah 5, 1 v., referred to the forthcoming municipal election. Alter alluding to what the world of to-day considered good men, and to what God's order to Jeremiah said they should be, Mr. Yonge remarked that the tendencies of the times were not any too favourable to sensitiveness of conscience ; or to too great refinement of virtue. Many religious restraints had been considerably relaxed. Many stand- ards of truth, purity, and justice, had been a good deal lowered. There was more anxiety for what pleased and paid, than for what was good and true, and hence it came to be that good men, true principled men, were still considerably unique and rare. They sometimes needed looking for even yet God was ever on the search for these men. He sought them because he needed them. He needed them in all the walks and grades of life. There was a divine woik for these men of God every- where. God needed witnesses for Himself in all places. In workshops, in offices, in markets, in exchanges, in recreation clubs, in political clubs, in town and county councils, in boards of guardians, in the senate of the land. Men of this order should be conspicuous and in the front in all these places. How was it they were so rarely to the front in these places? especially these more public places? Be had a conviction that grew stronger In his mind every day,and it was that good men in these days were'many of them terribly neglectful of very pro- minent duty. He had been reminded of it afresh here in Warrington in these last few days, in a way which made him think of that very text. Good men were wanted just now for their Town Council. He did not wish to imply any personal \ disrespect by his words for any who already 'were members of it, or from those who are now seeking election to it ; but he would make bold to say this, for he deemed it simply beyond dispute, that the character and therefore the efficiency of our local senate needed to be considerably raised. He had met in recent months in frequent conference in this town touching some of its manifest evils, and its much needed reforms. The Opinion has been commonly ex- pressed that until the tone of the Council Chamber could be made higher, it was vain to expect progress in the social welfare of the community. What he had said more than once in some of those private conferences he would now venture to say publicly. Until the best men of the town would set aside personal convenience, their preference for leisure evenings and surburban dwellings, and come forward to take their proper share in muni- cipal work and responsibility, we should never see better social ccinditions in our town. What was it that we saw at this very time ?We saw this—a strong effort being made by members of the liquor traffic to gain scats in the Town Council. What for? To benefit the town ? Hardly! but simply so as to defend their craft, and oppose all endeavours for its regulation. In face of this why do not our intelligent men of social position, high character, aed religious repute, siandoforward to discharge their obvious duty ? Be meintained that good and.religious men, who by successful commerce in this town had gained gocial rank in it, owed a duty to the town which had been the scene of theireuccess. He should say they were evading their responsibility to this community when they suffered the places of power and authority to be filled bY inferior persons for selfish ends. Our beet men by their abstinence from the public service had suffered that service to be seriously de- teriorated. The Cauncil Chamber of a town ought to be worthy of the highest confidence of the whole com- munity. It should be not only the fountainhead of good order, but of every desirable and possible reform. As it is to-day—although be might be deemed to say ft bard</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 28, 1893.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 28, 1893.</p>
        <p>and severe thitig,.yet he was only saying what he knew to be true—it was not a, help to order, and it was u•- friendly to reform. He 'didn't say those words with a light heart, but from a deep sense of responsibility, and with an earnest desire that the day might soon come when our Front rank men, altogether irrespective or party and creed, should fill the seats Iltd the Council Chamber, and lead the community in all true social pro-- grew,. There waa one thing which seemetl to him to be of,greatest importance, and it was this, that-their chris. Sian churches should make Town Council elections, and al elections to public and legislating bodies, the matter. of thought, prayer and effort. Every voter should make it matter of conscience to vete, to influence others to vote for the truest man ; and also the churches should create a public opinion to call out the best possible men forth is work.</p>
        <p>"CHRISTIAN CITIZENSHIP. ANN ITS .RELATION TO</p>
        <p>MUNICIPAL AFFAIRS."</p>
        <p>Preaching on the above subject in the Emmanuel Church, on Sunday, the Rev. E. T. Reed, said thet from the Chief Constable's report it was found that in War-- rington 606 persons were charged with drunkenness dining the past year, and of these 491 were convicted. Now, if all these persons were drunk they must have got intoxicated somewhere, and as the only places where drink could be legally obtained were the licensed houses It was a fair inference that the great majerity hacrizot into that state in the licensed houses. Yet only sir keepers of licensed houses were proceeded against during the rear, and only four of the six were convicted: If the Watch Committee was composed of men in sympathy with the liquor trade, the laws against drunkenness and against supplying drink to drunken persons were not likely to he strictly enforced, and, as a matter°, fact, the condition of things in Warrington was such that the Watch Committee of this town, or at least a majerity of that committee, were putting obstacles in the way of the administration of the laws. The Watch Committee were responsihle for the fact that the chief constable and the police were not supported as thee' ought to be in We discharge of their very import ant duties in regard to the suppression If vice. There were laws of a stringent' nature against betting and gambline, and it was a well known fact that there were in thern town several houses ha which betting and gambling were alnp-ist openly carried on. That was known to the Watela Committee and to the police, and yet this great evil had not been put down. Young men in large numbers were known to frequent these Wane% with the resultthat their moral character was anon utterly ruined. Be (the preacher) asked why did not the Watch Committee put- a stop to that disgraceful and demoralizing state of things ? Again, it was well-known that in Warrington a' great amount of Sun lay trading was carried on. Yet there were laws on the Statute book Intended to secure for busy workers one day'S rest in seven. It was not right that honest and upright trades- men should have to contend against the unfair com- petition of those who traded on the Lord's Rey. The law on this Subject shnuld be impartially enforced but the Watch Committee were neglecting their dutyin this I matter. Why were the laws upon these points—drun- kenness, gambling, and, Sunday trading—allowed to re- main practically a dead letter?' The answer was to-be found in the character and standing of those who repre- sented the ratepayers in the Town Council. How could It be exported' that the town would be properly governed in a righteous way when there were on the Town Council at leant ten Aldermen and Councillors I wive were directly or indirectly interested an the liquor trade, while an the Watch Committee there was at least a majority of gentlemen answering to the same description. Turning now to the coming municipal election, there were no fewer than five candidates for the nine seats, who were directly interested in the liquor traffie. Was it not beyond the range of common sense to suppose that those who were carrying on a trade which was the chief cause of rice and crime would im- partially administer the laws for the suppression of that which their own trade helped to foster ? That there were many right-minded men belonging to both politi- cal parties who placed the good government of the town before the claims of party he was sanguine enough to believe, and it was to be hoped that all such would earnestly repudiate the bringing forward of candidates of that description to represent the ratepayers in the TownCeuncil. The remedy for this state of things was to be found in the' Christian electors of Warrington rigidly refusing to vote for candidates merely on party grounds, and in their supportitig gentlemen, be they Censervativeor Liberal,, who would de their utmost to advance the moral and social status of the town by mak- ing it as easy, as possible to do right and as difficult as possible to do wrong. He aopealed to the Christian citirms of 'the town to make their influence felt at the coming election on the side of sobriety, honesty, and Sabbath observance.</p>
        <p>party, and that it was a proof against all the assaults of the enemy. Councillor Fcorm who took the chair owing to Mr. Morris being compelled to leave, next addressed the 1 meeting after which Councillor Fleming moved a resolu- tion pledging those present to use every legitimate means to secure Mr. J. Shaw Green's return to the council. He said that he had been closely associated with Mr. Green for six,y ears and that gentleman had been very faithful in the discharge of his duties.—Mr. J. Griffiths seconded , and Councillor Beaman supported the resolution which was carried unanimously. The meeting was brought to a close by a vote -of thanks to the chairman proposed by Councillor J. Shaw Green and seconded by Mr. J. Owen. MEETING. hN. SUPPORT OF MR. A. BENNETT'S CANDIDATURE.</p>
        <p>--- A largely attended meeting in support of the candida- ture of Mr. A. Bennett, was held on Wednesday evening, in the National Schools, Church-st. The chair was oc- cupied by Mr. J..Fairclough, J.P ,and he was supported on the platform by Alderman Francomb, J.P., Dr. Can- nell, Messrs. .11. Lington, W. P. Skelton, T. Mason,W. Diggle and others. A resolution was passed pledging the meeting to do all in its power to secure Mr. Bennett's return on the motion of Mr. T. Mason, seconded by Mr. Meacock. Mr. H. J Longton, candidate for Fairfield Ward, addressed the meeting at some length, referring to several municipal matters contained in his election address.—Mr. A. Bennett, who on rising, was greeted with loud cheers, after referring to the smallpox epidemic, and several matters he would support if elected, contained in his address, he spoke of the com- plaints about the van nuisance. Mr. Diggle, he said. saw him the previous day and told him he would with- draw from the contest, and he was glad to see him on the platform that night., He (the speaker) was a bit inclined to think that Mr- niggle had not met with the attention which he deserved, and if:Mr. Diggle brought forward any suggestion relating to the van nuisance or other matters, which seemed to him (the speaker 1 prac- ticable, they would receive his support. Mr. Bennett afterwards referred to the unpaved and unswept streets in the town,and said lie heard numerous cothplaints that the watering carts were to be seen about when they were rut wanted, but when dust was about the watering carts were not at work. After referring to the inter- cepting depots,Mr, Bennett spoke on the question of gas, and said he didn't think the gas itself was very good. He, thought also that the Corporation should be able to supply the gas at a lower rate, or make a profit out of it to assist the rates. The policy of the Corporation em- ploying the unemployed in a time like the present com- mended itself to him, and deserved all the encourage- ment they could give to it. Referring to the Art Gal- lery, the speaker said it seemed a disgrace to a town like Warrington, which had had in her midst such men as Luke Fildes, Henry Woods. George Sheffield, Warring- ton Wood, and others,that the Art Gallery scarcely con- tained a single specimen of their art. He also was in favour of branch libraries being established in different parts of the town, and anything to brighten the lives of the people would have a warm claim upon his sympathy. But he told them straightway he didn't want to see the rates increased. He thought the gas and water works ought to give a greater inosrne than they did, to the assistance of the rates. The baths, now that they had been made infinitely better, might also make a profit, and he had an idea they would do so. He also thought if homes for the people were established and worked successfully,they might also relieve the local rates. Mr. Bennett also spoke of the filthy sate of the river, con- trasting it with the time when salmon was caught in it, and said he was in sympathy with measures being taken for purifying it. The speaker also dwelt on the subject of widening the principal streets of the town, and said the subject of the Council taking action in regard to trams had been suggested to him, and this lie thought would be a great convenience.—Alderman Francomb proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. Bennett for his address, and said he should have liked to have had time to an- swer Mr.l3ennett.There was the hard fact of rates which must be kept down. Mr. %V. P. Skelton seconded the motion. Dr. Cannell moved a vote of thanks to the Chairman, which was seconded by Mr. Diggle. The , votes of thanks were carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>BEWSEY WARD. MEETING OF COUNCILLOR J. SHAW GREEN'S SUPPORTERS.</head>
        <p>BEWSEY WARD. MEETING OF COUNCILLOR J. SHAW GREEN'S SUPPORTERS.</p>
        <p>- - - A alerting was held in the Co-operative.Hall,Winwioltz- st., on Monday evening, in support of the candidature of Councillor I.4haw Green in the Bewsey ward. That, was a very good attendance and the chair as occupied by Mr. 7. l‘l_rris, who was supported on the platform by the candidate,. Councillors Fleming, Flood, and Bea- man and Messrs. Win. Furness, and J.Griffiths. The CHAIRMAN. remarked that he was very sorry that . they should be in the throes of an election this year., There was no election last year because of the epidemic of smallpox, but this year although there was no small- pox, there was something worse. (Hear, hear.) Last year the ratepayers grumbled because the smallpox epidemic was going to cost the town two or. three hundred pounds, hut to-day there was an epidemic of poverty which was losing the town between £3.000. £t,oooa week. He "hoped that the party which had been the cause of this election would lose all round. (Applause.) The most to the town and to the candidates would be some £3OO and this £3OO could very well go to tbe distress fund. (Hear, hear.) He had come to the meeting to reCommend his friend Shaw Green. He had known him for 20 years; and he was a man who could grapple finance in an extraordinary manner, and he had not been in the Council more than twelve months before lie was made vice-chairman of the Finance Com- mittee: He thoroughly understood financial questions. The same could not be said of Mr. Baird,, as he had not yet been tried. He wanted to know what Mr. Green had done amiss as it was those who had black marks against them that they kicked out. Mr. Green had had an excellent record as he had attended 12S meet- ings a t the Town Hall, during the present year, and he (Mr. Morris) hoped that he would be returned to the Council by a very large majority. Councillor J. SHAW GREEN who was received with loud applause, after expressing regret that an election should be sprung upon the town at the present time, said that when he was first elected, he tried to keep politics out of the Council, but he soon found that there was a certain party in that Council which was only non- political -.vhen they were not affected. As long as they got everything on their side they were non-political, but when the other party thought they ought to have some- thing it was a different matter. After alluding to his work in connection with the water works, Mr. Green came td the subject of the School Attendance Commit- tee. In a certrin remarkable publication puhlisha trip to this town, he and the rest of that Committee were accused of spending the ratepayers' money to have to the Clio. Well, he might say that that Committee had a tremendous amount of responsibility put upon it. They were responsible for 38 children who were sent away from home, and they only thought it right that these children should be visited sometimes. He would, however, show the doings of another Committee. That Committee was presided over by a Councillor commonly called " Colonel" Hutchinson. This Committee asked the Council to orders new fire engine. This fire engine was ordered,and should havebeen delivered two months arr., although as far as he knew it had not arrived yet. The same Committee some time ago actually suggested to the Council that a deputation should be sent to Lon- don to see the engine before it was painted. (Laughter.) They evidently wanted to haves honday, and he might tell the meeting that the particular Committee alluded to, was composed chiefly of Conservatives. The Tory party was complaining of the spending of £3 or £4 by the Committee which bore all the. responsibility of the training of these boys, and yet they would speed £lO or £l5 for a pleasant journey to London to look at a fire engine. (Laughter.) Alluding to his work on the Finance Committee, he said that formerly that Commit- tee did not know what the different committees had been spending until' the figures were published by the borough treasurer. By a little ingenious device he himself intro- duced, that was altered, and the Committee knew the expenditure of the different committees each month. He thought it should be possible to pay the rates in one pla-e. At present, the Improvement Rate was p rid at the Town Hall, the Water Rate at Cairo-st., and the Gas Account at Mersey-st. fle,W(lB of opinion that there should be one central cashier at one central 'p'ace for all the accounts to be ?aid in. It would not be long before that arrangement was made. There was a tremendous amount of expenditure to look forward to in the near future. There was the widening of Bridge-street, Horsemarket-strect, Sankey-street, and Buttermarlitt-street. Then there was an extension of the Gas works required. and also a new police court. A new sewerage scheme was also necessary,and the council were going to apply for an extension of the borough to take in Sankey Bridges in connection with the scheme. There was also a large amount of insanitary property in the town which required removieg. Considering that so much was to be done in the future, it was necessary that they should take rare wleem they sent to the council. His opponent, Mr. Baird, in his election address said that he represented the largest ratepaying firm in the town. That statement caused a lot of awkward questions to be. asked. What was that firm? and how did it get, its money ? The trade which Mr. Baird represented, grew rich on the sufferings of others. Personally, he was an upright, honourable man, but he (Mr. Green) thought that it was a great m'stake for him to mention the firm lie represented. There were four out of 'nine Conserva- tive candidates this year directly cmnected with the drink trade. It showed that that party had fallen into the clutches of the drink party, although he was glad to say that there were some Conservatives who had refused to bow the knee to Baal. It was of the grratest importance that they should not send a man who only represented a single firm,but one who would represent the whole ward. He hoped that on November Ist, they would prove that Bewsey ward was an impregnable felt of the Liberal</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>ONE VIEW OF THE LOCK-OUT.</head>
        <p>ONE VIEW OF THE LOCK-OUT.</p>
        <p>£3,000 A WEEK LOST TO WARRINGTON. At the annual dinner of the Warrington Cricket and Bowling Club, held at the Lion Hotel, on Satur- day night, Mr. T. Morris, F. 0.5., in responding to the toast of the "Town and Trade of Warrington " said : I have never before in my life, either in %Vol- verliampton or in Warrington, had to speal: of such melancholy, nay, criminal associations as those from which the trade, and as a natural consequeece the people of Warrington are now suffering. Our workmen are in enforced idleness, and I will not say it isaltogether because of the leaders of the colliers. The colliers them- selves are to blame. I may as well speak straight on this subject, for Ido not think I ever felt so much grieved as Ido now at the suffering our workmen have to endure. It is a most serious thing for Warrington inasmuch as it is a manufacturing town. (Hear, hear.) An enormous amount of coal is used here, and if the manufacturers cannot get that coal their works must be and the labourers cast adrift. Everybody suffers, whether employers or workmen. Now, I would like to say this,, that while I believe the coal-owners made a mistake in asking for such a large reduction in the men's wages, I do say this, that if it is true—and it is quite on the cards—that the Germans and Belgians are taking the iron trade from this country, it follows that each penny that the collier gets in excess of what he is entitled to, is knocking a nail into John Bull's coffin, and is striking a heavy and perhaps fatal blow at the iron sides of old England. (Applause.) There is no doubt that trade, in nearly all its branches, is leaving our shores. The cotton trade is going to India ; and in all our possessions where cotton is grown fac- tories are being established, and the spinning is done on the spot. If the iron trade leaves us, England's wealth will begin to go in earnest. '(Hear, hear.) We have all read the newspapers, and I think we all view the pre- sent struggle very seriously ; personally, I feel very strengly about it.. It was thought about 18 years ago that there would he trouble with the puddlers. Fortun- ately, however, there was no strike, but it was border- ing on a strike. They thought that they ought not to have less than 7s. a ton, and so twelve men met twelve ironmasters, with an independent man as arbitrator be- tween them. The men stated their case, and the reply of the masters was to the effect that " If you will not take a penny lower than 78. a ton f6r puddling we will not give you anything higher than 12s. a ton." The 1 bargain was struck and the men kept to their work. Some months after, they asked for Is. in the pound ad- vance, and it was agreed to. A few years later they applied for is. 6d., and that was agreed to likewise with- out resorting to a strtkr. Therefore, while I contend that the coalowners made too large a demand, I consider that the colliers and their leaders should without strik- ing have gone to the coalowners and said—" Now we think the miners should have so much per day, or for so many yards of coal that they get," and then have asked the doalowners to meet them and settle on a cer- tain wage. If the miners determined not to go below a certain sum the masters could have said, as was done in the case of the puddlers. " Then you cannot go higher than such sum as might be fixed by us." Why should not that course have been taken and so have avoided an unhappy strike which is losing us millions of money within a radius of 20 miles of Warrington ? (Hear, hear.) Will you he astonished when I tell you that dur- ing the last eleven weeks from £2,500 to 2'3,600 nes week have been lost to the workingmen of Warrington ? All that money was formerly spent every week in the town solely through the iron trade. So you see, property ! owners, shopkeepers, and even bankers must be suffer- ing. I say, therefore, it is an unrighteous thing that : the leaders of the colliers did not attempt to settle the dispute with the coal owners without resorting to extreme measures, which have such far-reaching conse- quences and entail such universal suffering. They suould have done this 14 weeks ago, and I am sure the coalowners would have met them in a reasonable spirit.. The masters have asked arbitration, and the men (or their leaders) have said " We have so much to-days kad we will get that or we will starve." I say it is a sin ;if they were the only sufferers I would not cars what course they took. But there are 180,000 colliers not working at the present time, and that means that twenty times that number of persons are affected in consequence. It is an awful state of things, and it shows' a laak of common-sense and common feeling. (A, Voice: " Bartarous.") it is not what it should be, and our educational system is not wot th a fig if in this year of our Lord 1893 so many men can be found who by their actions do not show themelves deservine of a better title than " barbarians." If coal cannot be obtained the whole country will go to ruin. We cannot keep our manufactories working without coal, and if science can- not do anything, common sense, and judgment and ells- crttion must be exerised to bring our workmen and capitalists together. England may as well die to-morrow as submit to be beaten by Belgium and Germany ; and even France is beating us. Workingmen can live in this country in spite of all the royalties that are talked about. It is all a farce to parade the royalty question. If there were no royalties at all, we could not beat the Germans, and it is simply because our labour is paid ftr at too high rate. At the same time royalties should bear their share of losses when trade is bad. Our men go to :Brussels, and they talk about the labour question, and the labour leaders in BrusPels and Germany gull our representatives. We pull the chestnuts out of the fire for them and burn our fingers instead of letting them pull them out themselves. The Germans and Belgians are,as a matter of fact,grow- ing rich on the ignorance and stupidity of our workmen and coalowners. God forbid that this state of things should eentinue one week longer. (Loud applause.)</p>
        <p>The Royal Laundresses say Critchley's " STARCH GLOSS is unequalled for imparting a beautifnl finish to starclird I:nen, ezc., either cold or boiling water starch. Mr. —, Corn- wall, says :—" My wife will not finish our linen without your "Starch Gloss.'" From Mrs. —, Bradford : "Dear sli.— I am pleased to Inform you that I have tried many so-called Starch Glazes and Polishes, but find none equal to yours." It should be used in every house. Sold at ld., 33., cud fid., by most starch sellers. If not in stock will procure it. T. CUITCHLET. Blackburn. P.vprietor.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>"THE LABOUR QUESTION."</head>
        <p>"THE LABOUR QUESTION."</p>
        <p>SPEECH BY MR. A. H. CROSFIELD.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. 11. Crosfiehl took the chair at a meeting of the Warrington Debating society on Tuesday even- ing, when a debate on " The Labour Question" was opened by Mr. John Gandy. In the course of his speech in winding up the debate, and referring to remarks made on the subject of the history of labour, Mr. Crosfield said what was that but a record of the gradual steps by which labour had achieved its main object in securing a larger share in the products of industry. He said industry advisedly, because there were different kinds, and labour alone did not create value, for that consistod in exchanged ability which was the result of labour plus direction and demand, and the services of capital in securing the desired result had to be recognised. It had been suggested that profit shark ing was perhhps the best final solution of the labour - question. (Hear, hear.) In some very important in- dustries indeed, he ventured to say that the workmen', made a great mistake if they did not feel that they had shared very fairly of late years in. the results of indus- . . . .</p>
        <p>, try. No doubt in the early part of this and in preceding centuries capital was able to make pretty well its own terms with labour. But those comparatively easy times, for capitalists were gone. References had been made to the work of trades unions. So far as he under- stood their working he did not think they were faultless. He did not think they always acted in the best interests of commerce. and ultimately of the community. (Hear, hear.) But they were needed and they had done and were doing real good service.,' (Applause) And while the conditions of labour had been</p>
        <p>improved in many directions without them, he could not but think their mere example had tended a good deal to bring about that result. (Hear, hear.) But they were told this was not sufficient ; they must take another step and that a great departure from the rid lines of progress. There came from several quarters the demand for nationalization ; and he was sorry to see that Mr. Burns, for whom he kid a very great respect, lent the weight of lVs name and influence in favour of letting, as he puts it, the state run the commercial machine. He should like to say a word or two upon this project of nationalization. The object was to make profits go to the nation instead of the individual. But the very first question they had to consider was this,—will there be any profits? Think of tfte cost of carrying out this plan. They were bound to assume compensation at fair valua- tion. (Hear, hear.) He observed even the most extreme men—or some of them at least—took this for granted. Common honesty and what is perhaps equally impor- tant,common interest—(langhter)—would revolt at any- thing else; fir a greater or less interest in the rights of property extended throughout, not merely A fraction, but the majority of the whole people. And therefore he supposed state bonds would to:, offered in exchange for land and the whole int truments of production. Lie did not know whether they would ba accepted. But assum- ing that the Government had sufficient authority for the purp Ise, did they think it could make it pay sufficiehtly to cover even the interest on the bonds at the existing rate of wages let alone at an advance ? That was not a question they could answer off-hand perhaps, but they had a good many facts to guide them in forming an opinion. Some of them very likely noticed a remark made during the recent dispute in the coton trade by Mr. Mawdsley to the effect that though Socialism had not attained any hold on Lancashire, this, and in other words the nation- alization of the cotton mills, %night have to be considered if certain individuals pushed things to extremes. Ile received the very prompt answar that a good many in the cotton trade would be very glad to hand over their mills at cost price. There are lots of largo manufac- turers in the cotton trade, who if you take the last 10 years could hardly show interest on capital. (Hear, hear.) And there were plenty who could not show it. And they knew very well gentlemen,that the same could be said of other important industi ies. The position was at present, that it capitalists, by special ingenuity and ability of their own, were able to secure certain advant- ages, they were still enabled to reap them. Enterprise and skill in commerce was protected by the patent law just in the same way a; the scholar, was enabled to reap the reward he secured by, the fertility of his brains, through our copyright law. Take for instance Mr.lluskin froin whose writings they had had some eloquent extracts that night. Ile was able to charge a high price some of them thought too high a price, for hie books, thanks n . . . . . . .</p>
        <p>„ . the different copyrights by which they were protected. , As regarded large branches of commerce which many firms were competing on more or less even terms, the pressure on the one hand of conflicting capital and on the other of labour, kept down interest let alone profit, within a very reasonable limit. (Elear, hear.) Then he asked them to consider, that at the head of these firms they had men for the most part of experience arid shrewdness and stimulated by the constant pressure of personal interest and competition. They had in acidi tion the advantage of the right to exercise their author- ity and maintain discipline so as to obtain good work from those in their employ. Are a vast body of officials even if drawn from the same class, but minus this pres- sure and incentive likely to produce equally good I results 1' He did :IA believe it. The cost of production would rise and we should lose our position as leaders of the commercial world. As it was we had been losing some ground in our huge export trade on which 80 inane millions depended for subsistence. It seemed to him that wholesale collectivism would inevitably and heavily increase that loss. Of course he knew the remedies of the ideal socialists, but he was not going to exaapina the fallacies under that head, on which he had had his say elsewhere. The better side of socialism, if they omitted the grave and fatal drawbacks to this, carried to its ultimate extreine,seemed plausible enough, hut it was not in the extbietion of individual enterprise that labour vt as going to flud salvation. Of course he did not tnean that pure individualism was the alternative. Mr Frodk. Harrison remarked rated r naively the other day that while there was no diubt a great deal of nonsense talked.as socialism, what they wanted was someone to point out what was practicable, and what was imprac- ticable. IVlty, of course, they did, and he for one had rather hoped that Mr. Harrison might in certain direc- tions have thrown some light on the subject.. To deter- mine how far individualism or collectivism was to pre- vail in the future was however beyond the power of Mr. Harrison or anyone.. (Hear, , hear.) In the Meantime though the conditions of trade and international compe- tition absolutely precluded advance on the present rate of wages, and though in the case of some businesses there• might be a choice between some reduction and shutting up, still in many ways, cost and interests of labour would, he helievt d, gain in the future. Without striv- ing for an equality which lord no counterpart in nature they could and must aim in the widest and fairest sense of the term, for eqnalizat ion of opportunity.. (Applause.), Tee want of better opportunities for rational amusement mid recreation, physical and mental—(hear,. hear)—the need for substitutes for, the redundance of tipping shops —(hear, hear)—which some pe ,pie were so desirous to retain in their lull cempiement, gave plenty of scope for individual and collective activity. They needed in their large towns the same admirable civic development which struck anyone entering Birmingham—that remarkable centre and example of health,, progress. and local self government. In order that these things might he the better carried out in the future, the incidence of taxation would need to be mended —and graduatad taxation was therefore a question which they had yet to deal with. (Applause.) Capital accutnulated in times of healthy industry was readily enough applied to those objects which tended to make wages go further in securing the advantages of advancing civilisation. And in this connection it was fair to remark that labour was by no means uninterested in the increase rather than the decrease of capital. Again, as labour abroad approached in its ideas more nearly to the standards of English labour that would all tie in favour of employers and employed at home. With re- gard to the unemployed,there was one great branch of home industry that they mu.st stiffelook to, to relieve them out of the difficulty, and further, of the increasing pressure of their population. One of the greatest boons to the whole country would be a successful scheme for small holdings, and the development of that incentive to cultivation, which„ given security and confidence to the peasant cultivators, would conduce to the renewed agricultural prosperity. (Applause.) The great indus- trial conflict which was exercising such a disastrous effect on the whole country's trade had naturally been dwelt upon by some of the speakers. Had not the agri- cultural question a very direct connection with it ? (Hear, hear.) Agricultural labourers in the midland counties-earning 12. s a week preferred to work three days in a coal mine for 14s. It was a question how far this increase of labour should be counteranced by ccalowners, or encouraged, as Iliad heard it alleged correctly or in- correctly by the miners' leaders. He supposed that while the output of coal had for some time past remained at a pretty even figure the number of colliers had increased by at least rio,ooo. A great deal had been said on one side about depression of trade, and on the other needless and gratuitous com- petition, as a reason for reduced prices and wages. Doubtless there had been a certain amount of cut-throat competition but surely by far the most important cause of these reduced, prices was the falling off in demand.. The greater skill shown by German manufacturers in applying science and chemis•.ry to the manufacture of iron and steel, together with the low price o: labour ins these industries there, had enabled them to undersell us in many foreign markets and positively in our own, where we read with dismay of the Glasgow City Council the other day ordering rails fort ramways from Germany, and of large manufacturers at our own doors ordering iron plant from abroad. The effect of this of course was to force English manufacturers of iron and steel to buy coal cheaper, or if they could not, not to buy at all. If coal owners won't supply them cheaper then their own output fal:s off and the expenses of working the mine go up, while if wages were maintained at the full ad- vance—as these form 70 to 80 per cent, of the total cost of putting coal free on rails—there was only left to the owner the choice_between evils, aud tho result of the high wages was in the end shorter time and less work for the collier, and therefore, if auything, a falling off in the weekly wage. Public sympathy had, on the whole, been largely with the colliers, hut let them be just on both sides, If the masters. vrsv, 7ait ill-advised in refusin</p>
        <p>4 the• Mayor of Sheffield's proposal and in making their counterproposal for a 15 per cent. reduction, were the menwise and acting in the interests. not alone of others, but In the end, of themselves, in refusing to listen to any ieducttorr? There was one other question which affects the wliole interest of capital and lanour perhaps as much as anything. He scarcely dared to breathe the name of bl-inetallikra at that hour still less should he think of making any' remarks on it as he was booked for a piper on the subject in December. ((lear, hear.) In conclu- sion,lettim iust remark that though there might be necessarily a certain amount of higgling in the great market for labour and capital as in other markets, yet if the interests of the tw&amp; were in some respects opposits. they had yet a great deaf more in common and when they 'came to close quarters in any disastrous struggle like the present,. then as had been remarked when the visors were lowered, each, would recognise in the countenance of the other his own likeness.</p>
      </div>
