      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <p>-</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BRANCH OF Titig. ENGLISH CHURCH UNION.</p>
        <p>In St. Austin's Chambers, Bold-street, oti Tuesday .afternoon, the annual meeting of the Warrington . branch of the English Church Union was held under .the presidency of Canon Heron. There were also present :—The Mayoress (Mrs. Edward Greenall), • the Hers. E:.Dt Garven, F Slaw, W. Stanton, A. C. Hordern, H. Ttott, H. Vernon, J. Robertson, and T. 0. Marshall (London); Messrs. John White, Herbert Woods, H. Nt Itonghton, James Bolton, W. Johnson, •H. B. Melville,. John Hankinson, B. Hutchinson, Stc.</p>
        <p>Canon Mem,. who had been carried into the room owing to feebleness, addressed the meeeing at the outset, rand gate many reminiscences of the early days of the Union, when, he said he was persuaded to become an active membar,.owing to the vituperations of a minister 'in the locality who, had been in the habit of denouncing the Union, both from the pulpit and the platform. The rev. gentleman spoke of the great assistance which the Union rendered to the Church of England generally, but regretted the fan': that it seemed to find so little support from the bishops. . . _</p>
        <p>, Mr. JOHN WHOM, hon. sec.' presented the following report for the year ending October :—" The present number of metnbers and associates of the branch is 89, showing an increase of six over last year. Since the annual meeting last year one meeting of the branch has been held in February last year, when a very interesting address was delivered by the Rev. W. F. Cable on Church Reform.! Other meetings would have been held but for the fact that a Minch Society had been formed in the town, and had arranged a series of lec- tures on the Reformation, and wlsich were exceedingly well attended. As4nailar series of lectures was being arranged by the same society for the following winter season." The electiomof, officers was next proceeded with. The Rev. E. D. Gersten proposed that the Rev. Canon Heron I be elected president. Mr. H. Woods seconded, and the motion was carried.—On the motion ot the Rev. F. Slater, seconded by Mr. H. N. Houghton, the Rev. E. D. Garven was re-elected vice president. Mr. John White was re-appointed hon. secretary, and Mr. J. Bolton hon. treasurer, a committee consisting of the Rev. F. Slater, Rev. W. Stanton, Mr. H. N. Houghton and Mr. C. S. Burgess also being appointed. The Rev. T. EX hlaesnatt next addressed the meeting upon some of the difficulties which the Union had to meet. Among other things he mentioned that there was an increasing difficulty complained of greatly by the Church in getting sufficient money to carry on their schools. Money was in itself a temporal thing, but the maintenance of those schools was not simply temporal— the religious training of the children was by no means temporal. But the difficulty did not lie in procuring the money. There was much reserve wealth among the laity—there were wealthy members of the Church who could be, by asking, persuaded to give to the cause some portion of the m .ney which they usually expended in china and pictures and so forth. Yes, they had a source of wealth in that direction still untapped. Their great difficulty was in getting the children properly instructed. They were not properly in- structed n3w for it was a fact that when the children grew up very few of them became communicants, and many. of them went over to the Nonconformists. Then respecting the Beard schools, there were many wrong impressions abrcad. It was commonly supposed that the children could not he put under a religious instruc- tion of any definite denomination. He assured them they could, and in many Board schools the Apostles Creed, the Lords prayer, and the ten commandments were taught, though the teaching of a catechism was forbidden. Churchmen as ratepayers had a right to take part2in the management et the Board schools, and it lay with them to ga a majority on the Board and to determine greatly the nature of the religious instruction given. In respect of the Parish Councils Bill he said there were several clauses in it which churchmen objected to. They were matters which would injure the church, :although the clauses he thought appeared in the, Bill simply through the careless manner in which it was drawn up. The chief clause was the one by which the school property managed by churchwardens would be handed over to the management of the district or parish councillors. To meet the emergencies of this Bill, and for other purposes the English Church required to be more fully represented in the House of Commons. At present it was worse off than any Noneonform'st body, even the Salvationists, who by the disturbances at East- bourne were able to get passed a special bill repealing a good act which enabled the authorities of the town to for- bid the playing of music in the streets.—The meeting was concludtd by the passing of votes ofthanks to the presi- dent, and to the Rev. T. D. Marshall for his address.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>ST,. HELENS MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>ST,. HELENS MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>FOUR WARDS TO BE CONTESTED.</p>
        <p>The Town Clerk of St. Belem (Mr. W. J. Jeeves) at five o'clock on Tuesday night dislarged that in four of the wards of the borough, contests would take'place. and that in five other wards the retiring councillors had hetm returned -unopposed. The following were the nom illations : Central Ward.—Mr. J. B. Leach (L), auctioneer and land agent, Mat Wand Lodge, Huyton Park, Huvton, had six proposers and six seconders.,—Mr. Peter M'Kinley (ALab.), smallware dealer, Bridge-ittreet, St. Helens, pro- posed and seconded by 'lhomas Foster and Charles Shulßebotliani, Robert Pownall and James Myers. Mr. M'Kinley is the new candidate. , North Eccleston Ward.—Mr. J. B. Rose (L), wholesale and retail confectioner, West Park-road, St. Helens, pro- posed and seconded by Thomas Sandford and Joseph Appleton.—Mr. Charles Liptrot (Lab. and C), licensed victualler, The Beehive Hotel, Bridge-street, St. Helens, proposed by John Morris mad John Fairclough.—Mr. Benjamin B. Glover (C), colliery proprietor and rope manufacturer, Line-grove, St. Anne's, proposed and seconded by Henry Ellard and Thomas Clitherow. Mr. Rose and Mr. Liptrot are the new candidates. Parr Ward—Mr. Frederick William Fletcher (L), ; pawn-broker, 23, Corporation-street, St. Helens,proposed ; and seconded by George Dixon and William Farthman. —Mr. F. J. Brown (C), mineral water manufacturer, Bishop-street, Parr, proposed and seconded by W. Edmondson and W. Greenall. Mr. Fletcher is the new candidate. \West Sutton Ward.—Mr. Henry Water Griffin (Lab.), artificial teeth manufacturer,. 4, Cotham-street, St. Helens, proposed and seconded by Henry Rawlins and George Conway.—Mr. Edward Johnson (C), retired pro- . visionalealer, proposed and seconded by W. J. Thomson. and James Ashton. Mr. Griffin is the new candidate. Hardsbaw Ward.—Mr. John Forster (L), engineer,. Cowley Hill-lane, St. Helens, proposed and seconded by Thomas Cook and Robert McNicoll. Unopposed. North Windle Ward.—Mr. A. J. Foote (U), retied. I grocer, 52, Kirkland-street, proposed and seconded by W. F. M'Donald and Henry Broady. Unopposed. South Windle Ward.—Mr. Thomas WLachlan ZL), provision dealer, 164, Duke-street, proposed and sea/Soled by John Rimmer and James Prem. Unopposed. East Sutton Ward.—Mr. Charles Walsh (L), draper, Peeker's Hill-road,. Sutton, proposed and seconded. by Robert Shaw and William Robinson. Unopposed. South Eccleston Ws td.—Mr. Joseph Massey (C;,.solizi- tor, Scholes House,. Eeeleston, proposed and seconded by James Malkin Nod Henry S. Oppenheim. UnopTosed. The surprise came in the nomination of MT, asaao It. Rose (L) for North Eccleston Ward, the only candidate mentioned prior to Tuesday as in the running against Mr. Glover beiog Mr. Liptrot. Both Mr. Glow and Mr. Liptrot are Conservatives, but the former,- is a colliery proprietor,, and the latter lias figured conepicuoualy at the Central Soup Kitchen in the relief of distressed women and children. North Eccleston is.a.Conservative Ward, aad if Mr. Glover and Mr. Liparot go to the poll, there May be a chance for Mr. Rose to slipin between. Mr. Peter M'Kinley is a Liberal, and Air. J: B. Leach is a Liberal. The former hopes to oust Vie latter on the Labour ticket, but his•chances seem; very uncertain, as Mr-. Leach's friends contend that. Se. has rendered effici- ent service to the working people-both in a,nd out of the Council. He is the founder of the.free breakfast to poor children on New Year's Day, Mr. Frederick Fletcher goes on party lines, and expecta,to.give a gocd account of himself, although Mr. Brmen.has served Parr Ward a good many years. Mr. Heney Griffin, as a Liberal and Labour candid ite, has made up his mind to try conclu-. sions with Mr. Edward Johnson (C)at 'rhatto Heath,l3o with what success it is daticult at present to predict.</p>
        <p>Naltkenhoff of Geneva says that there are a.u.oct blind persons in Eoiwpe, moatiy from fevers, anti ttlat 75 per cent. would 'we kept Moir sight had tirq baen properly treated.,</p>
