        <p>PRAYERS FOR .TilE DEAD IN THE</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>ENGLISH CHV4tO}l.</head>
        <p>ENGLISH CHV4tO}l.</p>
        <p>JUDGMENT BY CHANCELLOR ESPIN.</p>
        <p>Chancellor Espin; presiding at the Chester Con-</p>
        <p>sistory Court, on Thursday,ga—ve jedgraeut in a case involving the question of the legality of prayers for.' the dead in the Established Church. On the 28th September an application was made to the court for a faculty authorising.an inscription to be placed in the Parish Church of Old Rode, which inscription asked for prayers for the departed. Chancellor Espiu said that •when (ho matter was first . .</p>
        <p>brought before the court he referred to Hie well known case of Breeles v. Wonlfrey, but he dills° on the spur of the moment, not having seen the inscription until just before the court opened. On consideration, he said, it might be doubted whether that case and the judgment on it would have warranted that court in sanctioning the proposed inscription at Odd Rode. fhe ease referred to was indeed a leading case, and the judgment was a care- ful one. The judge, Sir Herbert Jenner Fust, was rec- koned a strong judge. But he (Sir Herbert Jenner Pint) did not giye direct sancti,n to the incription before hiM; he only refused to order the tombstone which bore it, to be removed. It did not appear that he would himself have authorised th4l inscription had he been asked to do so. Then, again, it might be argued thatthee Odd Rode inscription—" Of your charity pray for the soul of Henrietta Francis," &amp;c,—went somewhat beyond the one placed on the tomb of Mr.Woolfrev in the church yard of Carisbroko. In the church of St..Saviour,Leeds, built when Dr. Hood was vicar, thou,gh not by him, was' an inscription which ran nearly thus : ." All ye that enter this church, pray for the sinner that built it." It . must be some 50 years since that church was dedicated, and no doubt the builder had long ago gone to his account. He (the Chancellor) supPoseci also the bishop —it would be Bishop Lougley, afterwards Archbishop of Canterbury—did not disapprove of it, or he would have ordered it to be erased before ..onseernsing the church. Something to the purpose were the inscriptions placed beneath several stained-glass windows in the church of Llangurig, Montgomeryshire, to which his atten- tion had been called by Colonel Lloyd-Verney, but it seemed prettyrcertain, whatever be their signi.. ficance, that they were placed in the church with- out any authority; and therefore illegally. There was no doubt that in private devotion the practice of praying for the departed was retained by many bishops. and theologians, John Wesley amongst them ; but the definite forms of such prayers as were. found in the first English prayerbook of 1549-, were withdrawn in 1552,. and never restored in the revisions which followed in 1559, 1603, and 1661; And it was noteworthy that our reviewers hesitated thus, because they did on the last occasion restore the commemoration of the faithful. di- parted to the Church militant pram. Bishop Cosin had left it on record that certain words in the first thanks- giving after the Communion were retained in toe service of set purpose as a cumulative expression intended to in.. clods both the living and the dead Still, admitting that, the words were only an oblique and general form of such prayers as they were now considering. They had not that direct and personal reference and application which the proposed inscription exhibited Some devout persons had recently hoped t hat:such- intercessions for the departed should he reinserted in the Liturgy as were in the Prayer Bock of 150; but that reinsertion had not vet - taken place, and the reason for caution was clear. (kit , of the ancient prayers for the faibrilul departed grew the motion that they needed to be succoured by the prayers of the living; hence, that they were undergoing suffer-. logs and even torments from which the prayer of survi- vors might assist.to relieve them; and so by rapid strides we reached the Arman doctrine condemned by the 2n4, Article. Prayers-for the dead were unquestionably associ- ated in the popular mindmitla the. latter .exaggeration, referred tom n that Article, arid it might be added that a• bequest made for suelvprayers being offered would be void by the common law of the vim as superstitious.. Therefore—though in private such prayers mighk be offered as conformable to the ways of the primitive Church, certainly from the second century _downward, and however deeply one might sympathise - with sentiments of affectionate regard in the bereave4h fired as thy often were by strong realising of the truth of the communion of saints—it did not seem to belong to a court of first instance to do what the formularies-of the church had abstained from doing. It was not for' him there to authorise dinectly the setting up in a place of public worship of an inscription demanding the prayers of the worshippers for the souls of certain persons who had departed this life. Mr. Crunap (who appeared in support of the applica- tion) said that the original applic4tion had been with.</p>
        <p>drawn, and an amen-ded applicaljon, omitting from the proposed inscription the words in question, hadibeeni made and was /Inopposed. The Chancellor said that was ro, and thelacuity had been decreed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>SIR WILFRED' LAWSON ON MB HOUSE</head>
        <p>SIR WILFRED' LAWSON ON MB HOUSE</p>
        <p>OF \LORDS.</p>
        <p>Sir Wafted Lawson attended the annual meat4ig of the Liberal Club at VVigtert, Cumberland, on l'huraday night, and supported a resolution of confidence in the Govern- ment.- He said the Liberal members had been sent to the House of Commons tor the purpose of giving flame . Rule to Ireland,.and a Home Rule Bill had been carried triumphantly through that House—(cheers.—but this was the most extraordinary country that. the sun ever shono upon. They set one thing against another. When they had a.general election, am] after a gre:it deal of of shouting and conspiring and perspiring, writing and fighting, they sent five Liberal members from Cumber- land, they thought they should izet something, but what was the end of it all P It was just likoo.donkey race in which.the one that came in last won. (Laughter.) Be- sides the douse of Commons, which Wa4.4 a constructive House, they had the Rouse of Lords, which was an ob- structive !Lame, a Efotpe which was kept up by the people of- this country to destroy what:the people wanted. It-was a most absurd monstrosity, and a LICOat monstious absundi. (Cheers.) There ought to be a line added to.flte National Anthem— Let laws and learning, wealth, .and. commerce die, lint leave us still our old alisizOlty., (Laughter.) After generations of misgovernment lir Ireland, which had brought disgrace and disaster and danger and real disintegration in that country. Mr. Glad- stone suddenly decided to substitute cortiVatian for coercion so sat; make the two.nations friends for ever- more. He passed a bill through the House oh COrilalOYls, and it was worried like a fox by a pack of hounds in the 'IOUS° Of Lords. For his.part, he would send no more bills ta the House of Lords. Bife.was too (Mentor these maim-believes. There was a bigger question than Home Rule to be considered now., They had to settle whether- the democracy or the aristocracy were to govern this country. (Cheers.) Thattwas a Tatter in the hands of the people. If they deeithd in favour of the aristocracy, he did not see much good in the memtrrs of the House of Commons being made fools of, but if they decided in favour of the dinnoeracy there was no place at all for the- Heusi). of Lords. (Cheers.) They might ask what was to be done with the House of Lards at the present kincture., If Mr. Gladstone felt that he had the nation at his bark he would find half a dozen ways of dealing wlth them. There were several courses open. They might elect peers to swamp the poesent House. They might petition the Queen not to summon the House of f,,erds a,ny More. Then, as the people paid for working the House of lords, the House of Commons might refuse to vote suppfies, and the fourth stwestion was that the House of Commons should pass a. bill abo- lishing the House of Lords—.(cheers),-.,.and if the House ' of Lords refused to pasa that bill the Commons should ' stop the supplies. That would bring them to their bear- ings. (Loud cheers.),</p>
        <p>FE AT LIPTONS,—The centre of Londca) was the other night a scene of a great fire. The huge.coffee and coffee essence warehouses and factory belongings to the well- known Lipton, were found to be atisze shortly before ,sight, and within a few hours the whs)le of the top floor. ;together with much valuable maanhery, was practi- cally destroyed, while the contenta of the floors beneath ' are totally vlamaged with the torrents of water poured into the pietnises. The buildieg, which is five stories high, overtopping all the neighbourhor'd, Via 3 Vie." (11Y fitted t) cope with the lire, an shown by the fact that the flames wade little progress batow the second floor. Over one hundred firemen were engaged on the task of sub- deing the flames, and 11ct few, r than sixteen engines, three manuals, one watva-tour, four escapes, and three salvage engines were beought into requisition. Fortun- ately the burned-out premises are situated some dis- tance from Mr. Lipton's head-quarters. These latter premises are ginntrie,, a staff of no less than nine hun- dred persons tieing employed there. The tea and other departments are all carried on tile head-quarters, so that last night's calamity, ocourring in only one ot Lip- ton's secondary este blisb tatiU not affecltt general buainen 1p any degree,</p>
      </div>
