      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>Toitti•arto</head>
        <p>Toitti•arto</p>
        <p>So far as this part of the country</p>
        <p>Tii,,. Lock-our.</p>
        <p>is concerned there is but little</p>
        <p>improvement to comment upon in connection with the lock-out of miners. It seems to be taken for granted that a few of the largest coal-owners in Lancashire were leading spirits in organising the lock-out, and it certainly looks as though they will be amongst the last to again throw open their pits. These Lancashire coal-owners may be animated by unreasonable obstinacy or by heroic determination—it all de- pends upon the poitit of view—but, whatever may be the proper epithets to describe their con- duct,the result is the same—industry is hopelessly crippled, and privation and misery is felt in thousands of homes which are at other times fairly</p>
        <p>comfortable. Tho most hopeful part of the on look is the fact that the masters' combination is undoubtedly suffering from a process of slow integration. By the end of last week all of wi may perhaps be described as the weaker limbs the combination had already fallen away, with' result that something like eighty thousand mil were again at work at the old wages. This \ there have been defections from what was um stood to be the central body of coal-owners--4 the ring which, it was said, would hold out the bitter end. On Tuesday the miners emplol at the Poynton pits of Lord VERNON were formed that they could re-commence work at old rate of pay, and-on Wednesday arrangemt were made for re-starting on the same terms the large Hemsworth collieries near Bar, This last announcement is regarded by the leaL of the miners as being of special importance, as. means the first break in the solid phalanx Yorkshire mine-owners. The reasons given Lord V RANON for re-opening are significant, I of the kind of influences which are operal against the masters, and which even tho stubborn of them cannot resist for long. The opening of his pits, says Lord VERNON, or agent for him, is an act of self-defence, as his ' kets were being undermined by collieries al open. Thet is a reason which will have g, weight with some mine-owners who could sh‘ their eyes and close their ears to the cries of' tress and the commercial ruin that are the of the lock-out. There is another consiclei which tends to bring the stoppage to an end. can now hardly be denied—for coal-owners' selves have not hesitated to make the chat that one of the objects a few, at all events, of prime movers in making the fatal demand of twenty-five per cent reduction ' had in vie' was the breaking up or the weakening the Miners' Federation. It is to be feared with some of the masters this was a more tent object than the securing of a reduction wages. But, as things have turned out, even most sanguine of the coalowners can scarcely 1- te bring about such a consummation as thin Whatever now may be the final result of the flict,—whether the masters capitulate lineal' tionally or whether they secure something in nature of a compromise—the prestige of Vi will remain with the men. It is almost t. that the struggle will result in the tightening the bonds which bind the various parts of Miners Federation together, and there aro sevel reasons for believing that the area of the Fed 4 tion will be extended and mot lessened. Not has been more strikingly exemplified during sovero trials of the last twelve or thirteen' than the in telligent loyalty of the miner's to organization, and further proof is being given ' the readiness with which men who are now in wr pay the heavy levies towards the support of tht who are still out. On the other hand, we WO alincist every day evidences of how little unit' the coalowners are amongst themselves ;' and may easily be believed that the present crisis sown amongst them seeds of distrust and , sion which will not be eradicated for some y, to come. With such a prospect in front of tl may it not be expected that those coalo. whose bosti:ity has been mainly directed agail the Miners Federation will be forced to the' elusion that their" game is tip" P If it be so are surely near the e-• dof the stoppage. Ma of the class referred to, after their main obi has been frustrated, are not .likely to het unduly over a five per cent, reduction—more less.</p>
        <p>Another result, which May be set down as against the enormous losses of this disast struggle, is the fact that the, miners' wages not be so readily resorted to in future Iv' economies in production are to be ,effected. are other sources in which economies might made with a much better show of justice, royalties in particular have, in this corm( been a good deal commented on of late. are, of course, some people who say that no can be found in this direction, but it is not to understand by what inental Kocess they get that conclusion. A li.)yal Commission luta, cently reported that something like £5,000,0w year is pail in royalties to land owners, and tl are several reasons for supposing that even I sum, large as it is, is an under-estimate. A l'weeks ago, Mr. E.K. Musrasrr, in a letter cent buted to our columns, mentioned the case of to irorunaster who, while paying £.7,500 a S'eg,l wages to workmen, paid £15,000 a year in roysli to landowners. Even th is week Mr. ..1-smzs 0: BURN, one of the directors of the Bamfurlong Iliery, in a letter to the London " Daily chrorocle' mentions the fact that-bis company pays LSO° year in royalty to one landlord as a mininv- rent. He then asks the pertinent questieo "Cannot some means be devised to approach landlord and ask for a twenty-five per cent. re tion ?" Such means can a nd will be devised. '- system under which the men Who contribute the labour to coal-getting are to be mulcted their wages when trade is depressed, ad t 11( who do nothing still receive all their °nom royalties is altt:gether too. vicious to stand brunt of free criticism.</p>
        <p>WITH the exception of St. Atisl Thr. Eections. ward, there will, on Wedru next, be an'" all round " muniejl contest in Warrington. Under the conditions‘, prevail in the town at the present time, Pleb contest might, with great advantage to the ' payers generally. have been 'voided. The df which exists justow in Warrington if; bably unexampled, in the history of the berol4-1/, Owing to the stoppage in the coal trade liti3dre of workingmen have been thrown out of et01)1'-?1; rnent for weeks peat, and the fact that tbe rrimerous workmen are deprived of their cusL" cry earnings has diminished the " takiogs of shopkeepers and other tradespeople .04 a most serious extent, to say n°tbibo of. the . -many small property-owners 11 are being " hardly bit." ConsegnentlYi ever an eflort should be made to Pre' an unnecessary expense being thrown 111)°11,_ "'it. rates, surely the present is the time to.rno‘e,„rs Such an etiort hss been made-by by. the kcal of the Liberal party, but these who are. controllileld the policy of the Conservatives have not digPl3/0. any reciprocal feeling. In suggesting °- promise the Liberals were not asking the vatives to do anything which they had not the selves done before. Last year, when sir(' thre° tives retired frorn'tho Town Council and unii - for Liberals, there ra a promising opportunitYc tio Liberal gains, but the Liberals, in view 01 , smallpox epidemic that then prevailed, arlree4%' a compromise, whereby each party retained its 0.0.0, seats. From the point of view of the rater 'lee, who have to bear the expenseS of contestO teuo tions, the present . is. even a more , Imo fur 'i:set.'s:,l.% C iendiCuro than - yr"</p>
        <p>i) 4 ie 1</p>
        <p>t 1 J</p>
        <p>It- Lis dis— dist of inets week frolfl to ) in- the k elite . at irnaleY. Ides of by toor tting most re- hi mar" dready great hut dir results 3ratiou It their rge • the PA' of the Tor- of the hoPe COO' , Idl- - certain, of the gal lore' 'thing the o.'ooo their ,ork too aye Itea it bee diasen" ;ears vnere ,inat coo' ester° hject iggie or gaio itioo will ;he° 01 Thee' be srA .41 teotiooY The° f relie eael to .1 re- -9 ,here that felv all • 1 , Itie9 WO- if A tain iJ • the We' all lill Lose .flion9 • the made): th,lt h rate- tiatref3g pro- Atgli* rode *))" ,se 'tor°, tO eg 4. • 19' COtlaerVi</p>
