      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <p>November. Mr. JOHN WHITE, in his reply to Mr. IV. PIERPOINT, points out that the election ex- keetses will mean a very small addition to the rates. But if this is the position taken up by the Conservatives what becomes of the numerous protestations of a .desire for economy which are being made by their candidates. If they will not economise in small matters it may be taken for granted that no eeonomies will be effected. We do not hear that they are acquainted With any heroic methods of reducing the rates.. There is only one way of carrying out a policy of retrenchment in municipal expenditure, and that , hY rigidly diecountanancing needless expendi- ture, however small it may be. But for small ueonornies of this description the Conservatives seem to have a supreme disdain. As a matter of fact, however, the expianation of the contests is' ,t!lat the Conservative organisation at the present woe is completely under the domination of one rut, ion of the party—and that is the publican sec- Tin. contests are being fought not on behalf of the Conservatives as a whole, but in the riintereats of the liquor trade. Exclusive of Mr. 'l4l.lraan, who has gone in unopposed, and who is Maltster, there are four out of the eight Censer- ttive candidates who will go Jo the poll next .vvednesday who are directly engaged in the brow- or the retailing of intoxicating liquors. We have no desire to deny the publicans the full exer- -4_eiBe of their privileges as citizens. We admit the fullest extent, that they are just as much entitled either to vote or to be voted far at a "unieipatelection as any other townsmen. But urly one contend that those engaged in the 'or trade in Warrington form anything like one if of even the C inservative party of the town? ~.tet at this election they are in the proportion ef 1.41 out of nine of the Conservative candidates. reason for this anomalous state of things is not for to seek. The publ kens have made an or- egarlised attempt to " capture " the party machin- L7tand they have succeeded. They have manipu- r, Ted the ward and other meecings with the result teat they have had all their own way. No one teurnhl n'esire more conclusive proof of this than the beatnlent which Mr. W. P. SKELTON has received. ore a Conservative point of view, Mr.Sriumor,— tun many respects a representative trides- g nil—was an excellent candidate for Town trada ward, which is above all others the pr2sulon's ward of the town. He was rd to come out for that ward by Several rticill:lntial Conservatives, yet all his claims were 111,_ery rejected because the publicans had deter- bo'n„ to run one of their own nominees. We ovrve that on this occasion the publicans have therstepped tho murk. No one wishes to prevent Inanui having a fair and legitimate share in the to tit_lg.ernent of the town's affairs—in proportion that mabersannd_ importance—but we believe oral Conservatives as well es s re many lv! 10 have no desire to place a controlling Influence • Ass the hands of the Licensed Victuallers °elation.</p>
        <p>ONE of the incidental controversies 4 Contrast. raised by the coal crisis is that Rios relating to the earnings of miners. rov a cont relating gives ample scope for the most in 174trarlictory statemebts, and such statements are ee g rhade pretty freely. On the one hand -we tbliva figures to show that miners are in the envi- ehilerPosition of heiug able to make ten and twelve tb lr'ge a day, and on the other hand we are told 'at their earnings are far beneath a."living tlzl We suspect that there is truth in both ° "be statemera—but only part of the truth. No i,ll"t aome miners haVe, under very favourable e-eutnatances and for limited periods, been in a POSjf ..10n „ tO make what would seem tobo handsome 144? aven to well-paid artisans ; while under conditions other miners have laboured ttrra -141°11°1Y for the barest subsistence. What is :C..1.1 Wanted is information to show the earnings 'a i average miner year in and year out. A a'rt • te, ,er In the Manchester "Guardian" has en- aitu ian elaborate calculation, based upon the available statistics, in order to sup- tl lab na information. The results of his ticm°ura sertainly do not bear out the conten- i h 1 - those who argue that the miner s a ai i tLg Y paid workman. He comes to the conclusion trint in 1886 the average weekly wage of the tilnets of this country was 153. 61; in I'J it had 213 en t? 263., and in 1892 it had again fallen to Dr% Pa.rt from the few weeks of activity which aile that d Just before the lock-out, he concludes laR„„"e wkges of the miners this year were even `i`lan in 1892. There are few that will say that I tn"ntLnea a week is Ocessiqe remuneration for a hen: ,W}lo digs the black diamond out of the vicycls of the earth. :htve made Then, there are many people „.7r) are curio rs Inc e us to know what the coal-owners of the little " deal" which has dis- ated th o Point, trade of the country. Upon this loft); ")°, wn are furnished with soma (lir illation by Mr. bons 011Avntrux, one of. the 117(krs of the 13amfurlong colliery near Wigan. 4rll il:trabßlTß's isof opinion that the Wigan Coal 0r.4-ar" Company have realised at least £450,000 the sale of their stocks, at increased 14:es, while another firm had" netted,lt2o,ooo. ato:13: other firms had large accumulations of 11r4tite' and it flay be safely surmised that their 44,1° a_lso to be stated iu five figures, Thu. BOWLING AT unkarr. tteason hthernbers of the Horse Shoe inn , l;p Bowling feir closing social gathering o time was *tivit A c•int rday evening. Each member friend tree e' I° brit' hi h ~11.11,1 the to t a l an wilbee.rorraeserneltatwi saes aboutor 4 afte-`cellent kni re nada forir tea was partaken . vlb un excellent hand discoursed datr.ine "list the party indulged in. " a social .'"l4-14''t5ti1;.4._..3,411-9.ovear.n.—.l. and W.DUTTON. 8M1kC3,41t.. Wit) arni.r: CLassró roucEmEN* the object of forming n branch of the Nice Association in,Warrington, a meet.-1 tocaa•va'' held ie th„, Bold-street Wesleyan School- afternoon, whea Miss B. Dutton was 4 -"▪ Inv - • Nlissliopkinson, Bawdon, delivered an 4 Was tiltinively decided to form the oo taken charge of by bllB4 Unit°n* ftt thr-a" will be hehl every Titesnay afternoon tharacT u'eltlek, and will be of an unsectarian 4 14.44 e • COUNTY COURT CA.SE.—Tø€ JUDGE ~ TI) OBJECTED TO. d'iqbrii rtslitiv at the Warrington County Court, Judge is 'innoUr Wynne Ffoulkea. the Lon! cove 41 North-ev ißailway Co, sought to o .rrr b: ,ett, Pracnls in Earl-sc., rtiate Itriayrip, of Earle:town. Jr._ Preston, Marleatowo., from Bland. a▪ iaa cd Liverpool, instructe by Mr. Blan• IZetplol,reprebented the railway Con?lnu: !tar te'tit. L. Riley, boliciter, of St. Melee u)s. p defen.ce.. sir. Preston was opening • othle —wit his liononr interrupting Pella that a.st ,7ould kan intereat in the b.&amp; N.-W. Railway Co., it fitHea-ta necc4sary to have the consent of bnim npi -;)efore tie could try the ease.—Nir. Riley Was 411)°,,n, t,hat his Honour could not try the cas