      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <p>POOR RATES IN RUNCORN Ihnltter. At Runcorh Petty Sessions on Monday, the magis- trates sanctioned the levying of a new poor rate of 104. in the 2 for the township of Sutton, estimated to realise £302, and of is. id. in the le, for the township of Elattm, estimated to produce £313 24. id.</p>
        <p>- FAIZIAWORTH WESLEYLW On Sunday,special services were held in this chapel on behalf of the Wesleyan Foreign Mission's, when sermons were priazhed by thb Rev. H. J. Park.r returned missionary. A public Meeting was held in the school- room, on Monday evening, when he rev, gentleman gave an acnount of his labours in China. Addresses were also given by several local friends.</p>
        <p>When In want a a CHasrs and ELEGANT Wannixo or MIAMI/AY PRESENT go to PRITCHARD'S, 51, Eigh-50043L. Ad,t, RUNCORN WOMEN'S TEMPERANCE Assocwrlon. Under the auspices of this society Mr. George Allison delivered a lecturein Bethesda schoolroom, on Tuesday evening, on the subject" Children arid drink, and life in the slums." The lezture was illustrated by photographs snown by &amp; hme4ight lantern, manipulated l?y Mr. H. W. Handley, of scenes in the slums of Liverpool. The Rev. John Simpson presided.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN MUSICAL SOCIETY. The first concert of the season will be given in Run- corn Public Hall on Monday evening. _ When a capital programme of miscellaneous items will be presented. The vocalists pse Madame Fanny Moody, Miss Lily Moody, and Mr. Charles Manners, and the. instrument- lists, Mr. Frank H. Crossley, solo violin ; and Mr. F. W. Reynolds, solo pianist. The chorus will render part songs by Roberts, Pinsuti and Schumann. if you want your WATCH KIYAIRED by skilled workmen take It ID PR/TCHAAIO'S. • sidt /. •</p>
        <p>•••••- INSPECTION OP RCNCORN POLICE FORCE. Colonel Bamersley, Chief Constable of Cheshire, on Tuesday inspected the police fcrce of the Runcorn Petty I' Sessional Division at Runcorn. There were on parade IBurierintendent Atherton, live sergeants, and 29 con- ' stables. The inspecting officer carefully examined the books in the office, the clothing and appointments of the men, the courthouse and the cells, kc. Everything was found in a highly satisfactory condition. The men were put through a course of drill, the evolution being carried out with promptitude and precision. • MORE COAL STEALERS AT WIDNES. At the Widnes Police Court, on Mond ty,beforeMe.srs. G. Pilkingtnn and F. A. Stank tan-Bretherton, three men named JAI' Carmen, 84, D tvies-street; J4MAS Ash- ton, 38, Terrace-road, and Richard Johnson, 87. Bea- mont-street, were brought up on remand charged with stealing 4cwt. of coal, value .63., the property of the London and North-Western Rails% av Company, from the Widnes Dock, on .Friday last. Police Constable Evans met the prisoners at 11-30 on Friday night, in Terrace. road, carrying fcur I aza of coal. It was stated that the railway company had missed tons of c)al from the dock during the last two months. All the prisoners pleaded not guilty. The Bench considered there was a doubt in the case of Johnson, and he was dismissed. Carmen and Lshton were fined 2Ja., with the alternative of 14 da3s' imprisonment. JOSEPII PRITCHARD, 51, MO-street, Miriam°, keeps SPECTACLE.' IO suit all sights, from a abiding a pair.—Adat.</p>
        <p>WRIAN COLLTRRS SENT TO GAOL AT RUNCORN, On Monday, at Runcorn Petty Sessions, before Mr. J. , Brundrit and Dr. Steele, two colliers named Thomas Cohen and William Cohen were cl.arged with begging in Church-at. on Saturday night. There was a second charge against William of assaulting Police-constable Richardson. The men were in company with three other colliers from Wigan, and were begging in Church- st. They were asking for money for the colliers out on strike. The constable, who was on duty in plain clothes, was taking them into custody when the prisoner William Cohen Ick d him aboutthe legs, andattempted to throw him to the ground. Thomas Cohen was committed to gaol for seven days with hard labour, and William Cohen was sentence dto twenty-ohe days' imprisonment with hard labour. Do to PRITCTIARD's, 51, liigh-st., Runcorn, for the best FIVE GUINEA Macs a working man can have. —.4drt. WIDNES AND ME COAL [IMPUTE.</p>
        <p>The weakly meeting of the Wi ines Relief Committee was held at the *Town Rail on Wednesday afternoon, under the presidency of Mr. H. Wade Deacon, J.P., C.C. The catering committee had passed for payment account amounting to £2lO 9. 61. and had further decided that 1170 hotpots should be distributed to-morrow (Sunday) This is an increase of 40 hotpots. The Chairman of the Finance Committee (Mr. Councillor F. Neil) stated that last we k's expenditure showed a deciesse of RS. The total amount subscribed since the fund opened was £l4lB ss. and of this £1248 is. 4i. had been spent, leaving a balance in hand of £lBB is. Btl. At the pres- ent rate of expenditure the whole amount would be scent in three weeks. The subscriptions from the work were decreasing owing to more men being stopped for want of salt,and the amount contributed from Gossages Soap works was also considerably reduced. The Thar- sis Company would give £5 for two weeks in sd.lition to ' the amount already subscribed by them. The sacred concert given in the Alexandra Theatre on Sunday night by the Substription Prize Band; Mr. Edward Grime I and Mr. J. J. F. Ainsley, bad realised about £25.</p>
        <p>VIOLENT IRISHMEN AT RUNCORN. At Runcorn Police Court on Monday evening, before Mr. J. Brundrit, and Mr. P. Speakman, Thomas Gar- retty, Thomas Connolley, William Shaugnessay, Daniel Connolly, and Pa.trivk Featherstone, all men, who said they came from Widnes and were on theiravav to Ire- land, were charged with being quarrelsome and refus- ing to quit the Railway Hotel, when requested by P.C. Gregory and the landlord, Henry Darlington. Garretty was also charged with breaking a window and doing f'amage to the extent of 153.4 and the brothers Counolley were charged with assaulting P.C. Gregory. It ap- peared that the prisoners entered the hotel at half past , two that (Monday) afternoon, and were each served with a glass of beer. One man had also 11. meat pie. A sovereign was tendered in payment, and Garritty al- leged that he had received wrong change. A row en- ensued, and Darlington ordered them to leave, but they refused, and persistedin their refusal,when P.C. Gregory arrived on the scene. Both the Connolles struck the constable, and then one threw at him a tumbler, which fortunately missed :him. A few minutes later P.S. aVotlev and two other officers arrived and the men were arrested.—P.C.. skregdry informed the magistrates that the men acted more like madmen than christians, and Superintendent Atherton said it waa a fortunate occur- rence that the four Or five (:fficers were in the vicinity of the hotel, ha-vino been escorting prisoners ta the rail- way station, Cr probably the men would have quickly demolished the hotel. On reaching the Police Station they were" wild." They were well pruned in drink.— The prisoner arknewledged refusing to quit but denied the other chserges.—For the first offence each prisoner was fined ss. and costsss.4d. or seven days ; Garritty was ordered to pair BOs. for damaging the window, sr i. 4 days, and the brafhers Connolly were each fined lea. and cnsts 54. or 14 days, for the assualt. The money was paid in every instance. JOSEPH Parasitize, Watchmaker and Jeweller, 51, (gi- st. Runcorn —Advt. A NMFI- FAMILY SQUABBLE AT WESTON. Before Mr. Brundrit and •Dr. Steele at Runcorn Petty Sessions on Monday. Samuel Hulse, Cheshire-lane, Weston, was acaranneed for ass4ulting Ellen Brown' wife of Peter Brown, of the Ratter's Vaults, Brunswick-street Runcorn, on thellrela inst. Mrs. Brown was summoned tor being violent and quarrelsrane on the licensed pre- mises of the Village inn, Weston, and refusing to quit when requested on the same date. Mr. Linsilter appeared for Mrs. Brown. Holse acknowledged gommitting Use assault, but said it was under great provocation.—Mrs. Hulse stated that on the previous, Tuesday evening. about g. 30, the foga&amp; defendant, who was her daugh- ter, came to the bouse, and entered thobedrsom where witness's husband was i3an.; ill. Mrs. Brown commenced to use disgraceful lam:nage and threats. This was the ' fifth occasion on which she had acted in the manner named. It was neeeesairy to fetch an officer. She was sober. She was requested to leave the house, tut said ,she wanted her tsrothefalife and would have Hulse, the landlord, eel(' the present was not the first time his daughter Ellen laid I•been turned out of the house. Ple desired her to keep away. He had occupied the Village Inn for 23 years.—ilbert Tudor spoke to the assault, and said that Mrs. Brown took up a ginger beer terttle and put it as well as her umbrella. in her brother's face, who thereupon struck her, and witness hal to sepazate thein.—By Mr. Littaker It WAS a hugging match so far a 4 he could make it nut. kLaughter.)— p.c. (infield spoke to being fetched to the house. fie found matter.; all unto.. There were I r +en pleases strewn about the floor. The language if Mrs. Brown was very disgraceful. Ile hal to tj-,ct her.—Mr. Linaker said his instructions were that ' Isis client was sent for to see her father, who was very ill. She went upstairs and tra k him some fruit. No bother took place rntil her brother Samuel demanded what right she had ti be there, and told her she would hare to go out. Ulti- mately they went finwnstairs and the dispute was con- tinued. The Wither hit her two or three times on the fide of the fat'. Mrs. Brown was excited„and naturally she resented the firders to leave. 'rue unpleasantness MILE on account of her brother owing money to her which he refused to pay.—Thia side rif the argument was testified to by four witnesses —The _Bench con- sidered it a pity that family squabbles like the one in question should take place. .There was bad blond among them, end ,the present incident was the outcome. Although the male defendant had received Provocation he had no right to strike her. Ile would be fined 2s. 61. and 84. 61 casts. Ellen Brown was told that she did not behave as she ought to have •done. She ought to have obeyed the request cif her mother and others. She , woukl lave tO pay a Atte or snd 17s. 6.l.'costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER. 28: 1893</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER. 28: 1893</p>
        <p>A SCUOLARSHIP_FOR ARUNCORN THACIIER..</p>
        <p>Mr. B7C7crs-b-y, head teacher of Runcorn Wesleyan School, and teacher' of science at,Runcorn Board School science and Art Classes, has on a County Council Scholarship for science. Success OF A RUNCORN STUDENT. Mr. Thomas Cooper, who for more than two years has been with Mr. J. H. Weston, chemist, Devonshire-build- ings, Runcorn, has passed the first examination of the Pharmaceutical Society of Great Britain.</p>
        <p>ME. BANDMASTER OUTMWAITS, RUNCORN. 'Mr. Outhwaite, conductor of Runcorn Volunteer band, has been appointed bandmaster of the band at the head- quarters of the battalion. The appointment carries with it a salary. We congratulate Mr. Outhwitte upon this recognition of his ability. PRITCIARD'S have now stood the test for fiftv yeara,and quality and first class workmanship are stilt guaranteed.— Advt, BRUNSWICK BAND OF ROPE,,RUNCORN. A meeting was held in the Brunswick schoolroom, Runcorn on Monday evening. The chair wai occupied by Mr. T. Simister, and an address was delivered by Mr. A. R. Norman. An interesting progranune was gone through by the children,</p>
        <p>ACCIDENT AT SALTPORT.</p>
        <p>On Thursday morning, Edward Anderson. seaman on the Norwegian ship Bessie Arnold, lying at Salt port fell down the hold of that veasel and he was removed to the surgery of Dr. McKenzie, Runcorn, who on examination discovered that his collar bone was broken. ASABEIDGE-STREET GOSPEL MISSION, RONCORN. •</p>
        <p>The opening Band of Hope meeting for the season was held en Monday night. Mr. Daniel Bisbrown occupied the chair. An interesting programme was gone through by the young people. A song was given by Mrs. Marshall. entitled " Runcorn's Cure," which was much appreciated. MERSEY MISSION TO SEAMEN. - The.trst of a series of entertainments to be given dur- ing the winter, in connection with the above mission took place on Tuesday evening. There was a good at- tendance. A thoroughly interesting programme was contributed by the members of the Parish!, Church choir, under the coriductx.rship of Mr. James Littler, who bad made the arrangements.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY. The Widnes emnloyees and friends of the above society held a social gathering on Wednesday evening, in Messrs. Gow. Richards, and Co's Hall, Simm's Cross, which was kindly lent for the occasion. All enjoyed a good tea, after which there was an entertainment inter. mingled with songs, games, Sze. Mr. W. Cropper, of Runcorn, was in the chair. RIM. DR. PRESTON OF RUNCORN. The Rev. Dr. Preston. vicar of Holy Trinity, Runcorn, with his family, has had another bereavement in the death of a near relative, Miss Preston who has just died in her 91st year at her residence, 'Axton Bray. The deceased lady, who MLR widely known and much esteemed, was, according to Buikes Peerage, the grand- daughter of the late Hon. the fifth Lord Csrbery, and daughter of William Preston, Judge of Appeals, who Was the intimate companion of the celebrated Henry-Grattan the originator of " Grattan's Parliament," and also of i the accomplished statesman, the Earl of Charlemont.</p>
        <p>SUCCESS OF RUNCORN DOGS. On Thursday at the Border Union Coursing meeting, Mr. J. Brundrit's greyhound, 'Bright Planet divided the stakes for the Netherby Cup with Mr. A. H. Jones's, Jtss Jones, Mr. M. Fletchyr's Falconer, and Mr. J. Cokes Character (runner up for Waterloo Cup). Mr, Brunclrit's share was £lO5. On Tuesday, at the Kenhel Club Exhi- bition, at the Crystal Palace, London, Mr. T. B. Holland secured the first prirt in the novice cltss, and in the limit class fur Airedale terviers with his fine dog peppy. The dog is only nine months old, and is a splendid speci- men of the breed.- Me: A. Stan way of R-gent-st., was the breeder of the dt g.</p>
        <p>THINGS H.EAItD AND SEEN AT ItUNCORN.</p>
        <p>BY AN OCCASIMAL CORRESPONDENT.</p>
        <p>The third of the series of the Gilchrist Lectures will be delivered in Runcorn Public Hall, on Thurs- day evening next, by Professor Marshall, who will deal with tins subject. of " pedigrees." The two previous lectures have been listened to by crowded audiences and there is no reason to doubt at the capacity of the 'tall will again be severely taxed, when there is 310 interesting and instructive a topic to be explained and illuetrated. As has been stared the primary object of the lectures is to aid in the establishment of the Technical Institute, hut I do I not hear that donations are flowing any faster than has been the case and that is exceedingly slow. The sum now required is about .£1,200 and as the moneyed men of the place do not appear inclined to loosen their purse strings, could not a scheme be devised for collecting small subscriptions from the multitude? Of course a subscription list has been opened for over twelve months, but the items upon it are very few. Mr. Wiswall, the chairman of the Technical Instruction Committee, who has the project very much at heart,would lam sure, gladly acknowledge any subscriptions however small. It is the shillings that make the pounds.</p>
        <p>Dutton Workhouse will be quite gay and festive on Tuesday next. Some time ago the Rev. J. Ilanby requested permission to give the inmates a magic lantern entertainment, and the Guardians acceded to his desire. But the " treat" has now exceeded all first anticipations. Mr. S. Yarwood, of Runcorn, takes a great interest in the inmates, and he has busied himself in collecting money and , gifts in kind, and there is to be a flue spiead. The table on Tuesday next will literAly groan und-r the 1 good things. Rounds of beef, legs of mutton,fowls, sausages, and, as Sam Weller put it, the usual trim- mings, are to be provided, and it may truly be said that the. men, women, and children, will have a banquet. Afterwards Mr. Hanby will.. give his magic lantern entertainment. Mr. James-Li'tler, conductor of the Parish Churn', Choir, is also taking glee party, who will waken the echos of the thually quiet institution with a flood of harmony. Let it not be supposed that the Guardians are going to starve in the midst ot all this plenty.. Mr. Thos. Lea has been cornmissione4 to prepare a plain tea for their delectation, and to satisfy the natural curiosity of the latepayers I may mention that the Guardians will pay for it—from their pockets, and not from the rates.</p>
        <p>Here is another side to the Poor Lsw question. I was walking from Runcorn railway station on Thursday morning, when I met six men being es- corted thither by several police officers. Enquiry elicited that they were on their way, to Her Ma- jesty's mansion at Knutsford, where the will be well cared for for seven4days, for neglecting to pay their poor rates. A member of the " party " was a local tradesman. In every instance the amounts were small, and as to the cause for the bon-pay- ments, I did not ascertain. From the appearanc,. of the prisoners I should not think it .wss the coal strike. There is not the slightest doubt that had there been a justifiable cause, the overseers would have had them put on the "excused list."</p>
        <p>There is an abience of excitement with regard to the municipal elections at Widnes,a fact due in some measure, no doubt, to the fact that the political parties are holding aloof. Two councillors are re- turned unoppoPed—Messrsilodgkinson and Sb uttle- worth, and Mr. David Wriklit takes the place of Mr. F. Neil. The results of the other contests will de- monstrate to some extent tho power of the Labour party. It is not probable that the constitution Of the Council will be•mucti changed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>MARRIAGE OF • DR. CA.RILUTHERS, OF</head>
        <p>MARRIAGE OF • DR. CA.RILUTHERS, OF</p>
        <p>HALTON.</p>
        <p>On Thursday, the marriage of Dr. William Hodg- son Carruthers, of Halton House, near Runeorn,and Miss kiabd Hodgson, youngest daughter of the late Rev. Joseph Lowther Hodgson, incumbent of Weathers), hear Carlisle, was celebrated at the Parish Church, Armath w aite,Cum herla nd. The bride, w Ito was given away by her uncle, Mr. G. 11. Dixon, of Armath- waitr! Rail, looked very pretty in her dress of rich Whie faille, trimmed with lace and surmounted by a tulle veil fastened with peat I ornaments. She wore a pearl neckla-e and bangle and carried an exquisite bouquet of the choicest white flowers, the gift of the bridegroom. The bridesmaids—Miss Ada, Miss Ella, Miss Alice, and Miss Daisy Dixon—wore dresses of ivory satin and black velvet hats with white feathers. Miss D Hodgson, who acted as trainbearer, wag attired in an accordeon- pleated white silk frock,with large white hat to match. They all wore gold bangles and carried shower bouquets of scarlet geraniums, also the gifts of the briclegnsdn. Mr. Charles William &amp;Ilan Hodgson, of Harker Grange, was the best man. The marriage ceremony was per- fortmed by the Rev. W. G. C. Hodgson, of Distington Rectory, Whitehaven, alsiated by the Rev. A. G. Lottie, of Calder liridge, Carnforth,and the Rev. Lewis Morgan, rector of Ainstable. After the wedding, Mr. and Mrs. Dixon held a rt seption at Armathwaite Hall, and later in the a fternonn the bride and bridegroom left en route to Paris. The bride's travelling dress was of dark li,liotrope 'cloth, trimmed with velvet and fur, and with hat and cloak to match. It</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>WESLE'YAN CENTEX A RY AT 17 RODSHAM.</head>
        <p>WESLE'YAN CENTEX A RY AT 17 RODSHAM.</p>
        <p>-- On Wednesday the centenary of the Frodsham Wesleyan Sunday Schools was celebrated, and con- siderable interest was manifested in the event. The proceedings commenced with a procession of the ! scholars connected with the Sunday echools in the circuit. This started at two. o'clock, and paraded through the principal streets of the town, headed by the Volunteer Band. In advance walked those belonging to Trinity School, numbering 212. Then Cam, the Xingley scholars, who were RI strong,fol- lowed by 63 from Helsbyeind 170 from Fie e Crosses. ! They were attended by their officers and teachers. ' After the a^ade they returned to the chapel, where a service was held, and a sermon preached by the Rev. t. H. Kelly, ex-president ef the emiterence, t 0 A fllOW(le.i ! congregation, After the 'service the children from Ilelstw, Kinesley, aped Trinity Schools partook of team the Chapel-lane iostitution ; those from Five Crosses in their owtt schooletioni, and the friends in Trinity Seim I. The friends afterwards assembled in Trinity Chapel, where a public meeting was held, under the presidency of Mr. Richard Proud. There was a large attendance. Tlw Rev. F.F. Edmunds gave a highly interesting his- tory of the movement in Prodshem and district. Ile stated that the celel ration 'of that event carried them . back in imagination to the Frodsham of 1793. which according to a geographical dictiettary of. 1781, was port ant market town: consist lir, of one large street. It ! had formerly a castle, the seat of the icavages. The towa stood on the Weaver, on which was a Ftnne brid with a harbour for small shipping. Its werkir market was yin Wednesday, and it had two annual fairs. Haying quoted from Mr. Sister's " Chronicles of Lives and Reli- gion in Cheshire," as to the low mental and moral con- dition of the people, and the visit of John Wesley' Sn Acton Bridge and Northwich, lie showed that in 1775 Mr. Hampson pr,ached in the Market House at Fred. sham, used by Mr. Jannion for tint purpose. A few Sunda VEI after his brother-in-law preached in the open street to persons who had collected from all tact aof the ' Tetrishte witness the strange sight, and with the excep- tion of the throwine of a Sew turnips and potatoes little opposition was offered. In 1790 the Methodists fitted up a barn given them by Mr. Thomas Lewis, of Netter- ton. This place they occupied for 12 or 14 years, when a substantial chapel was erected on Windmill Hill. ; About 1792 a parish Sunday school was begun at Over- ran, at the solicitation of Madam Gastrin, the wid of ' a former vicar, with bequeathed £lOO in aid of the insti- tution Two years later the school declined, When Mr. Hayes and another friend waited upon the incumbent, the Rev. Mr. l'age, and staled their wish to cemmanee a school in the barn fitted up for divine worship. Ar*er so ne explanation that gentleman gave them the honks belonging to the Church Sundae school, and the int ere-t arlaing from Madam Gastrilfg legacy, and preached a sermon in the Parish Church to encouragesthem. Thus the first Sunday school was revived and established in ! Frodsham. and Mr. naye3 was joint superintendent. In course of time the sclutol was broken up hy parts' pre- judice, a circumstance that grieved Mr Hayes, who removed the school to the chapel, where it flourished, the children attending it numbering 200 or 300. It was interesting to note that Mr. Jannion, who with the gen- tleman na.nual was inatrnmental in t S'IO)RM-deg Method- ism in Frodelutm, was also a pioneer in the cause in Runcorn, where preaching was commenced in 1781. Having referred to the cause in Norley. Kingsley, and Helrby, Mr. F. tmunde spoke of the changes that had taken place in the chanet and school premises. The ill chapel was built in 1804, And teinained as erented until 1863. The schoolroom bore the date 1832. It had two separate ent-ancee. and a diclaion ran ' the length of the building to separate the males from the female. When the new chapel was opened in 1873,the echoc,l was taken .0 the new premises, but it was soon afterwteds deter- mined to adapt the old building for school purposes. From his ilmited experience has had found the men and women who had taken an active interest in Sunday reboots had been among the most useful and honourable of Christian workers. (App'ause.) Addresses were also delivered by the Chsirmao, Mr. P4rrirfr. n, the Rev. R. Renton and the Rev. C. IL Kelly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>WIDNES MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>WIDNES MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>TELE NOMINATIONS.</p>
        <p>Mr. 11. S. Oppenheftn, town clerk, sat at his office in the Town Hall until five o'clock on Tuesday evening for the purpose of receiving nominations for the, annual election, on Wednesday next, the first November. He was asbisted by Mr. T. Craik. The following nominations were handed in:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>FARNWORTH WARD.</head>
        <p>FARNWORTH WARD.</p>
        <p>Thomas Shuttleworth, Wilmere Lane, Parnworth, farmer, proposed by IV. S. Pritchard and seconded by John R. La and assentcd to by Thomas Boardman, mo. Catteral I, Jesse l'arr, Robert Parry, Elwin Jones, Richard Kent, William Morris and J. Hamblet. He was also propused and seconded by the Rev. J. W. Williams and William Howard, Jno. Brock and Robert Kidd, Peter Longton and C. E. Barlow.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>HILTON WARD.</head>
        <p>HILTON WARD.</p>
        <p>David Wright, 44 Warrington-road, provision dealer, : proposed by Councillor F. Neil and seconded by Council- ' lor Christie. The assenters are E. Pardox, G. Leath. wood, J.,Williams, I. Hunt, Jan. Oliver, Elizabeth Whit- field, J. Bell, J. Kelly. There were also five other nomination tapers handed in, the proposers being Elizt- both Hulme, E. M. Harrison J. Valentine, T. Minton, and P. Tallon ; and the seconders It. Campbell, T. Ire- land, W. Gerrard, E. Hardman, and A. Bennett..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>StXSI'S CILOkS WARD.</head>
        <p>StXSI'S CILOkS WARD.</p>
        <p>Alexander George Lowry, 6. Lacev-t., journeyman cooper, was nominated six times. The Zrst taper was signed by John Hughes and John White as proposer and seconder, and the following assenting burgesses S. Worttaingtcn, Arthur Lines, R. Carroll, Thos.Etheridge, E. Young, Rd. Evans, John Hollias,and J. Sankey. The proposers on the other papers were ; Robert Tonne, Walker Cathie, Wm. Crilly, Elztheth Magee, and Henry Lewis. me seconders were Edward Hardman, James Rooney, F. Eastwood, F. Easton, Ellen Lightfoot, and W. Birchall. _ _ . I •</p>
        <p>.... SAmtiel Owens, Wilmere House, Bold, accountant and estate agent, was nomicated tour times. On the first paper he was proposed by T. Parry and seconded by A. W. Southwood. The assenting burgesses were W. Wit. hams, J. Shaw, IL Webster, C. Yates, Plicebe Hughes, D. Bowe, and Eliza Francis. The other proposers were W. Farrant, F. Lawrenson, and Councillor G. Davies. The seconders were J. Houghton, A. J. Loines, and T. Roberts.</p>
        <p>VITTORI-A WARD.</p>
        <p>Michael Mannion, 38, kent-st., chemical labourer,was nominated three times. On the first paper the proposer was Patrick Horan and the seconder Thomas fißgan. The assenting burgesses were Patrick Pavne, Peter McChitty, Charles Doyle, J. MeCrack-r, N. Doyle, Pat Bevan, Pat Kelly, and W.litirke. There were two other nomination papers, the proposers being R. Curley and J. McCann ; the teconder4 J. Mahon and J. Groghar. Henry Singleton, Victoria-road, butcher, WAS nomi- nated six times. ThefirA paper was signed by Ad rman John Williamson as proposer, and Councillor Sam Quinn las seconder, and the following assenting burgeves Michael Ford, A. Eiwards, N. Harper, Rev. P. Collics.C. Edwards, P. Henry, R Tilley, and F. Ilitclfmough. The remaining papers were signed by TllO5. Drury, J.Devlin, R. Wilkinson, R. Brierley„ J. 11. Lee, Edward Mat tley, itev.John Rirnmer, Thomas Laws, A.E. Rev. Jos.Clarke, Calvert, Patrick Keaveny, and Wiiihm Gallagher as proposers and seconders. Richard Tebbutt, Cow-lane,Ditton, Draper, was nomi- nated three times. The first paper was signed by Coun- cillor Fred Neil as proposer and John Sinicock as seconder, and the following assenting burgesfea C. E. Harrison, Joseph W. Jones, J. L. Broughton, Ellen Lunt,Ann Makin, Ellen Davis and Henry Drapery. The other papers were aigned by Thomas Steele Swale, Robert Rooth, J,ltnes White and Tho:ra; Atherton as proposers and secoir!ers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>WATERLOO WARD.</head>
        <p>WATERLOO WARD.</p>
        <p>Three nominations were handed in for Councillor Thomas Carney 33, Mersey-road,grocer. Tho first paper was signed by William Miller and Charles Flynn as pro- poser and seconder and the following assenting burgesses Samuel Hill, 11. Radcliffe, J. J. Tomlinson, W. Ice, W. Byron, John Green, W. Sterns, and Patriot Connor. The remaining papers were. signed by John Wigglesworle, and Alexander Borthwick, John G nigh, and John Cam- bell, Owen, Lenahan, and Joseph Carroll as p-oposer and seconders. Frederick Hattland, 10, Oakland-st., labourer, was nominated twice. The first paper vas signed by Chia. Stead as proposer, and Thins. Harper as seconder, and the following assenting burgesses : Jas. Knowles, 0. Halewood, W. Whinirenny. 0. Roberts, J. Burgess, C. Stringer, J. E. Basnett, and Thos. TAWIe. Ties second paper was signed by Philip Rescorl as proposer, an Walter Bray as seconder.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>WEST BANK.</head>
        <p>WEST BANK.</p>
        <p>Wm. Eindgkinson. 69, Albert-rmyl, grocer, was m- onger! by Gen. Turton, and seenrd.d by Oen. Henry Stowe. The as burgesses were Henry Holton, !I. Hargreaves, P. Robinson, Thos. Boardinan,C. W. Owen, EL Atherton, .T. W. Leach and W. J. Brookes.</p>
        <p>A perusal of the above list shows that there will he contests in three of the six wards into which.the t )wn is divided. The political parties are taking no part what- ever in the present election, and each contmt is twin; caused by would-be representatives of the Labour party. Councillor Shuttleworrh, Conservative, retnine his seat for the Tory stronghold of Farnwnrrii, and Council!. r Hodgkinson, who secured his seat 12 months ago as an independent, is also indulged in a walk r Councillor F. Neil,in consequence of his intention to reside at South- port, which is outside the fifteen miles limit, does mt seek re-election for Halton Ward, and Mr. David Wrigrit Is nominated in his stead and is unnpnosecl. Mr. Wright who last November, was twice defeat was for many years a member of the old Local Board, and held the important position of chairman of the Health Commit- tee. There is only OVA labour member on the Gamcil at the present time, and the Trades Council are making strenuous efforts to secure the citadel of Simms Cross. They have put. forward their secretary,Mr. A. lir Lawry, who was nominated no fewer than six times. One paper was sigrud by coopers, anotho-r by bricklayers, and a third by plumbers, the fourth by ironfounders, th. fifth, by railway servants, and the sixth by females—a truly formidable array. Mr. Lowry lISS issued an able address, and I.e has a corps of wnrkey acting under a member of the Trades Council skilled in e'ect'oneering. Cnincillnr Owens has Hies a hard fight to retain his nest.. During the past year he has acted as chairman of the Finance Committee in a most able manner, and in the general work of the Council he has taken an Active part. Ile may with truth be slid tr, belong to the" Forward" division and it seems rather a pity that Mena ies Coun- cil have not chosen a battle ground where they could have meted a member, who was les:: in acc,rd with their maim 'alien and aims than is Councilor Owens. No ' doubt they hove chosen the ward in which their power can best be displayed. Last year Councillor Singleton attained a victory in Victoria ward as the riontim e nf the local branch of the Irish National League but a rplit appears to have arisen, and the young rouncillnr s'a !id between two flies. On the one hand is Mr. Maanion,t nominee cf the League and o' the Copper and Chemical Workers Onion, and on the other is Mr. Telfic.V sa seets electinn as an independent. The result of this three-carnered struggle will he awaited w'th inter/Ft. In Waterloo ward Councillor Carney (N) is opposed by Mr Fred Ilartland,who comes forward as an In lependent labour candida.e. Both gentlemen are well known in the ward and a keen contest is assured. The election takes place next Wednesday, and the arrangement in the three wards will he on the same lines as in N vember last. The polling lymths will be open from 8 am. 13 p.m. and the r.sult will be declared at the Town Hall prJha';)ly shortly after nine o'clock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>ItODS HAM.</head>
        <p>ItODS HAM.</p>
        <p>NEW RATES.—The magistrates on Wednes lair affixed tapir signatores to poor rates for the following t;asoiships patina ite I ti produce the sums rutia•d. N •w'o 11d. i 1 the 2. £3O 7s. ; Alvsnley. 10 1.. £lO6 9.. 7.1.•, Kingwood, 114.. £95 2e. 8d ; Minle.lli. £llO Ili. 5i.; and Frodsham Lordship, II 1., £327 Is. 104. FARMERS AND THEI R Dons—At Froderim Petty Sessions on Wedeesdev,Williain }Lindley and Ralph Bate,farmers, Frodsham, were suimnomcl fr keeping dogs without a licence on September Ist. —P.O. Davies sp 'lke to finding a cross lietwe.sti a St. Bernard and mastiff on the premises of the former defendant, aod a fox terrier on those of the latter. He hail never seen them used for driving cattle.—Defendants produced exemption orders in each case; and said the dogs were used for the purpose named —Both cases were dismissed.</p>
        <p>_ WARNING TO COAL .DRALUIIS.—At FrndShaul Petty Sessions on Wednesday, Henry Limbert, Church. at., Frodsha Wa% S,lntrwmerl for selling coal without carrying a cnrrect vreighing instrument, contrary to the hyclaW, on the 5.11 inst. A further Funmenrwar t nut against him for ffering coals for tale in bags with- out a metal label al ta,hed. Defendant rtated that he was ignorant of the requirements of the byelaw. He was fined la. and 84. Cl.costs in the first case. and the Costs in the other—Francis Watt, Newtown, Frodaham, Was summoned for selling coal wit'mut a weighing machine. He also was fined 14. and costs, B+. 61.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>BREACH OF EXPLOSIVES ACC.</head>
        <p>BREACH OF EXPLOSIVES ACC.</p>
        <p>On Wedneslay,s.t ',rods five men,gamed Richard and Reuben Winfield. Meadowcroft.flioth and Dobson, were charged with a breach-of the Explosive live-laws. Constable Thomas said that in consequence of a telegram he went at night la t'utt Rear's Paw yard, and (mind five carts, containing 203 boxes of gelatine. weizbing over rmir tons. Th 4 nearest men were over fifty yards away. The two Winfidds were &amp;deep. The men had been per- mitted to put un their carts at the Ileat's Paw by gating that they cort tined goods. Tne men were conveying the gelatine from Brotnhnrough Not to ship at Frndsha in In defence it was alleged ti at the yard gates were locked. and the piaeo enclosed, and that Dobson and 11,,ntli were within five and twenty yards of the carts of gelatine.The bench inflettd fries of 10i. anti costs.</p>
        <p>The florae Se 7reta ry has ordered tho release of Walter James Lyons, who was three years ago sentenced to Reveislwor• rt,nal aerri`ol .for the manslaughter 0' Sergeant M•jor Btev art at Ilumetead.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <p>rim RECENT" CHOLERA " SCARE. The Medical Coaled of Health has received a further communication from London with reference to the supposed case of cholera at Warrington. Additional experiments prove that the case was not oue of Jisiatic cholera. TWIGGERY BIUDGE, PADOATE. The thrashing machine which precipitated itself over Twiggery Bridge, Padgate, ashort time ago, did eo much damage to the structure that the local authorities condemned it and ordered it to be rebuilt. The bridge has now been completely taken down,and the work of re-building is being proceeded with, a large staff of men being engaged on the work. The traffic along Manchester-road, which is very great, is entirely suspended, and all convey- ances bnve now to go through the village of Pad - gate. The bridge, which was situated on the old stage coach road between Manchester and Liver- pool, was a very ancient one, and several old itiliabi- tbnts say they never remember oho same having been repaired. Mr. Ephraim Aukland, Warring- ton, has been engaged on the work during the week. UMBRELLAS RE-CIVERED.—J. and W. DUTTON, Sankey-et. Warrington .-4 t WEDDED ACROSS THE SEAS.</p>
        <p>The "Riverside Daily Press" of America has the following account of the marriage of Miss Owens, of Warburton :--" A happy wedding party asseroh. led on October 4, at the house of Mr. and Mrs. A. M. Randall of Indianapolis-avenue, Riverside Heights, the bride being Miss Emma Owens. of Warburton, Cheshire, England, the bridegroom William James Hunter, late of Canada. The Rev. Mr. Wright, of the M. E. Church, performed thccereinony. Besides the friends of the happy couple was Mrs. Margaret E. Parker, late of Scotland and Penketh, whose in- terest in the bride arose from the fact of Miss Owens coming to California at her snggestion six years ago. Numerous presents testified the respect in which the parties are held. An elegant supper, provided by Mr. and Mrs. Randall, followed the ceremony. The bride carried a large bouquet of orange blossoms and tube roses tied with white satin ribbon, and orange blossoms in her hair." Acx, iinUSRUOLD UNE++, hemmed an i marked. free of drugs —J. and W. DurreeN. Sankey-et --Ad. t. StreeitalEl OF A WARRINGTON PUPIL TEACIIFII. Mr. Herbert Natal% formerly of the Parochial school Warrington entered St. Mark's college Chelsea. London in January last. and has recently passed his matriculation examination in the first class. Mr. Nuttall came from St. Peter's infant school. at seven years of age, passed his standards most successfully at the Parochial boys' school, was then awarded a scholarship at the Warrington Grammar School, and at the expiration of three years, returned to the parochial schohla and became an excellent pupil teacher. During his appretice- ship he passed in the first class in religious and secular knowledge, as well as in drawing and several sciences. At the end of his apprenticeship he stdod nineteeth on the Qaeen's scholarship list., and was second mau at St. Mark's College. Chelsea London. The departmeot excused him taking his- tory and geography in his future examinations, hence time has been given him for special prepara- tion for the matriculation examination in which he has succeeded so well. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
