      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>WARRINGTON MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>(Continued from poye 3). ORFORD WARD.</p>
        <p>MEETING IN SUPPORT OF MR. WM. PIERPOINT'S</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>CANDIDATURE.</head>
        <p>CANDIDATURE.</p>
        <p>A largely attended and enthusiastic wetting of Mr. Wm. Pierpoint's supnorters in the Orford Ward was held ' lathe Silver-at. schoolroom on Wednesday evening. Mr. T. MOrris took the chair, and he vi as suppor.te.l by Mr. Wm. Pierpoint, Councillors Garner, Beaman, Flood, J. Shaw Green, H. Roberts, and Wilkinson, Messrs. J. W. Hayward, J.P., C. J. Holmes, Wm. Roberts, and C. Bunch. The Chairman said that be had come to recommend his friend Mr. Win. Pierpoint. Those Who knew him best liked him best. He was one of those men who, when he had formed an opinion that a question was a righteous question, he went in for that question lika an English- man should do, heart and soul. He was a thorough go- ing man for the working classes, and everything which would tell to their welfare. Ile had been in the Council Chamber before for nine yr ars,and he (Mr. Morris c mid tell them candidly that a gnat many Conservatives were heartily sorry when he was defeated in the Town flail ward. It was a downright shame that Mr. Pierpoint should be pushed out of the Corporatkn. He appealed to the electors of the Orford ward to put him into the Council that day week. He was a well tried man, and was respected by all parties. They would make a inis- takr if they did net record that little cross to Mr. Pier- poiot's name next Wednesday. (Appkuse.) Mr. Pier- point had no axe to grind, but had than welfare of the community at Ivan. He was born in tVarrin,:ton, and was a regular Warringtonian from head to foot, and he hoped that on Wednesday the record would be that Win. Pierpoiot headed the poll by something like 100 or 200 majoi ity. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>Mr, Was. PIERS:ANT, LP., who was greeted with loud applause uporrrising, said he thanked the Chairman for the maily kind expressions cf opirinin he I.a I used to- wards him that night. He had sra.k..n to them of his personal character and had acquainted them with the fact that he mai arringtori " from top to toe," havin been born in the town, and having with a trifling ex- ception lived in it for almost 50 years. (Hear, hear.) Will, he not only. came before them that night as a rate- ' raver, but also as the chairman of the Executive Com- mittee of the Liberal Associalen of Warrington. (Ap- plans%) Ile thought it was due to himself, end it was (be to them, and to the Liberal party of Warrington, that he Should give them some explanation of ceitain misieatting statements which had !sem circulated. Neither he nor other membersaif the Liberal party in the town oared one iota for criticism that was pissed upon them. as long as it was fair, hut when people went nut of their way to irake misleading statements, he thought some sett of rebutting explanation was required. (Utar, new.) It had been st ited that "Mr. Wm. Pierp.int and his patty are like a certain race who live with rhyme as possessing one fault in excess, viz. : "The fault of the Hatch-is giving too;litt le and asking too much." It was further stated in illustration of this that he (the speaker') wrote a letter to the Conservative party be- thoughts of a crntestrEd offering to let the three Conservatives be returned ui opp sed if they would allow the six Liberals to retain tsar seats. It was further sated that he (the speaker). had bewailed the had state of trade and the iniquity of spending money. This vrassentirely false. He had not written begging for a cnnpromise, and to prove this he would read them the correspondence which he had with Mr. : Jim Witita upon the subject. The first letter he sent was as follows r—"Ootober 3, John White, J.P., Chairman of the Warrington Conservative Asseetation, Heir S.r,-4t. a meeting held last night of the Warring- ton Liberal Asseziation, it was decided in the event of ' opposition being offered by the Conservative patty to the Liberal nominees in the Town Hall, Bewsey, White- . cross, Howley and Orford wards in the. forthcoming • municipal elections that arrangements should be male to con' eat every card in tie town. I Wish' to appeal to you to use your influence to prev eat a contest on two ground's. (1). The t.the 3C:ittll of the Liberal party hist year when we prevented a contest and consequently six Conservatives and three Liberals were returned 'inep- t-Ks:id j 3 worthy of imitition this year. (2). Seeing the rateplyers are unduly burdened with extra rates in con- s, quenee of the epidemic through which the town has passed it is imprrat ire that both political parties should I seriously consider whether they would he Justin:el in , provolring elections which must add further burdens. In conclusion yew' areement would not interfere with your maj rity in the Council and I believe arculd be the cominrnciment sf an era of peace in municipal matters. —Yours 5t..., W. Piraemsrr."—Now this was written in the belief that the ?teller: Suggested Was the best for the town; ter it: was considered by the Liberal party that : the money which it was proposed to spend on time elec- tion would be much better if spent in relieving the (Us- . . . . .</p>
        <p>_ tresssd. (Applause ) • This viii als° eerie with a view I to catrling out *hat had been in his mind many years ' and that wseearrying on the municipal matter: of the town wit unit introducing port'es. (Applaus7.) Well, in reply to that letter he received one from Mr. Toltri White, In which he stated that the Conaervatire .kssocia- t tonconsidered that in the case of the coinprotnise hat year the Liberal party bad been linitienced by the result of the grown! election ; whilst with respect to the expense of an rte.:Mon this year, continued the letter, he (Mr. Pierpoint) could net have rr..me far iota Calculations or he would have seen th it the expense ineurred wadi not mean one-fifth of a far: hing in the £ en ti e rateable value - of the borou.h. In re dy to tli,s tear the followirg communi• cati in Wai fOrWAnied to Mr. White: —October (3.h. join, Avid e E q., J. P. —Mar Sir;—My absence in Len- don Until late yesterilay.has prevent° I my aeknowledg- e,,iir letter sooner. when you brie g the Parliamen- tary lettinn in, as one ot the reasons why the Liberal • a tv tin k no actin the elections Novernlser 1892, ailaw me to assure you that you are lemming under a miiianpreliension. atil that our reasons for MO- fag to lit had retard to the welfare of the town. We eana:d -red that any action of the Liberal party that would have the effect of bringing about an all round N 2112, ith the atter dint merVngs and canvaasing otilv r, Stitt in a wholesale spreading of,smal-pox and be nothing less than criminal./ This was • the motive the'._ actuated us anil I tea that we only did the riehr. thing in r: specting the health of the team and re ittlitiourhond Fief. re the interests •of our party. Yiura, &amp;c , WI LLIA* riEttrOINT, ' Chairman of the Warrington Lilwral ition.— To I. t'er he Lai not since It td a reply, ant matters I lima been allowed to assume tie aspect they had now tsken. Tip re W 33 a itt her point, to which lie wished to allude. It li id been stated that lie (the speaker) and ether members of the Li heral party had endeavoured to " square" that %Nina Well, all lie had to say was if he be had cared to 't /quartz " the ward there would lime been no one mere willing than Mr. .I.k ltori. (Hear, hear ) Mr. Sk,ilton, as they kaew had first been sub- mitted t) the Conservative Committee as a cindiclitte for the Town Hall Ward. But unfortunately a numberbf the Coeservative party—he thought they wert those asencii.t ,d with the nublican interest—thought that Mr. Skelton was too milk and water kind of a candidate for them, 80 they submitted in opposition t him, Mr. Shil!cock the publican champion. IVell, he rather sympathised with Mr. SkAton, bee.ause be had not been anxious to come forward as a candidate and had only given hi 4 consent under considerable pressure. Now, lie I was not going to say a iltaing derngatary (ifMr. Skelton. Relied knawn 111. n for it great number of years, and ha! always found him straightforward and honourable. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Skelton was a business ' man, and he contended that he ought to have known i his own mind and after having expressed his intention of contesting the Town Hall ward, he ought not to have allowed himself to be " shelved "on to the o:ford war i• (Heir, hear.) H )wever, he preferred to have a man like Mr. Skelton as his epponent rather than a pubic in. (Hear, hear.) Ile (the sgeakcr) vat nat a teetta all'r,auud he had no doubt most of them knew it. But he did not think he was a drunkard. (Applause.) Ho differed from many oh his teeti t ii friends, in respect to pubScaria,for hue knew many of them conducted their houses ill a propel. manner and kept them in aceordance ith the terms of their license. (Hear, hear.) Ile had nothing to tay to publicans of that stamp; but Vivre a; ere publicat,s who had to resort for the purpose of creating business, to ways and nieaus which were injurious to the neighbour- hood in which they were sltuati d. (Hear, Liar.) How- ever, he would like