iderably • out ot town. (Ltis..r, Lear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <p>He wished for a moment to refer them to his address, which he thought fairly expressed what he meant to say to them. He had told them that he had occupied a-- seat in the town council for nine years, and during the . whole of that time he thought he was justifier: in saying- - that he did his work in a manner of which he need not be ashamed. (Eh ar, hear.) He knew that it was a matter - of regret to a good number of his Tory councillors and: aide' manic friends when he was defeated twoyears ago, and he had never heard anyone yet take exception to the work he had done in the Council. At all events wlmat he did, he did what he thought was in the best interests of the bcrou,gli, and t theifthesyatoreetpuorinioerd rihim.ehroe would endeavour to carry o was a paragraph in his address whien said that he believed healthy recreation conducive to happiness. He . was very fund of out-door pleasures. Ile had for a great portion of his, life taken a very active share in everstliin, relating to out-door amusements. Some • people did not think very much about foothan. Well, he did. He liked football because it gave workingmen. opnottunities to engage in out-door pleasures which otherwise they wculd not have. Fifteen years ago when there was no football within the town, it was. difficult for the workingman to occupy their leisure time. Ile was not going to say that the workmen were only to occupy their time in football. There were many who found pleasure in study and intellectual attainments. There were others who found pleasure only in athletic exercises. He would afford every opportunity that the student and the lover intellectuelity should have for the carrying out of his desire,because after all,educatinn was the most powerful factor ma man's progress. (Applause.) A man might aave lacked education when he was at school, hut at the technical scheols he could mak) him- self sufficiently clever to be on a level with any ordinary bmisiness man of the present day. As he had said that while he was only too wilting to afford every opportunity for out-door exercises, at the same time he would have just as great pleasure in advocating the acquirement of knowledge in any shape or form in these cia-see. He was also ofaopinion that small free lending libraries might be opened in different parts of the town, together with newsrooms. (Applause.) He thought the museum and library were excellent institutions, and were carried on in a mostefficient manner. He believed that they had comet ttle difficulty in expending all the money they •, Ilea on that instituticn. There was no more pleasant sight to him than to go into the news room of the War- - ringtort museum. He had many times found it crowded with a large number of ireelligent workingmen. Stude- ! ing the newspapers and magazines placed at their dis- posal. (Applause.) He thought, however, that to ask a workingman to come from the cenfines of the borough by the Jockey, the Cemetery, and the end of Letchford was A great tax upon his time. (Applause.) He there- forethought that small news rooms might be established, In these districts and also Bank Quay, or wherever it was desirable to tike them. There was just one little paragraph he would read out of the address of his - opponent. He was glad to see the paragraph in it. Mr. Sk:mlton rail that the coming ye-1r would be a very im- portant one financially; for Warrington. Already a scheme was prepared, involving an expenditure of £30,000 or £40,000. This clearly reintired very careful watching, and he consider" d the present time a very inopportune one for the spending of so much money, aid any part of it which could well be postponed, oueht to be." Mr. Skelton ought to have knowo, and lie da;ed say lie did know that this referrid to the contemplated alteration to the Warrington Town Hill. He believed that this scheme had been discussed in committee, and that it had been decided t) advertise for plans earl a premium awarded of £lOO. £30,000 or £40,000 to be spent in altering the Town Hall ! This was a scheme of ono ot Warrington Tories. If he went into the Coun- cil again, lie would object te any part of it being -spent in an alteration of the present Town Hall which was ample ter all purpeses. He thought the Tory party should give their reasons for msklna any alteration. The money that would be used for that scheme could bib much better used in abolishing the present insanitary areas. (War, hear.) Some of the houses in Warring- ton were a dieerace to thOSe who owned them. He could take them to a p'ace not five minutes walk from the l'olice Court, Where there were a row of privies within three seeds from the froet doers of another row of houses, and the centents of the privies were running down. (Shame.) He should like be menthol, else, the remarkable character Of the composition of the Tory candidate. They had Mr. Shillcock, a licensed vielialler, Mr. Beird, a brewer for Messrs. IValket and Sons, who stated that be represented one of the largest ratepayers in the town. Ile evidently meant Messrs. Walker and to., 53 that they were to trider-t and that the firm in question had relegated to Mr. Baird the duty of representing them in the Council, and that he was therefore justified in men- tioning the fact. (Hoar, Ite.ar, and taught T.) Then theme was Mr. Ashton' of Latchford, of whom the Tories would be glad to rid themselves, when they could get some.ne else to stand. Meer, bear.) In addition to these were Mr. liePlierd, who owned a public-house— which was always valuable property to own,and always ma he the ow ner A very respectable and desirable indivi- dual to tee Tory Patty. (Laughter.) In addition to this thy had the drink traffic further reprerentel by Mr. Slaw. In his opinion it was &amp; scandelous thing that the Tewn Cuccil should be cmvertee into a society frit. the pr,4,•ct i• al of brewers. (Hear, hear.) When the Watch. Comni t me becime composed of publicans, it was an easy Nang for the management of the police to be clis- ter.ed ; it was a simple ita-ter tomgivc policemen a hint that when they passed,sac,the "BrowriCat,"or whatever it might be, and saw a drunken man rolling therefrom, they were to -divert the:r eyes. and fait to observe hint. (Applause, and " Shame.") He was inclined to corrobera'e the materneut made by time Rev. E. T. Reed,. that the police maesgement d:d not do its duty. (Hear, hear 1 'there was another point which lie wished tl mention, an lit as in connectien with the meeting held In support of the cindidatere of Mr. Suite, in White- crops W.rd. Alderman Francomb in supporting Mr. Smith's eal•iiiletnre said that they d' d not Fant to be represented byanen who were simply possessed with i the " gilt of the gab." (Laugh e 1..) Mr. Francomb for a great number of years was engaged in the-te iching of the young, and he would be able to undarstana and weigh the value of the words. The " gift of the gab" mielit be very expressive, but to his mind, Mr. Fran- cnmb r ight have made a more choice selection of words. (Hear, hear ) Mr. Francomb also said that he saw " hundreds of honest working-risen walking the streets ereith pale faces and brsken hem ts," and that they were "4 suffering from the" gift of the gah of agitators." Mr. Frencomb was a public men and had been Mayor of the borough twice, and lie was els. Alderman, justice of the Peace, and chairman of the Conservative Club. He shettlel therefore, be careful of his word:. There were agitators and egit mters. There were agii ators in favour of temperance, aml Viers was also a large party in War- rington agitating very strongly in favour of drunkenness. He would rat her be a temperance aediator than the other. (Appniuse ),Ile took it that the agitators Mr. F ancomh referred to were the colliers ligents. He was not pre- pared tem enter into the merits of the disputiabut he con- tended that the suppert the colliers gave to their leaders showed that they were true to their trades union. (Ap- plause.) A men would he a coward if he did not stick up for the leadrra he had chosen. The colliers were not firlstimr foranv more money. They were only fighting to keep their wages the same as they were before, and lie contended that the men were not on strike, they were looked out. (Loud applause.) The Liberal party always showed taust in the peoplee he Tore piety always feared' teara,and it, save them am little aTsoirtycownolidiai.nirldnitheant wonelry, only put them Up in iwunnadtrie,iers FaThnolTteydown,witile nn the other c4C. wheremP ru :I; ni rgllimoeit'rvt) d tryw Cenneinors Flood-and Beaman who were guin niking•men. There were also two working- hpann men nut ueon time bench. One ot the Tory Justices—he would not give his name—went so .a.r as to say that the bench of maeist rates in Warring: ci had got so low and s,l common thot lie really WOUldn,t be able to sit there any lonaer. (Laughter.) That gertletrai should remem- ber the lowly orl.iri frump wldch lie sprang. (Hear, hear. lie. Mr. rierpoint, was mud or tho part he took in get- in g the two woileng-men elevated to the bench. In con- clusinn lie hoped they would honour him by electing him on Wectnesilavaand he would premise them that he would thither their intere ti to the tnst of his ability. (Loud apriause.) Mr llesien flawed the following resolution : " That Mr William Pierpoint is a fit and proper person to repre- sent the Orford war I in the Town Council, -and this, wain/ pledges itself to return him en Wednesday by a laree mejeriiy." Mr. EDWIN WHIGHT seconded the resolution and cnrne. mented very rtronglv upon the attacks which had been. made .n Mr. Vierpolat The resolution in being pia to the meeting was carried' unsnimouAr amidst applause, ' Mr. WM. Homers /VIP that he was shrry that he wase not lighting St. Ai:silted ward that raght, Flo only con- sented to be nominated to pat to shame some o€ those Liherala who would rot de liattle for the party there.. There were private reasons which compelled him to retire a' the last moment. Another time he might beat their service when he was required.</p>
        <p>- - Cuunalier FI.OOEI, Its a member of tha raving and' Sewerage Cnntni,t•se, vigorously defendtd Councillor- Itobeas in tho question a the Corporation contracts. and 'aid that he never received any contracts unless it% was the Inv e .t tender.</p>
        <p>Councillor BRAMAN who is rho only other Liberal coun- cillor on that comm tine also Funpnrted Flood's aoscrtloo, ad proposed a vote of thanks to tho Chairman.</p>
        <p>Conneillorentw GIIREN seclnded the motion and Mr. riorpoint supported, and :be motiwo was carried uttaci- mousty. The meeting anon afterwards terminated.</p>
        <p>Emile Z Is writes extremely slowly ; his pen literally clavvlß over the piper, and it is deult to believe what an enormous am ',int of work he gets throu2h in a year. The Bironem Bunt t'43-utta Ii a lir.n believer in till advant Ices of early rising. She rr actices it herself and vrt looks as ft, ius [mint at the theatre or dinner party in the evening. Coombs, the young man who was suspected cf ths murder of the girl Luke in a cave at Itathuarreated,an.l liherattsi, has v )luntrered to give evidenett at the al- , er'2E; NOTcrr.b.:: .•</p>
