      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>' EMBEZZLEMENT BY A CARTER.</p>
        <p>Win. Dumb-el-1",-s-vh-; for iWo- -ye-air-s--ti-a-s-breen employed as a carter, by the Wigan Coal and Iron Company, at their branch at Arpley Station was charged at the Warrington Police Court, on Saturday, with e:nbezzl- ing the sum of £2 104., on the previous Wednesday. Prisoner pleaded guilty. Mr. Brandreth (agent), said the prisoner's duty was to take coal out in bags of one hundredweight and to bring the money for it, or to bring back the coal. He was sent out on Wednesday with coal and should have returned with £2 10e., but he was not seen again. The prisoner had now altered his appearance considerably, and shaved his moustache off. Ile (witness) did not wish to press the charge un- duly against the prisoner, as he was aware he had been unfortunate with his wife and family. P.C. Rylatt gave evidence as to apprehending the prisoner at, his mother's hoilse. . The magistrates committed the pri- soner to gaol for one month. FINED FOR BUYING THREE PENNYWORTH OF LEAD. At the Warrington Borough Court on Monday, before Alderman Harrison, and Messrs. J. W. Hayward, and S. Wright, Win. Phillips Chorley, Buttermarket-street, was charged that on the 13th inst, being a dealer in old metals, he did, dontrary to the Prevention of Crimes Act, 1871, purchase ceittin metal, to wit, lead, of the weight of 41hs. 1 dram, which was less than 1 cwt of such me' al.—Detective Monaghan said that on the above dare, he saw a man go into Mr. Chorley's shop and sell a quantity of lead fot which he received 3d trom the manager. Witness went in and teld the manager that be had no right to buy less than one CWG of lea-1 and said he would report him. There had been a great amonnt of lead pilfered lately. The magistrates inflicted a penalty of £1 and costs, amounting to £1 13s 6a in all. SMASHING A WINDOW TO BE LOCKED CP.</p>
        <p>Before the Warrington magistrates on Monday morn- ing, Catherine Bowden, of no address, was summoned on the charge of wilfully breaking a window of the Maypole Dairy Co., Market Gate, en Sunaay morning, doing damage to the extent of £l5. Frank Tuck, manager of the Co.'s shop valued the window. P.C. Chrirnes slid that he met prisoner on Sunday morning at 5.40, and on his remarking that she was out very early, she said that she was going to smash a window in order to get locked up. He saw no more of her however. P.C. Snnith said that at 6 5 on Sunday morning, he raft prisoner in Butlertnarket-strest. She told him that she had broken a window at Market Gate in order to be locked up. Bhe</p>
        <p>took him t.) the Maypole Dairy Co.'s shop and he foand that she hai broken the window in two places with a</p>
        <p>stone which she h id in her hand. He took her to the police / station, and when charged with the offence she replied that she had no money, and she dared not go home to Runcorn, and rather than parade the streets, she broke the window.—Priinner was committed to the Quarter Sessions. ALLEGED EXTENSIVE TTIEFT air JEW2LLERY. At the WarringtOn Borough Court, on Thursday morning, Richard Willis,and Richard Lowe, of Warring- ton were charged with entering the shop of George Jackson, pawhbroker, Church-street, and stealing there- from, or receiving a quantity of jewellery value £2O on the 17th July last. Mr. Dalton, manager for Mr. .Greaves, at the Tower, Bridge-street, said the prisoner Willis offered in pledge, the day previous a signet ring. •On being questioned he said he had received it from a Mrs. Woods, Ship Yard, who requested him to pledge it. Mr. Dalton not tieing satisfied called in the police, when the prisoner said he had received it from a man at the door, who was waiting. Mr. Wilson, from Mr. Jackson's shop, Church-street, identified the ring as be- ing part of a quantity of jewellery stolen from the shop on the 17th July last. Evidence 'X as given of the appre- hension of the prisoners. Wilson said that the ring was given him to pledge by Lowe, but he didn't know the man. Lowe's defence to the magistrates was that he -picked the ring up near the Saracen's Head, Wildersnool. He met the man Willis going into Greave's shop with a _shirt, and he asked him -to pledge it. The prisoners were remanded until Saturday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>MINOR. CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR. CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>43,crtra";;;:li;f;;;Mr: Joim_Burgess (chairman), and</p>
        <p>Councillor J. H. Fleming. DRUNK AND DISORDBRLY.—Robert Robinson was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Stanley-at., that morning. He was fined ss. and costs. A Pinvkre IN TROUBLE.—Peter Griffiths, a private in the militia stationed at Orford Barracks was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Huttermarket-at., also with assaulting P.C. David Jones whilst at the police station, and I urther charged with stealing a razor, the property of Alex. ( lark.—P.C. Jones said he was in Buttermarket-st. when the prisoner came up to him in a drunken condition, and on the way to the police station he became very disorderly, and struck him on the chest on arrival at the police station. A razor was found upon him, and it was found to belong to Private A. Clark.—Prisoner was committed to gaol for fourteen days' hard labour. Tarr BY Yourrss.—John James Higham (17) and Joseph Higham (15) pleaded guilty to stealing from a clothes line, the previous afternoon a child's frock, a flannel wrap and a shirt value ss. the property of Henry Edeleten, and the elder prisoner was further charged with stealing, from Mary Ann Charnock, 18 Scott-st., a flannel shirt and cotton shirt, v,siue ss. ids) from John 4:3harnock, 8 Naylorst., on the 18th inst. one cotton shirt, fais property. The prisoner pleaded guilty to all the charge .—D.S. Monaghan said the two defendants were brothers auntie elder prisoner sold one of the stolen articles frrr fivepence at a secorid-hand clothes shop, and the other prisoner pledged the flannel wrap, and shirt for Is. 6d. Pawn tickets were fout.d for the pledging of the other stolen articles at the prisoners' home, in the name of John Jones. Each prisoner was sent to gaol for four- teen days' hard labour, and a conviction was recordcd against John James Li igham on the other two charges. WANTED ELSEWHERE.—A man who had been giving the name of Hughes in Warrington was brought before the magistrates in order that he might be handed over to the Newcastle-under-Lyrne police.--D.S. Monaghan said the prisoner was wanted by Vie Newcastle police in Staffordshire for stealing a pony and harness. From in- formation received from the p lice there, H.S. Monaghan said he went to 19 Turner-st., and apprehended the pri- soner. He found on the prisoner the marks and num- bers according to the description sent. On being charged with the theft, the man plc aced guilty to stc aling a pony. but not the harness. He was handed over to the New. Castle-under-Lyme p6lice. MONDAY.—Before Alderman Harrison (chairman),Messrs. S. Wright and J. W. Hayward. DATTNIC Axn DISORDERLY. —John Logan, Friar's-green; Wm. Wade, 34, Frederick-st. ; John Ryan, no -address ; Elizabeth Harlow, no address ;Wm. Cuuninghan, 38, James-st., were dealt witrefor this offence. ASSAULT ON TUE POLICE.--01111 Higgins, Old-road was committed to gaol for a month on the charge of being drunk and disorderly, and also with assaulting P.C. Jones 'on Saturday afternoon. P.C. Jones was mon- plimentql by the Bench for his couragro, as the prisoner was very violent, and a large number of navv:es sur- rounded the officer when he arrested the prisoner, and he was compelled to call for assistance. PARF.ACH OF THE PEACE. —Arthur Taylor, s,orford-lane, and Wm. Litherland, 88, Lytligoes-lane, were each bound over to keep the peace for six months, and ordered to pay costa, for fighting in tlorsemarket-street, on the 14th inst. ' I</p>
        <p>PROFANE LANGUAGE.—Martin Dolan, 5, Berry's-yard, was fined 54.- for using obscene and profane language in Bewsey-st., on the 14th inst. OVERCROWDING OF A 'Bus.—Thomas Drink water, an omnibus 'driver, was charged with allowitu,, his 'bus to be overcrowded on the 14th inst. P.C. Hollingsworth stated that he saw the 'bus in Corporation-st., and it contained 23 passengers in alt.—Defendant arid some witnesses becalled, alleged that there were not as many in the 'bus as the officer stated, but the magistrates ordered defendant to pay costs. OBSTRUCTION OF THE STREET. —Frank Naylor, 'bus driver, 61, Wakefield-st., was fined 53. including costs, for obstructing the free passage of Corporation-street, with his 'bus on Saturday night. HE was Amino OF BEING BEATEN.—John Hill and John Dodd, each lodging in Turner-street, were charged with stealing a purse containing 403.,0n Saturday night, from a fellow lodger named Juhn Lock. Fr,m the evi- dence, it appeared that Lock was asleep, with his trou- sers under the pillow, when he was awakened by Dodd rifling them, and Hill was standing in the room lodring on. They went out, and soon afterwards Lock followed them, and on Detective Monaghan being informed, he took the prisoners into custody. Lock was asked by Hill why he did net cry out when he saw his pockets be- ing rifled, to which he replied he was afraid of being beaten, if the two men knew he Was watching them. The prisoners were committed for threee months' hard labour. PROSECUTIONS UNDER THE WEIGHTS AND MEASURES Acr.—JarnesHallwocd, baker, Wilderspool Causeway, was summoned for having scales unjust on the 16th inst., contrary to a byelaw of the Warrington Corpora- tion.—The Town Clerk who prosecuted, said that the byelaw required that a pair of scales should he carried in the cart when engaged in trade. He con-idered the present case was one of carelessness, as when stopped by Inspector Crabtree, a pair of scales was found in the cart which w0.31d not weigh at all, as they were broken. —Mr. Arthur Browne for defence, said that these scales could not be unjust as they would not weigh at all. Mr. Hallwood was quite unaware of the scales being in the conditinn stated before the Inspector called attention to them, his assistant never having informed him concern- ing them. Defendant was fined in the costs.—Samuel Silcoch, coal dealer, 39, Cartwright-st., was charged with not having a pair of scales on his cart on the 13th inst., and also with having a number of coal bags on sale without having any cheques upon them. Defen- dant was fined in the costs.—James Wright, 345, Win- wick-road, and Alice Bennett, 33, Dallam-lane, were each charged with having unjust pairs of scales on the 16th and 17th inst., respectively. Inspector Crabtree said that the scales were in favour of the nurchasers and against the sellers, so that they were defrauding them- selves. Each defendant was fined 53. including costs. TUESDAY.—Before Mr. B. Garnett (chairman), Dr. Starkey Smith, and Mr. E: J. Guest. THE ONLY CASE.—Mary Chrimes, who said she came from Liverpool, was charged with being drunk and dis- orderly in Sankey-street the previous night. It appeared the woman came into the town with her child, penniless on Sunday night, and was admitted to the Workhouse. On Monday, she pawned her shawl, and got drunk with the money, and was found with the child crying in San- key-street. It was decided to pay her 'fare back to Liverpool out of the poor box. - -</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr. John Fairclongli (chairman), _ _ .</p>
        <p>, and Mr. John Geddes. TnEFT OF A SHEET.—Ann Bendell, a middle-aged woman, lodging at Daley's lodeng-house was charged with stealing from the lodging-house of James Daley, Scotland-road, a slfeet value 2s. Eld..his property. Mrs. Daley identified the sheet as belonging to the lodging- house, and had been on the prisoner's bed. She (witness) didn't miss it until the woman was brought into the house by D.S. Monaghan with the sheet in her possession. D. S. Monaghan deposed to following the woman from Bewsey-st., to Lyme-st.,when he stopped her and asked her what she had in her apron. She refused to show him, and he tick her to Daley's lodging-house, when Mrs. Daley identified the sheet as belonging to the lodging-house. The prisoner said she had taken the sheet to buy food, but intended ,to take it back. She was sent to gaol for fourteen days. Dsitanc AND DISORDERM—John Thomas Adshead was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Bridge-st., the previous evening. He was discharged on payment of the lock-up fee. Tnunsns.Y.—Before Alderman Sutton (chairman) Corn. _ _ . . . .</p>
        <p>cillor James Fairclough, and Majtir Ridgway. A DESERTER.—lternard McCartney was charged with being a deserter from Her Maitsty's Dublin Fusileers. P.C. Bruen said that on Sunday morning he met the defendant and stopped him. He (witness) told him he was wearing militarylclothing, and asked him to go to the Police Station. The man's shirt, socks and boots all bore the same number 4749. He was remanded to await an escort. _</p>
        <p>Mn. G. RILEY'S CASES.—The following cases of irregu- lar school attendance were dealt with : James Norman, 19, Gee-street, 2s. Cd. ; Patrick Dolly, 1, Pin Shop-yard, 2s. 6J. ; William Leyland,33, Bowcock-street, and Mary Ellen Grady, Big,,yard, boys sent to PathEr Nugent's Industrial School as they were incorrigiblr.</p>
        <p>_ FIIIDAY.—Before Alderman Harrison (cliairm,;.n), Alder-</p>
        <p>man Wright, and Dr GOrnall,</p>
        <p>MARY V. MARlA.—Maria Whitmarsh,4l, Old-road,was fined in the costs for assaulting Mary Whitmarsh, 100 Pierpoint-street, on the 21st October. It appeared Maria, had a squabble win Mary and struck her in the face. After the court was over, Maria, who got into the street first, saw Mary coming down the briclawell steps, mud the sight had an effect something like a red rag has upon a bull, for she immediately set up a shout something similar to a savage's war whoop. She was, however, soon silenced by the warning voice of the p?lice, and the two females went in different directions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>WIDNES POLICE COURT.</head>
        <p>WIDNES POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>Ittoarnay.—Before Messrs. 0. Pilkington and F. A.Staple-</p>
        <p>ton Bretherton7</p>
        <p>FINED FOR DRUNEENNESS.—James Water-st. ; James McTegue, Runcorn; Thomas Curwen, no fixed abode ; Bridget Brawn, no fixed abode ; and William Hughes, Ross-street, Ditton. A DESERTER.—Patrick Holland was charged with be- ing a deserter, from 22,id Cheshire Regiment. —P. C. Scott stated that defendant came up to him on Sunday afternoon, and said he was a deserter from the above regimegt, which was stationed at-the Curragh Camp. On examining tha " Police Gazette' he found the de- fendant ha l been wanted as a deserter since December 24th, 1892.—Holland was remanded in custody to await an escort.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>- b MONDAY.—Before Mr. J. Brundrit and Dr. Steele. MISCELLANEOIIS.—JOhIi Callery, of 17. Ellesmere-st. labourer, was fined 103. and costs 103. BJ. for using obl scene ianguage.—For using threats to Mary E. Jones, Alice Bothwell of 3, Cooper-street, was bound over to be of good behaviour for three months.—Elenry Cooke, Joseph McKenna and James McDonald were each fined for drunkenness.—Michael Moores, aged 15, and Arthur Richardson, ag( d 12. were each ordered to pay 43. ed. costs for obstructing the footpath in Church-street on Sunday evening the 15th inst. SCHOOL BOARD CASES.-17 parents were summoned by the School Board for not sending their children regularly to school, and the usual fine of 53. was imposed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>PAROCHIAL BOYS' SCHOOL,</head>
        <p>THE PAROCHIAL BOYS' SCHOOL,</p>
        <p>WA It RiNGTON.</p>
        <p>- The nev. J. Pulliblank, Diocesan Inspector of Schools, has forwarded to the Bishop of the Diocese and to the Rector of Warringt_on, the following report :—" The accustomed high tone ind excellent discipline still characterise the school, And the general character of the wurk done is remarkably satisfactory. Between groups I (standards V, VI and VII) group 11, standard IV, there is very little to choose, for according to their abilities, both do excel- lent work in all the subjects presented, as well writtm as oral. In group HI unusually geed work is done, and I most mato an exception here, and praise the teacher as well as theocholars. Group IV is very good ; sound, intelligent wait Is done in all subjects. Group V con- sists of two divisions kboth standard 1.1 both do very good scripture work and shew good beginning in catechism. Hymns are very well sung and collects very well said." Commended for excellence in Old and New Testament, prayer book catechism :—Group I, highest, Henry Bottomley, James Booth, John Fairclough, Stan- ley Taylor, Andrew Cheney, John Sco.tson, Ernest Saunders, Alfred Cook, John Lister, Frederick Nicholson, 1 Harold Wood, Percy Jarman. and Charles Sheath. Group 11, standard IV. Herbert Chapman, Charles Henry Cottam, Arthur Crutahleyi, William Hart, Gilbert Buck- ley, Harry Holt, Frank Humphries, George Hands. Percy Langley, and Evan Stockton. Group HI, Samuel Whitfield, Edward Baskerville, John Delaney, Ernest I Cook, Thomas Kenyon. John Box, Richar4 Lather, John Cirlett, and Henry Bibby. Group IV, Thomas Barlow, George Herbert Gilroy, George Chambers, RobertTrad- bury, Herbert Allen, Ernest Lunt, John Powell, %alter Potter, Louis Nicholson, and John Groovne. Group V, Walter Donbavand, John Crocker, Gerald Mooney, George Shaw, Arthur Spencer, Clifford Bird, Will'am Peers, Robert Mallet, Walter Williams, Joseph Hannan, Arnold Meld, and Henry Mortimer. The new and beautiful room in which the little ones have been recently transferred seems already to have had a beneficial effect on the children themselves. They are brighter, fuller of life, and seem more eager than they used to be in the old room. Group I does very specially good-work, it must be remembered in compar- ing this with past years that standard I no longer belongs to the Infants' School. Group II dpea very good work, the class is very large and the answering is very general. Group 111 (babies) does really a great deal more, and that more, much better than I could have expested. Repetition throughout is "Excellent."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
