      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>111 E EX 'MINI. SATtiRDAX, •OCTOBER 28, 1893.</head>
        <p>111 E EX 'MINI. SATtiRDAX, •OCTOBER 28, 1893.</p>
        <p>EMIIANITEL CHURCH SALE OF WORK.</p>
        <p>The second annual sale of work in connection with the above church, was held in the schoolroom over Arpley Station, Warrington, on Wednesday, Thursday, and Friday afternoons. On Wednesday afternoon the sale was opened by Miss Bessie Dutton. There was a very good attendance,- and among those prssent were the Rev. R. T. and MrL Reed, Rey. J. Yonge, and Rev. U. D. Mason. The proceedings were opened by the singing of a hymn, after which the Rev.l. Yonge read the 41st c. Isaiah, and the Rev. G. D. Mason engaged in prayer. The Rev. E. T. Reed in introducing Miss Dutton, said that all the persons connected with the .church from the youngest to the oldest had been working most earnestly to make the sale of work a complete success. It was quite unnecessary to introduce Miss Duttln to a Warrington audience, as they all knew the interest she had in all good work, and she was always ready to help these who were labouring for the Master. Last year's sale of work had realised double the amount they had expected, and he hoped that the present sale would be equally successful.—Miss Dutton in formally declaring the sale of work open,said that she was very much plaased with the arrangement of the sale in having so many rooms brought into use. There were many topics which they were supposed to touch upon on an occasion like that, but the topic uppermost in everybody's mind now, was a very sad one, she alluded to the distress in the town. They all wished that it could be brought to an end. She thought there should , be more in this worid of the spirit of Christian Socialism, so that they could have better relations between one class 1 and another, by trusting each other without standing on their fancied rights. But in Warrington, the needy had , no part in the dispute at present going on between the miners and coalowners,and on that account they deserved the sympathy and self-denial of everyone. She was glad to know of the satisfactory condition of the Emmanuel Church, and that the members were thoroughly in earnest in the work of God. They were doing a great amount of work with the Christian Endeavour Society, and they had also a large Sunday School. She was glad to hear that they had been successful in reducing the debt on the church. She had very great pleasure in de- claring the bazaar open.—The Bev. E T. Reed in the name of the congregation, thanked Miss Dutton for her presence that afternoon, after which the business of the sale ettnmenced. .</p>
        <p>The articles of the sale were arranged in a manner which reflected great credit upon those concerned. In the large room there were five stalls as follows : Mrs. Reed's stall under the charge of Mrs: Reed, Mrs. Henry Gandy, Mrs. Norman,- and Miss Eva Lees; congrega- tional stall under the charge of Mrs. Parsons, Miss Tetlow, Miss Evans, Miss Mason, Miss E. Parsons and Miss A. Trail ; Sunday school stall under the chargeof Mrs. A. Gandy,Mrs. John Allen, Miss Booth, Miss Heald, Miss M. Heald, Mies Lees, and the Girls sewing class; refreshment stall unclor the charge of Mrs. T. Allen. Mrs. E. Allen, Mrs. J. Allen, Mrs.....Ashbrook, Miss Jessie Booth, Miss Hughes, and Miss Lloyd ' • and flower ani fruit stall under the charge of Miss M. E. ,Hindley, Miss' Bibby, and Miss Heron. In a room adjoining was the tea room which Miss My fanwy Evans and Miss Gwenda Evans superintended. In another room were the gentle- men's Stall superintended by Messrs. J., T.,and E. Allen, W. Atherton, H. Gandy, A. Landon, John Bibby, A. Dickens, G. H. Heald, and F. Lloyd; and the children's stall over which Misses Laura Campion, Nellie Gandy, Beatrice Carriage, Sarah Ellison, Sarah Shepherd, Nina Gerrard, Annie Hardy, Nellie Blackburn, and Janet Bibby had charge. The Christian Endeavour had a room to itself and the articles on the stall consisted chiefly of Christmas cards, almanacs, protestant litera- ture, leaflets and tracts and was under the management of Miss Harding, Miss Alice Minards, Miss U. J. Bibby, Miss May Dickens, Miss M. A. Blackburn and Messrs. Norman. J. Callan, Wm. Dickens, J. Heron jun., J. Lloyd, Wallace Gandy, and E. Minards, The Committee in charge of the whole arrangements were as follows : Rev. E. T. and Mrs. Reed, Mr. and Mrs. J. Allen, Mr. and Mrs. E. Allen, Mr. and Mrs. T. Allen, Mrs. Ashbrook, Mr. Wm. Atherton, Miss Booth, Mrs. A. Gandy, Mr.and Mrs. Henry Gandy,Missos Heald, Mrs. Hesketh, Mibs M. E. Hicclley, Mrs. Landon, Miss Lees, Miss Mason, Mr. and Mrs. Norman, Mr. Page, Mrs. Parsons, and bliss Totlow.</p>
        <p>_ The sale was again opened on Wednesday afternoon. The Rev. E. T. Reed, in opening the rate of work said he was glad to say that the result of the previous day's sale was very encouraging, considering the great distress in the town. On this occasion they couldn't help missing two familiar faces, Mrs. Page and Mrs. Howard, whom they were sorry to miss. He had to ackaowledge two ortbree donations from friends interested in the wurk, including Mr. Joseph Davies, J.P., of the Old Warps, Latchford, who was invited to open the sale, but who was obliged to refuse to perform that office through in- disposition ; Mr. E. J. Guest, J.P., and Mr. Morrii. of Eccles • and also gifts from the Countess of Sefton, per Mrs. 11;le of Sefton Palk, and others living in the vicin- ity. Mr:Reed then referred to the distress in the town, and said they all felt deep symrathy with those who had been thrown out of employment through no fault of their own, and who were suffering such .great distress at the present time. It had been part of his duty to go to the Drill Hall very frequently during the last few weeks, and help to distribute food, and he met with truly piti- able cases of decent respectable people who were not re- ceiving a single penny in wages. Those who could afford, should help in these cases. The people's rents were getting into arrears, and del ts were accumulating. It ?night seem to some an inopportune time to have a sale of work, but the day was fixed before the strike came into existence at all. They felt they could not alter the date on accourt of the present distress, and were obliged to keep t ) the promises they had made.— He then declared the sale of work open.</p>
        <p>'Woods breaking away scored between the posts, Thomas kicking the goal. From this point the game was of a very fait nature, Green and Lawless broke away from the pack in fine style, but were unable t 4 break thmugh the Saints' defence, and on the call of half-time the score stood—St. Mary's, two goals; Parish Church, nil. On restarting the game was very even, but Lawless dribbled the ball down the field and scored under the posts, Barnes kicking a goal. After the drop out the Saints rallied, and forced the homesters on their line, where Bill dribbled over and grounded, but the referee failed to see it, and shortly afterwarls Belsby got over, which point was again disallowed. The visitors still pressed, and Bennett getting possession dMilied over, Thomas failing to improve. Just before the call of time, Burton crossed the line, the same player failing at the place-. Final score : St. Mary's two ,goals, two tries; Parish Church, one goal.</p>
        <p>OWEN'S COLLAR V. WARRINGTON RANGRlt..—Playei at Fallowfield on Saturday last, the visitors who were not fully represinited, kicked off, Foulkes returning in touch. Tho Rangers broke away, and almost scoreJ, M'Dougall saving in time. The visitors, however, continued to press and alter a fine piece of play J. Savage scored in good position and Warburton did the " needful " kick at goal. From the restart Foulkes made a fine run, but was stopped when dangerous. The game was very fast, each end being visited in turn, but nothing further was scored, up to half-time. Upon resuming the visitors rushed down, but again blifougall defended well, The College retaliatsd and James ran in a try, Holmes con- verting. Both sides now made strenuous efforts to gain the lead, the tall kicking by the 'College backs being more thark neutralised by the fine open Way of the visi- tors, who now adopted the four three-quarter system, shone to advantage,and after a clever passing movement along the line of tacks, Hinchliffe received, and dodged over in splendid style, the kick at goal failing. Time was soon afterwards called leaving the Rangers victors by 1 goal, 1 try ; Owen's College, 1 goal.</p>
        <p>_ - WARRINGTON RANGERS 2ND V. OWEN'S COLLEGE 2s.— These teams met at Lovely-lane, on Saturday, when the home team, showing improved form, ran out winners to the tune of 1 goal, 4 tries to nil.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN T. BURTON.—Burton are strong favourites in the Cheshire town and a good crowd gave them a hearty reception on Saturday. The general run of the play was of a scientific description, the men playing with great coolness. The half-back play of Abrams and J. Faulk- ner was the feature of the game, giving the three- quarters plenty of work, which they accomplished most satisfactorily. Two tries each were scored by J. Faulk- ner and Williams. Houghton was in his best form. W. Faulkner again went centre, being well supported by Morris and Warder on the wings, who took every avail- able chance offered. On the Burton side, Sully. Mars- den, and Ward were most prominent, the forwards being well led by Robinson and Lowe. The Runcorn score of two goals and two tries to the penalty goal for Burton is a true indication of the game.</p>
        <p>Eluvansvox v. WlDNES.—Ulverston came to Widnes on Saturday,with the best record of the clubs in the secorid division of the Lancashire championship, and in the hope of witnessing an exciting game some 3,000 persons assembled. Both teams played four three-quarters, and a place was thus found in the home team tor Toohey, a promising playe; from the" A" team, and he fully jus- tified his selection. The game in the first half was any- thing but interesting from a spectator's point of view, being for the greater portion of the time confined to the forward divisions. Widnes had the assistance of the wind, but the three quarters shoved very little com- bination, and the neat, short passes of the visitors al- most requited in the downfall of the home citadel on more than one occasion. Just before the interval Widnes were awarded a free kick,and from an easy place Drum- mond landed a goal. There was a decided improvement in the play of the Widnes team in the second half, in fact, it was hard to realise that they, were the same lot of players. They gradually wore th6ir opponents down, and 'the remainder ef the game was a continuous on- slaught on the Ulversan lines, fhe threlt quarters played a good passing game, notwithstandieg tbe high wind which prevailed, and towards the finish the visi- tors' lines had many miraculous escapes, but luck was against them,and Widnes were the winners by a penalty goal (3 points) to nothing. Brennan at full back had not much to do, but what little he did he did it well. Of the quartette of three quarters Tonhey was decidedly the best. Ire was always there when wanted, and his play was clean and well timed. Barber also played a good game, being particularly prominent on the defensive. Drummond was far and away the best half back on the field, and put in an immense amount of work. Widnes now lead the second division.</p>
        <p>BOOTLE V. WIDNES RANGERS.—PIayed at Hawthorne- road, on Saturday. The game opened on even lines, but as play progressed the Chemicals got well down,"and severely tested the home defence. At length the visitors 'fairly besieged the home quarters, and after a long Pres- sure Farmer ran in a try, which Peters converted. The second half was unproductive of scoring, and the Widnesians won a fairly good game by five points to nil.</p>
        <p>Rusiconw RECREATION-" A" TEAM- v. Havrox.—At Halton on Saturday. The Halton men played up very well at the commencement, and had the 'Runcorn men on their lines for some time. The visitors relieved. W. Gregory took a neat pass at half-way, cleverly eluded the opposing backs, and grounded the 1-all under the posts, Lightfoot kicking a goal. The home forwards now broke away, and Braerton got over, the kick at goal failing. This was all the scoring up to the interval. On turning round the . game" improved wonderfully, the Recreation team p'aying together splendidly, and W. Gregory and A. Worrall soon scored tries. Surnmerlield got the ball smartly away from the scrimmage, enabling him to score a try. The score was—Runcorn Recreation two goals, two tries, and seven minors; Halton, one try, six minors.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN VICTORIA v WALl,Asiir Fxxxv.7•Played at Runcorn on Saturday, belore a good number of specta- tors, both teams being well repreOnted. From a good dribble Coleshill carried the ball over, and, losing pos- session, Burton grounded, Johnson kicking a splendid goal. Shortly after the boatmen carried the ball close to the Vice' line, when a try was scored, a goal resulting. After the change of ends the Vies found the ferrymen plenty, of work, attacking their lines vigorously, but their defence was excellent. lielsby, Johnson and Faulkner were the most prominent players. Result : —Runcorn Victoria, one goal ; Wallasey Ferry, one goat. RUNCORN VICTORIA" A "TEAM V. WIDNES HORNETS.— Played at Widnes on Saturday on a very bid ground. The Hornets won a hard-fought game by two trierto nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>VOLUNTEER SHOOTING MATCH AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>VOLUNTEER SHOOTING MATCH AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Saturday a friendly match between ten members of the Runcorn Volunteer Rifle Club and a team of the same number of Widnes Volun,eers took place on Run- corn Heath, under Risley rules as to targets and scoring, but owing to the lateness of the season ortty two dis- tances (500 and 200 yards, seven rounds at each), were fired. The weather was much more favourable for shooting than usual, the light being fairly good, and the wind moderate from the left front. In order to get the full advantage of the light the competition was com- menced at 500 yards. The teams were well matched, and at the end of the first range the Widnes team had a slight advantage, leading by six points. At this stage Sergeant Dwerryhouse, for Widnes. put- on the magnifi- ent score of 35 points, being the highest possible, whilst his companion (Sergear.t Harvey, Runcorn) placed 33 to his credit. At the 200 yards range these two champions had matters reversed somewhat, as Harvey's score was only one short of the possible, whilst Dwerryhouse had to be csntent with 28 points, or seven below. The final result was a victory for the Runcorn teats by 15 points. The teams then adjourned to the Drill Hall, Runcorn. and partook of a bountiful supply of hot-pot, after which the presentation of a spoon to the lowest scorer in each team was pleasantly performed by Sergeants Dwerryhouse and Harvey, the two highest scorers, and gracefully acknowledged by the fortunate (?) recipients. Score : RUNCORN TEAM.</p>
        <p>........... ...---.. 503 200 oyris. yrIP. Taal. Sergeant W. H. 1/arrey 33 ... 34 ... 67 Private J. flout loon Sergeant J. A. Rushton 3O ... 27 ... 57 Colour Sergeant J. Poole . 29 ... 27 ... 56 Sergeant G. Joynson Colour-Sergeant J Mort 27 ... 26\ ... 53 Sergeant D. Edwards Sergeant W. Cooper Corporal I. (}Mena 2l ... 30 ... 51 Corporal W. Nellci l3 ... 29 ... 92</p>
