      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>RUGBY. WARRINGTON v. OLDHAM..</head>
        <p>RUGBY. WARRINGTON v. OLDHAM..</p>
        <p>With what interest the match tetweea Warrington and Oldham on Saturday was regarded may be judged from the fact, that there were 8,000 spectators to wit- ness it, who payed entrance fees, whilst more than 600 unemployed were generously admitted by the club with- out payment. It was the initial engagement of the two teams, with the cup tournament, and local enthusiasts having received some soft of justification from last week's match, repaired to the field untroubled by ap- prehensions about the performance of the home team. They will win and cheek another week" was a com- mon prophesy which was doomed to remain unfulfilled. Indeed though Warrington were without the services of some of their best players, the defeat which they sus- tained weighed heavily upon their admirers, and gave birth to much gloomy disappointment. The teams were :—Warrington: J. Boscow, back; P. Carey, T. Barnes, F. Heesoun, and C. Potts, three-quarter backs : 'James Bate and T. Lally, halfbacks; Fair. Turner, J. I Jolley, W. Nevins, %V. Berry, H. Ashton, .1. Taylor, T. Phorniley, and E. Bailey forwards. Oldham: W. L. I Pennington, back; J. Hurst, S. Lees, D. Gwynn, and I W. McCutcheon, three-quarter backs; H. Varley, and A. Lees, half backs; W. Chadwick, J.Bonser, B. Andrew, J. Sedan, J. Boardinan, E. Hughes, J. Morrell, and T. Fur- ness, torwards. Referee, Mr. J. Mills, Swinton. Neutral touch judges, Messrs. J. Robertson (Broughton Rangers% and H. Lombard (Broughton). H. Ashton for the first time this season donned the jersey again, under pressure, and captained the team during the absence of Its captain. Turner was deputed to start toward the pavilion goal, but no one was pre- pared for the sensational start. Scarcely had the tall been set in motion and Pennington replied than Varley got splendidly away from a peek, and Hurst. accepting a pass when at full speed he was auccessful in cross- ing near the corner, only a minute having elapsed since ' operations commenced. Oldham continued to attack, their backs passing ireely, and both Hurst aril McCut- cheon proved dangerous customers to deal with. Bos- cow saved well, but relief came about when James Bate ticked well, and Jolley,. Nevins and Potts, were responsible for passing half-way. Oldham would not be denied and Lees 'cleared well, and though Carey was given a chance oVdistinguishing himself he found more than a match in McCutcheon. Lally and Bate aided some fine work of the Warrington forwards, and Barnes and Heesom tried to force through, but it was no go,and the Oldham backs kicking judiciously, McCutcheon at last raced up and tackled the home custodian. The latter was penally d, and the easy place was successfully negotiated by Pennington. Although they mere six points to the bad, the ,Warringtonians did not relax in their endeavours, to gain soinething substantial. Turner twice had shots at goal from penalty marks, the last attempt striking the post. Borne capital passing by Barnes, Jolley, and Thorniley eventually saw Heesom racing over underneath the posts and being entrusted with the place, the easy goal was placed. Oldhatn again had a turn at attacking, and although Heesom, Barnes, and Berry partially checked their proeress, the opposition was eventually overcome when McCutcheon dribbled down the open, and, beating Boscow, scored between the posts, Turner making a bold bid to spoil the successful essay. Pennington was again at fault with the kick at goal. , Following this Warring- ton had hard lines in having a try disallowed scored by Lally, the result of good work by Nevins' and at half- time Oldham led by twelve points to five. On resuming Oldham again went great guns, and Andrews appeared likely to score in the first minute. The tackling was safe this time, and then Bate got away in true styie and handed over to Carey, but McCutcheon was a safe defen- der, and progress was not permitted. Thehome forwards worked well to a man, and eventually reached Oldham territory, and Nevins. Taylor and Berry were noticeable in attempts to break through. McCutcheon later on accepted a pass in true style, and bounding past Boscow scored a brilliant try, Gwynn this time kicking with no success. The game was now virtually won, and although the locals persevered, the Oldham backs were not to be overcome. The visitors' quarters were reached only to see Hurst effect another brilliant run three-pats the length of the field. Jolley had hard lines when he charged a kick down, and a miner only accrued, whilst Nevins was unlucky in one burst which should have been turned t ) good account. But these slips were placed in the shade when Carey missed a pass from James Bate, and McCutcheon raced up and kicked dead. Warring- ton continued aggressive, and the whistle sounded with no addition, the final reading--Oldham, one penalty goal aret five tries (18 points) ; Warrington, one goal (five points). Wansteerrote 1' A " OLDHAM "A."—This match was played at Oldham, on Saturday, before a very large crowd. The home team commenced te press from the comtnencement, and although Hampson and Mason by a smart bit of work transferred the game on one ecensien to neutral territory some passing started by Thomas gave Fillingham possession, but this player was finally tackled by Lawless before becoming dangerous. Holmes Buckley, and Eyres then forced Oldham back, but ;Ztheringtori was very safe at full back, and staved off Clue attack. Then Glenn had a chance of distinguishing himself, but this player's notions as a footballer were at once completely extinguished, the result being that Hurst got away nate floored by Phillips near the line. After Edlesten had cleared beautifully Thomas outwitted his opposing half back, and headed a dribble to the vlsitots' lines, but the well-meant screw was frustrated by Raybold, who punted grandly to Midfield. Oldham tried more passing, and then Edlesten was loudly ap- plauded for foiling a dangerous dribble and punting to centre. Half time arrived. Warrington restarted. and Eyres following up, secured and punted to the home quarters, a' free kick to Oldham, however, spoiling the advantige. A fine piece of play between Lawless Mason and Raybold found the latter running grandly to the line, but the chance was lost tihrough Glenn's inabilty. Warrington were very dangerous, quick play, by Hampson and Byres troubling Oldham, but even- I tually some passing amongst the visitors' three-quarters ended in Laws rushing to half way. Then Fillingham got away quickly, but Edleeten tackled him splendidly. Eavbold and Lawless relieved, and Phillips started a dribble, which carried the leather iAto the 'lonia quarters. Oldham retaliated, but Homes came away with the ball only to see Fillingham run back strongly, Lawless making a strong dash for his opponents' but nothing was scored and the match ended in a pointless draw.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON Sr. MART'S V. WARRINGTON PARISR Cnthiew.—On the ground of the Parish Church team this match was played on Saturday, Burton dropping the goal for the visitors a tow moments after the game started. The ball being again started, the Saints pressed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>LIVERPOOL JUNIOR LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>LIVERPOOL JUNIOR LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>• WEST HERBY V. RUNCORN "A."—At West Derby, on Saturday afternoon. On the ball coming out .of a scramble, Gus Webb, of the visitors, picked up in the smartest of fashions, and after a clever bit of passing crossed the line near the quarter flag. From an easy position Robinson failed to land a goal. Starting again T. Robinson got away and easily touched down. The goal kick again failed. Another clever bit of passing was now indulged in by the visitors, Robinson picking up and then passing to Hollingsworth, who passed to Nixon, and, surrounded by three or four opponents, he crossed the line, Robinson doing the finishing touch. After two or three smart exchanges,in which Robinson, Nixon, and Webb figured prominently, the last named player scored for the second time for the Runcorn team, and T. Robinson again put on the finishing touch. • The final result was Runcorn "A," two goals,two tries ; West Derby; one try.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE V. MIDLAND COUNTIES. —This match was played at Rugby on Wednesday afternoon. The ground was in grand order and the, spectators were numerous. The weather was tine, but a strong wind blew almost straight thiwn the ground. From the start the homesters, playlpg against the wind, pressed, but infringements by Nicol caused them to be several times penalised. Ben Tuke fed his backs well, aed they ran into the Cheshire's quarters, but grand tackling prevented any score. The game was divided into four quarters, and at the end of the first period nothing had been scored. On crossing over the Midlands cornmehced a vigorous attack on the Cheshire line, and ufter Saville had scored the home for- wards came away grandly, Rice obtaining an uncon- vert-d try in she corner. The Cheshire ' fervrards im- proved after, this reverse, and rushing tho ball to the other end Faulkner nearly scored,being held on the line. G. Tuke took the Cheshire pass cn the line, and ran to the visitors'.quarters, but half time saw no addition to the score. The visiting tacks had been neglected, but getting the ball now and then were repeatedly danger- ous, and Houghton dropped a splendid pal from a diffi- cult position, placing Cheshire a point ahead. After this each team strove hard to score, and just at thA close the Midlands penned their opponents. Carpenter hal a shot at goaLthe ball striking the post ; and, ruining through his menbrilliantly, Reynolds handed to Rice on the line for hiin'to score his second try. Bryne kicked a goal,and the Midlands won by a goal and a try to a dropped goal. Teams: Midlandsx-J. F. Bsyne (Moseley), back : A. Rogers (Moseley), U. P. Reynolds (Stretford), and G. H. Tuke • (Coventry), three-quarter backs; B. Tuke (Coven- try) and W. P. Nicol (Old Edwardians), half backs ; A. W. Gorton (Burton), W. Rice and G. Carpenter (Coven- try); J. Jones and E. Norbury (Worcester), B. H. Cattell Challoner (Moseley), A. E. Cooke (Leicester), and H. D. Hope (Old Edwardians),forwards. Cheshire—S.flough: ton (Runcorn), back ; F. Saville (Stockport), A. E. Fenton and P.R. Clatias (Birkenhead Park),three-quarter if backs ;S. Abrams and J.Faulk er (auncorn),lialf backs; W. Faulkner, J:Langler, an R. Reece (Runcorn), H. Ellis, F. Little, W. William S, and W. B. Smith (New Brighton), T., Morgan, (Birkenhead Wanderers), and J. Baxter (Birkenhead Park), forwards. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>ASSOCIATIQN.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATIQN.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON IT. Ow AMPLRFORDIANS.—The above fixtures were brought off on the Cricket Ground at Arpley, on Saturday afternoon, in beautiful weather. The League of the Cross Brass Band was present, and played several selections of music. There was a fair attendance. Connor, the home centre forward, was assisting his own school club, and his absence from the Warrington team handicapped them in no slight manner. However, Pierpoint, who filled the vacancy, played a satisfactnry game, and was not lacking in vigour and dash. The field was in excellent order, and at 3.30 the visitors commenced Operations. Combined play took the ball into the home territory, where Mather relieved. Norman obtained possession and passed out to Reid,who</p>
        <p>was neatly robted by Connor. Not to be denied War- rington through Phillips pressed, ard the visitors' cus- todian got a few warm handfuls. He was at length beaten by a long shot from Williamson. The visitors from the restart put in pressure, hut their final effort went wide. The game was of a pretty even character from now up to half time, both teams pressing at in- tervals. The second half opened rather sensationally. Fennelly, with a neat run and centre, enabled Norman to put on a second point. For some time after this nothing occurred of w;te, the honaesters having much the best of the game. Then Norman added another goal, after a capital display of passing on the left. Immedi- ately after Fennelly put in a grand shot from the touch line, the ball just going over. The Final result was Warrington Ore* goals, Old Amplefordians nil. Forest WRS back intis old insition as custddian, and he played capitally. Mather was in his usual form,and no further comment being necessary, and he was ably assisted by Francomb.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON ASSOCIATION RESERVE V. rADOATE.—This match was played at Padgate, and resulttd in a win for Warrington. The finish was exciting, and at ten minutes off time Padgate were leading. The Warring- ton forwards did not play their usual combined game, but th ir weakness was counterbalanced by good back play. Goals were scored by Kenyon, Dendy, and Fair- dough. Score: Warrington Reserve, three goals; Pad- gate, two goals.</p>
        <p>PENKETE VILLAGE V. ORFORD BARRACES.—OD EatUry day these teams met on the Recreation Ground, Pen- ketl , in fine weather. The score at half-time was— Penketh 4 goals; Barracks, 2 goals. The game was re- stsrted after the usual interval, and a fierce battle was anticipated. This it proved to be, but it ended in a vic- tory for Penketh by five goals to two. WARRINGTON UNITED V. PENKETH REsaavis.--This match was played at Warrington and after an interest- ing and hard fought game resulted in a draw of one goal each, Jefferies scoring for the United. RUNCORN ASSOCIATION V. ANDERTON RovEns.—After defeating Warrington United at Warrington on the 14th by four goals to one, Runcorn encountered Anderton Rovers at home on Saturday. The visitors-won the toss and chose the advantage of a fairly strong wind. Froin the kick-off the visitors had the best of the game; and pinned Runcorn for the most part in their own half. From scrimmages in front of goal two points were notched for the Rovers. Carter, however, kept his charge in goal very well. In the second half matters were reversed, although the wind had by this time dropped considerably. Douglas soon put in a long shot that drew blood. Hampson soon after tried another and was well stooped, but not to be denied he screwed in another and brought the scorers equal. Nothing further was scored, and the game ended in a draw of two goals each.</p>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES-WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>THE P.S.A. AT WYCLIFFE CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The president (Rev. J. Yongr) occupied the chair at the usual P.S.A., meeting in Wycliffe Church, on Sun- day. afternoon. Mr. Yonge read the resolution adopted at the autumnal meeting of the Congregational Union in reference to the recentstrikes and lockouts in the mining and other trades, which he considered was a proof that the churches were awakening to their duty regarding the working classes. He stat,d that the sum of £434 had been collected on behalf of the distress, a portion of which had reached Warrington. Mr. Alex Hider Porter, -If Manchester, vras the speaker for the afternoon, and he gave an excellent address in which he gave his hearers some praf.tical advice, urging. them to get the best out of life' physically, intellectually, and religiously. Mrs. H. H. Pearson, sang as a solo "Glory to thee my God this night." An instrumental selection was also given by Messrs. R. H. Person, A. Knowles, Burrows, and W. H. Payton. The meeting was also taken part in by Messrs. J. Irish and W. Travis. ST. LUKE'S CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The opening services were continued on Sunday, the pulpit in the morning being occupied by the Vicar of the parish (the Rev. E. (. E. Carleton), and in the evening by the Rector of Warrington. There wags crowded attendance in the evening, and many were unable to find sitting accommodation. The Rector selected for his text the first nine verses of the 2ad chap- ter of Haggai, and in the course of his remarks said he remembered the appearance of the houses of God when be was child. He should never forget Chester Cathedral how bare and cold it looked. That was a type of most of their cathedrals then. Now they could appreciate architecture. There were some, perhaps, who had been much the better for worshipping in a plain,cold and barn building ; but there were others who were the better for worshipping God in beautiful churches. No one could be insensible to the effect of architecture, which W 39 different tO what they saw in their own houses, and music in churches, which was different to what was heard in music balls, was purposely intended ta make them think about God, and to raise their thoughts to heaven. The'congregation worshipping in that church had indeed great reason to thank God for such a beauti- ful building. What a sad thing it would be if it were to remain empty I But, after all, the greatest glory wa§ not the architecture, but the people. He trusted they. would all use that building aright. But there was another glory. Let them look up tg the east end—why was tile east end made more beautiful than the other part ?—because it was the place to which Jesus Christ came. Here they came to Him, there He c.une to them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>LATCHFORD BAPTIST CHAPEL.</head>
        <p>LATCHFORD BAPTIST CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>At tho Baptist Chapel,Latehford, on Sunday, anniver- sary services were conducted,when the pulpit was occu- pied morning and evening 17 the Rev. F. Overend, Bacup. There was a large congregation at each service, and the collections, which were in aid of the chapel funds, amounted to £lO 103.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>ST. J9HN'S PRESBYTERIAN CHURCH. \ _ . .</head>
        <p>ST. J9HN'S PRESBYTERIAN CHURCH. \ _ . .</p>
        <p>On Sunday evening, the Rev. J. Warnock M.A. preached the first of a series'of special sermons in St. John's Presbyterian Church. His subject was "Clouds with silver linings," and he took as his text the words ",And he set his rainbow in the sky." There was a good congregation.—On Tuesday evening the opening meet- ing of the Mutual Improvement Society in connection with the church, was held In the schoolroom. The Rev. J. Warnock occupied the chair. A miscellaneous enter- tainment was gone through, which included readings, recitations and songs by Messrs. Longdin, Ashton, and Hunter. A limelight exhibition was also given of views in the Isle of Man, the lantern being manipulated by Mr. Albert Longdin.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>REMARKABLE DISCOVERY IN EGYPT.</head>
        <p>REMARKABLE DISCOVERY IN EGYPT.</p>
        <p>TFIE ORIGINAL OF CRESS DETERMINED.</p>
        <p>Chess-players, with a turn for the history of their strategic amusement, will ho interested to learn that all the hypotheses as to the origin of the game have sud- denly been "mated "by a recently extraordinary dia. covery in Egypt. It Was generally assumed until now that the ancient Indians bad invented chess ; that it was introduced from India to Persia in the sixth century and that by the Arabs, and in consequence of the Crusades, it spread from East to West. It is true the Chinese—who invented many things in times long gone by,which had afterwards to be re-invented in Europe— assert that they can trace chess in their own country to about two hundred years before our era. Now, there can certainly be no doubt that in the character of thp figures at present used, and in some of the words con- nected with the game—such as " schach " (Shah) and matt, or " mate "—an Indian, Permian, and Arabic influence is traceable. But here comes news, of the latest excavations on the pyramid field of Bakkara, which have brought to light a wall-painting, on which a high official is reptesented as playing chess with a part- ner at the time of the government of King Teta, who belonged to-the sixth dynasty.. Professor Lepsius for- merly assigned the reign of that monarch to about the year 2700 n.c. Professor Brugsch,correcting this chron- ology, puts it back to still greater autiquity—natnely,to the year 3300 n.c. So that chess would ha vu been known in the once mysterious land of Mizraim something like 5,200 years ago. It was north of the pyramid of. King Teta, or Teti, that two grave-chambers have been discovered; which ware erected for two high officials of that ruler. Their names were Kabin and Meruka, Called Mere. The grave chamber (" inastaba ").pf the for- mer consists of five rooms, built up with limestone, its walls are covered with exceedingly well-preserved has reliefs and pictures representing various scenes. The Other gratle-chamber, that of Mera. Is the most valuable. Until now, no less than 27 halls and corridors have been uncovered. Thera are beautiful grave-columns ; in the chief room there is, in a niche, a tinted statute of the departed, about 71t. high, with a sacrificial table of alabaster before it. Among the many wall paintings in this and other rooms, hunting and fish- ing scenes, a group of 4emale mourners, the three sea- sons, Me,ra and his sons holding each other by the hand, and Hera playing chess are to be seen. So grand is the impression tte grave-chamber makes upon the beholder that the Arabs engaged in the work would not call it a " mastata," but a " Ulna " (temple or church). Various valuable . sarcophagi have also been dis- covered. A structure of colossal dimensions sit- uated at the western end of the necropolis is being laid bare; the labours connected with it will probably extend over several years. Its direction is, like that of all the buildings of Old Egypt, from north to south. In Abu-Sir also Iseveral tombs have been excavated. One of the grave chambers be- longing to a certain Ptah-Chepses, who lived under the fifth dynasty, has already yielded very important re- malts, although only two rooms have as yet been opened. From inscriptions it seems that pilgrims had come there for centuries. There are so-called " lotos columns," the capitals of which imitate the lotos flowers. They are said to surpass in beauty of execution as well as in antiquity, those of Beni Masan, of Luxor, and Medamut. Three large statues which have been found are broken. The remnants of oneof them show it to have been seveu and a half feet high. Certainly, EgypE leeks like an in- exhaustible grourd for the arcbteological reconstruction of its history and of the life of its people.</p>
        <p>" Oinzniry's CocoA has. in a ren.arkable degree. those natural elements of sustenance which give the system endw- ance and hardihood, with a steady action that rtudgra it m et acceptable and reliable beverage."—Herath,</p>
      </div>
