      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>Conquers Pahl.</head>
        <p>Conquers Pahl.</p>
        <p>TL[t C AL TItAD3I; Ct IS</p>
        <p>THE HOME SECRETARY PROFFERS MILITARY ai It is said that the Mayor of St. Helens has receit letters from the Home Secretary stating that the distill armee in St. Helens district must be suppressc-cl, and if the local forces are not sufficient,assistance from Oth constabulary forces could be obtained, while milita would be at the service o: local authorities at One W.°, notice. The St. Helens police are now strengthen fifteen mounta d officers and fifty other pinked men Ir? the Liverpool city police. The chief constable I:1 issued notices nffi-ring a reward of £lOO for informat' which will 1(a1 t the conviction of the perpetrator? the explosion at the residence of Joseph Harrisoa, fireman, at Broad Oak on Friday mornin4 last. Aff4.l were quiet yesterday. HAWARDEN AND BUCKLEY COLLIERS RETURN°</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>TO WORK.</head>
        <p>TO WORK.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday, the Lane End Colliery, Buckley, the Aston Hall Colliery; and Marehey Colliery, Ilawarrlen, were re-opened, the men being permi 0" return to work at the old rate of wages. The collier( in which the Gladstone family have an int rest, einPl` about 1300 colliers. The pit I have been closed atol eight weeks. ANOTHER SOUTH YORERriIRE COLLIERY TO</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>RE-OPENED.</head>
        <p>RE-OPENED.</p>
        <p>----- - At a late hour on Wednesday night Mr. Pick,a, received inteliigence at Barnsley that arrangements been made for the resumption of work at the old rate!,' Thursday at the Hemsworth Colliery,near Ransley. colliery i* an important one, employing between son and eight hundred handy, and ad litional importaeoe attached to the decision of the employers inasmuch, this is the first secession of any note from the c6' owners' ranks in South Y.ukshire. It is believed ti? other firms in the district will follow the action of V colliery. MEETING OF THE MINERS' FEDER 4T10N.--C°' DEMNATIONOF A BISHOP. At a meeting of the LAncashire and Cheshire Mifie Federation in Manchester on Wednesday, the ge,n,e," secretary of the Miners' Federation, Mr. T. AS"'' reported that in the two counties 6,000 men were noW, work at the old rate of wa.es. He also added 111 private negotiations were going on with some Wave, of the Coalowners' Federation, outside altogether a suggestions from the Mayors, with a view to brio4l about some meeting of both sides, and he was 11013e0 that the negotiations might have a satisractory The delegates at the meeting passed a resolution strc"'" protesting against the remark made by the BiS"Pti Liverpool on the previous day, that the men in present struggle were led by " fluent agitaOrs who II nothing to lose by strikes or miqtakes." THE SPLIT AMONG THE COALOWNEBS,..% , LORD VERNON'S PITS 'lO BE REOPENEL''' _l,ll</p>
        <p>At a meeting of Lord Vernon's colliers at l'oricl near Stockport, on Tuesday. a letter was read froted lordship's agent stating that the pits would be oPene,.., Wednescliy at the old rates. The letter stated that If Vernon wa4 acting purely in self-defence, as he found markets were being undermined by collieries alreadi opened.</p>
        <p>LETTER FROM THE COALOWNER9. OFFER TO MERE TOE NIINEM. REPREEN TA'TIVES. The leading officials of the Miners' Federatian, ing Mr. Pickard, president, Mr. Wood, vice-Pres.— a and Mr. Ashton, secretary, met in Mancheste, Thursda.y,to consider a letter in which Mr.Edis,secre,!os of the Coalowners' Federation, expressed their 1,01 ness to meet the Miners' Federation to discuss the w" question at an early date without prjodice to the P°o ton of either side. Ater lona &amp;liberation, it t decided to call a meeting of the executive on Mends,' consider the letter. •</p>
        <p>The following is the letter, dated the 25th inst..c,n was addressed to the secretary of the Coalowlier?'" ation :--66 I beg to acknowledge the receipt of yours, ter oF the 21st inst. I regret you c3nsider the wei chester resolutipns add to the deadlock. Thei tl adopted with a view of removing-it. With regard ro suggestion contained in the last paragraph of yohr ef ti I stn instructed to inform you that a committee coal° cvners is prepared to meet the representativelle_it Miners' Federation to discuss, the whole question," ear out prejudice to the position of either party, at an date." LIVERPOOL TRADES UNIONS AND TUE SI. DELENS RIOTS. The following resolution has been unanimemsl9 by the Liverpool District Council of the Fedetl0pc Trades and Labour Unions :—" That this meetieg6ire presenting the federation of trades of the port ofiotee m pool, condemns in the strongest manner the actn the Mayor of tA. Helens and.. the Watch Contra" iort _</p>
        <p>Liver-1)0-'01 in drafting our police to Bt. Helens to t the minPra into submitting to tyrannical terms." COLLAPSE OF THE WELSH LOCK-OHT.--g</p>
        <p>RESUMING WORK. Work was on-Thursday morning resumed at thee rater of wages at Vauxhall Collieries, Ruabon, wileNrsi; miners are employed. The lock-out of North miners; which has now extended over three MOntilS' terday showed signs of collapse. The masters at se'l9 of the collieries have notified their intention to r</p>
        <p>the collieries on the old terms, while other c0a1..(7,6 offer to resume operation at a reduction of 15 in-rat 25 per cent. The colliers have returned to wort old rate of wages at Brynkinallt Oolliries, Chir6,l s Hall Collieries, Wrexham, Lane-end, Afitol2</p>
        <p>Marchey Collieries, in Buckley, Hawardert- anci it is estimated that only 8,000 Welsh miners 13' •</p>
        <p>main idle.</p>
        <p>THE WIGAN DISTRICT. A Wigan telegram says it is announced that WI ley Field Colliery Company in that district, ell some 600 or 700 hands, will re-open their pita at rates, and this example may be followed by a fell' early next week. Beyond this, matters remain were. Some of the coalowners, expecting a 1,r7e4 stoppage, have gone so far as to practically collieries, a step which has not previously been t" the South-west Lancashire coalfield. THE ST. HELENS ttIOTS AND THE LIVEBI</p>
        <p>POLICE. f At a special meeting of the Watch Committee v°et St. Helens Gorporation on Wednesday, presided o, t Alderman Gamble, C.H., the Mayor reported tI Tuesday, the 17th inet., the chief constable had ,r e "„PIO to him a serious disturbance at Ashton's Green which he (the Mayor) thought it right to reP,"ea Home Secretary, from whom a telegram had, ceived in reply stating that order must lys and, if possible, by means of the police ; that the jos hances having been renewed both just outside allit:lsr the borough on the two following days he ftbe,, e% had considered it his duty to arrange for soul, ;es P police. Having seen the Liverpool police authof 00 sonally he had been able to obtain the service 0 ter five of that police force, fift.en being mounted* I)ntioll submitted. It was resolved that the Mayor's ac approved and confirmed.</p>
      </div>
