        <p>1 THE DISTURBANCES AT IIAYDOCK.</p>
        <p>COLLIERS BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>Quite a little crowd of colliers were to be seen standing near the Occasional Court in Winwick-st., Warrington.on Monday morning. They were inter- ested in the proceedings, against a number of Hay- cock colliers, alleged to have been connected with the disturbances at Haydock ou - Saturday night. ' The prisoners were !belched through the town from I Bank Quay station, and were guarded by some .40 members of the constaeulary. Crowds of. people I ollowe I them down to the court, and crowded about the court house during the hearing of the I charges. Mr. Z.Armitage was the presiding magis- trate, and he was accompanied by Mr. John White. Seven of the prisoners were charged with doing wilful ' damage to the amount of Bs. 3d. t) windows in the dwelling-house of Aaron Litherland, of Church-road, Haydock, and assaulting P.C.'s Appleby and Rushton ota the 21st inst. They were Frank Barrett, Peter Mitchell, Patrick O'Donnell, John Keneally, John Caufield, Joseph Lynch, and Thomas Flynn. Mr. A. Browne, solicitor, '-appeared for the prisoners. Aaron Litherland was the first witness called, and he said that on Saturday night about 11-30 he and his family were in bed when they heard a crash as of falling glass. He (witness) went out to the police station and informed the sergeant of what had taken place. He estimated the damage at Bs. 3,1. He didn't see who did the damage. e P.C. Appleby said about 11'10 he was in company with another constable when they heard a smashing of glass at complainant's house. They proceeded there • and saw that a crowd of persons had assembled. The people were very disor- derly and were throwing stones. He saw Barrett throw a stone at the window of the last witness. rite police endeavoured to take Barrett into custody, but owing to his violence, and that of the crowd he gut away. lie was however, subsequently apprehended by another con- stable. 'Witness, who assistad to arrest the other pris- oners, was assaulted. Mr. Browne • ,Did you see each, and everyone of them throw etonee ?-:‘Witeedss :I would not say they were all throwing stones. They were all mixed up together.—Mr. Browne : Some of these men ten me they were in bed at the time. You aid not apprehend them there and then ?—Witness ;No ; only. Barrett and Keneally.—ln answer to further questions witness said he did not know the prisoners before, and could not say he saw any of the other prisoners throwing P.C. Rushton said he was in company with the last witness, and they attempted to take Baratt into .cus- tody for stone throwing, losst they were , bustled about by the crowd, and were unaale to do so. He (witness) was knocked to the ground.—rP.C. Atkinson dep oseeto being struck on the leg by one of the stmes which were thrown. The police applied for a remand. Mr. Browne contended that there was no case against these men. With the ex- ception of Barrett and Kenealle, there vi as no evidence against the prisoners. Two panes of glass were broken, aud this could only have been done by two or three per- sons. The ma gist rat es retired, and on their return, re- mended Barrett and Keneally to Newton Petty Sessions, and dismissed the charge against the other. prisoners. All the men were egaiu charged at the same time for assaulting r.c.'s Appleton and Rushton whilst in the execution of their duty. P.C. Appleby repeated his evi- dence as to attempting to arrest Barrett, when he he was used very roughly. He could ideutify Barrett as assaulting him.—P.C. Rushton said he was pulled away from arresting Barrett and O'Donnell. He (witness) was hit in the mouth by Barrett arid O'Donnell. He could also speak of Mitchell as having assoulted them.—P.C. Dickeason deposed to seeing P.C. Appleby assaulted.— P.C. Lightow lers said he was struck in the face by Lynch. fie could identify the prisoners as haying taken part in the disturbance with the exceptios of Catieeld. Three of the men were arrested in a bedroom,partly undressed. I —P.C. Ferguson deposed to seeing Cautield throw two stones. lie was hit on the chest and back. All the prisoners were remanded on this charge to Newton , Petty Sessions, which will be held on Stiturday.—Mr. Browne applied for bail, remarking that nothing was ' known against the character of the men previous ti the disturbance on the night in question.—lnspector Ryder I objected, and said that all the pi is• aers were single men. He did not think that they would appear •in answer to the charge at the Sessions if they were admitted to bail. —Sergeatt Bage also raised a similar objection. After some consultation. the Bench decided to remand Bar- rett, Mitchell, O'Donnell, and Keneally, in custody. Canfield, Lynch, and Flynn were admitted to bad, them- selves in £lO each, and two sureties each in the same amount. ALLEGED INTIMIDATION : APPLICATION FOR WARRANTS. ThOnlaS Waters, employed as a sinker at one of Messrs. Richard Evans and Co.'s collieries, applied for a warrant against three men. He said that on Saturday night one of them came to his house and asked trim to go outside. He declined to do 80, when one of the men took up a pick-axe and broke the door in with it. He had eeen a sinker in the pit for about twelve months. He was afraid of his life. The other men came into the house, but did no actual damage. One of the awn threatened to kill him, and he sent for the police. He supposed they svero annoyed because he was getting a living for him- self and family and they,. were not. Mr. Edward Verge said he wes secretory 'for Messrs. Richard Evans and Co., Ltd. The cornmercial manaecr had told him that it would be well for hip to go to Warrington to im- press upon the magistrate the necessity of something tieing done to prevent iutimiditiflfl. It might seem rather a stringent measure to apply for a warrant, but these were not ordinary circumstances. They had had men\ working down the Wood pit from the commence- ment of the strike, driving • a new fan. They were con- tract men, and worked for a contractor, whom the firm paid. They had nothing to do With the men themselves. There was a riot last week at the Wood pit. VW com- pany did not want to attempt to justify" knobstickirsg" or coal-getting, although they might do so. There were no" keoboCcks "down the pit at all, but simply fire- men and other people keeping the roads open from sheer necessity ; and by doing so, the firm thought they were doing a kindness to the men. If they were to yield to intimidation, and allow their pits to be cl-sed; in some eases six or eight weeks would elapse befero the men would be able to get employment. Intimidation at Hay- dock was becoming fearful they could not get firemen to work for them, and they had to threaten to discharge them altogether if they could not work. Tl..e Chair- man There is no coal-getting, I suppose ? Mr. George : Except to keep the engines going.—A warrant Was issued against the three men.</p>
