      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>Goats TEMPLARS ANNIVERSARY.</head>
        <p>Goats TEMPLARS ANNIVERSARY.</p>
        <p>The 21st anniversary .of tb6 institution of the Hope of Earlestown Lodge of Goo I Tempters was Celebrated on Saturday evening, when a public tea meeting was held in the Co-operative Hall, about 80 persons being present. After tea a public meet- ing was held in the lodge room, presided over by Bro. G. Clarke, L.D. After the ueual opening, the chairman gave an appropriate address, in which he reviewed the•work of the lodge during the period of .its existence. Since its institution it had never omitted a single meeting from weakness. it Lai held its usual weekly session, and had taken part in a large ' number of united missions. They had initiated over 700 members, and at the present time there were over 50 good members teonging to the lodge. This was a small numb( r in comparison with the number which had been adiniti,d, a number had gone astray through tile influ- ence of temptation with -"Medi they had to contend, but it was a pit astir° to /tote that a considerable number were still abstainers, and very many, active workers, and even leaders of other temperance orgaitisai inns. The lodge was now in a very harmonious and interesting state ; the members were united, anti there was every prospect of a successful future. (Applause.) Bro. It. Ambrose, E S., proposed " That this meeting, while acknowiedging with gia!itude the great value ct the important question of liquor traffic legislation in the gracious speech from the throne, and heartily thanking the Government for the introduction of the liquor traffic local control bill on. February li7th, expresses its very grein regret that the Government feel themselves com- pelled to defer the bill until another year, and this meet- ing is profoundly anxious that the Govt rnment should deal with the question at. the earliest stage in the session of MI." tiro. J. Taylor secvnded and Bro. E. Boreand,ed Liver- p nI, supported in an able address. An excellent pro- gramme ot sMigs was submitted in a very s.tisfactory 'manner by Miss Needham. Miss Smith, Bro. R. Ambrose, and Bro. W. J. Dale, recitations by Bro. A. Norzie, G. B. Warburton, Sister E. Traverse, and Miss It. War barten, and Bro. J. Taylor gave a Lancashir3 reading. Mislt W. Willis tfficientty accompanied on the American organ. The celebrations we're continued, on Monday evening, vidum the ledge was favoured with a visit from the Alpha lodge, Leigh. The visitors arrived in a wagonette at 7.30. There was a good atteadanc,., and the loom was well filled. Bro. Darlington prt sided, and tie Mho; nffices were filled by the visitors. An excel:mit progratnme of vocal and but .umental music was given r It) tile viiors, aid rendered in a very creditable man- ner. At the close a vote of thanks %VHS d to the,..,mi the ine,tion of Bro. Chu ke, seconded by Bro. G. Warburton an I supported by Bro. Q Se:gt. Roberts, of Wallington.</p>
        <p>Mr, De Comas, a member of the British Columbian LegislAture, i credi, ed by with makieg the longest speech on rec ird. In .rder to d feat a measure to which he mots o; p ,sed, title gentleman talked tor 26 hcuis at one stretcm Ex-Bailie Walls, of Glascose. who las just died, fe.und his fortune in a sewer. Years ago he bad a sperm oil refinery. Um waste products cf which ran int.., a di air. On init, occ tslon when the drain was opene d it was discovered that the chemical action I etst een the sub• stances which ran into the drain had produced an arti- fical Rpermact ti for which there was a pro at den and, So the worthy. Bailie speedily grew Hal from that which #1 teen aforetime rejected as ueeless.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <p>WORRY AND ALCOHOL.</p>
        <p>Under normal conditions nature makes over exertion of the brain impossible. When the limits of the natural capacity of the brain have been exceeded, it declines to undertake further exertion, unless the warning of nature 'is overpowered by some stronger influence. This over- powering influence often appears in the farm of worry. The maa whose mind is occupied with the all-absorbing motives of ambition or of fear, is carrivd beyond the natural limits of mental labour, and ceases to fat the weariness which acts as a signal for rest in ordinary cases. Ile has to make larger and larger demands upon the store of nervous energy which he requires fvr the purposes of recuperation until at leng h his brain is literally "used up." Worry, again, has an injurious effect by p:eventing.the methodical treatment of work. Oder and arrangement are of primary impoit ince in mental exertions, but the man who is worried cannot confine his thoughts to the business befcre him. His mind is constantly distracted by the anxious thoughts which have banged his worry, and his labour is increased both by the intense strain upon his mind and by the extra time which he has to take for the transaction of business. Here, again, he is obliged to encroach upon his reserve of nervous energy, and his brain will in time break down. The exertion of his mind has ceased to be natural, and he may continue to overtax his energies until he has gone beyond the possibility of recovery. When I named the influence of atcodol among the causes of nerve and brain exhaustion, I do not re°er to cases of excessive drinking, which are due merely to habits of self gratification and indulgence. I am dealing with the man of business who uses alcohol ass a stimulus to mental exertion. We have seen that worry exhausts the brain by overpowering the sense of fatigue. Tlif use of alcohol may produce a similar effect by deadening the same feeling. Under its influence a man is tempted to disregard the warning voice of nature and to expend in excessive exertion the reserve forces which are re- quired for recuperation. Stimulants never increase the natural capacity of Vie brain. They ran only abstract forlthe purposes of work in hand some of the energies which are sorely needed to repair and tc rest ire a brain which has already.been t ixed to the furthest limit which is consistent with health. To remove the sense of fatigue caused, by overwork by the consuinption of alcohol is to close one's ears to the voice of nature. The weariness of the brain is a protest ' against further exertion nail recuperation hiss been obtained by rest ; and if the weary feeling is deadened or destroyed by adventitious means niture will exact her pena:ty. Exhaustion of the brain is most dangerous when'it cannot be perceived ; and the man who deliberately rouses his jaded faculties t) excessive exertion by alcoholic excitement makes a cal upon his reserve energies which he often finds it difficult to repay. When the overworked man of business, hav- ing been on his logs all day add feeling fit t)drop, with a sensation of " all-goneness" about the region of the stomach, rouses himself witn whatever he is in the habit of taking, be it whisky-, champagne, or even tea or coffee lie does not add one atom of force to his stock of energy, although he ralcies he does, but having put td sleep his sense of weariness, simply appropriates some of his .reseree for the present necessity. He has accepted a bill at short date to which a ruinous rate of iaterest is attached, and his resources avid not allow him to tucks nsany repetitions of the experiment. His account at the bank of life will soon be overdrawn. Alcohol cannot add one iota to his reserve of nervous energy, but it may delude bins into exhaubt'ng it. The buSy man should once for all rid himself of this fancy, that he can credo by artificial means an abnormal store of brain power. " Opposuit nature." He cannot enlarge the limits which nature has set up.—" Health Troubles of City Life," by George Herschel!, M.D., London (Swan •Sonuensobein and Co.)</p>
        <p>THE _EXAMINER, Arlll3l?flik Y. OCTOBER, 28, 103.</p>
      </div>
