        <p>KID CREAM FOR LADIES' BOOTS.</p>
        <p>HARNE3S COMPOSITION. (TIIE CELEBRATED HORSE SHOE BRAND)</p>
        <p>FURNITURE POLISH &amp;c., ALL OF TUB BEST QUALITY.</p>
        <p>17. HANOVRR-ST., LIVERPOOL.</p>
        <p>STOTEIERTS' lIRADACHE &amp; NEURALGIA PO'A DES.</p>
        <p>A certain cure lu 10 minutes. From all Chemists. Grocers, and Co-operati,e Stores, or direct from the Proprietors. Post Free, 14 or 33 stamps. In Boxes le. 10., and 2s. 9,1.</p>
        <p>R. STOTIIERT AND SONS, WHOLESALE CHEMISTS, ATHERTON. NEAR MANCHESTER.</p>
        <p>TIIREE PREPARATIONS of Real and Attested Value : HM. ASIITON'S TIC AND NEURALGIA • MIXTURE, in Is. bottles, gives instantaneous r eller. ELM. ASHTON'S TONIC VEGETABLE LIVER 1 • PILLS, 70. and is. boxes contain no injurious in nerals. BOWEL COMPLAINT MIXTURE. in is. bottles, used in all forms of Cholert. These are to be obtained only of the Proprietor, SINTCRY- STREET PHARMACY, 63, Sankey-street (opposite Legh-street), Warrington.</p>
        <p>tetters to tbe Obttor.</p>
        <p>- -</p>
        <p>THE EMPLOYERS' LIABILITY BILL.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l have been a reader of your valuable paper for many years, and was sorry to find in one of your articles that you were in favour of the new Employers' Liability Act. I enclose a balance sheet f0r1.892 and a copy of our rules, and I trust you will spare a few minutes for their perusal. I may tell you that we havo about 60,000 members in this Society and that 90 per cent. are in favour of a clause being inserted to allow them to keel) their present arrangement with the Com- pany—that is the London and North-Western Railway Company. I have been a member since its formation in 1871 and can safely say in the district that I represent on the Committee of Management that I have had only two cases that would or could have eaimed under the Liability Act since it became law, and in both cases £lOO was paid without any litigaticn whatever, no lAwyer's fees and no court expenses; and if it is a husband that is maimed or killed, and he has sons old enough to work, they are taken in the service to support the widow. On the other hand if there is no exemption and a person meets with an accident and sues for damages what is his position.worth with the Company if he wants to return to duty ? And if be has sons in the service what is their position worth, and don't you think other Companies would look at it-in the same light if he applied for work? Our present arrangement is a ceitainty but under the Act it would be quite different only filling the pockets of lawyers and emptying our own in the majority of cases. —Yours very truly, INSURANCE. October 24th, 1893.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>DRINK TRAFFIC.</head>
        <p>THE DRINK TRAFFIC.</p>
        <p>Sir,—! have read with some interest the letters of Mr. R. Storey on the " Drink Traffic," which from time to time appear in your impression, and I should be much obliged if you would allow me a little of your space on the matter.</p>
        <p>Mr. Storey has got an idea that if a man brews, sells, or drinks alcoholic liquors, he is nurally unfit for any public duty whatsoever it be. Any reasonable man admits that one, or even two, glasses of beer do a man no harm. If a man takes over a reasonable quantity, either he is a glutton, or he has a depraved appetite. I cannot see why a brewer who supplies a demand, should be " hung, drawn, and quartered " by a certain section of the community. The chief crime argued against the brewer is that he is rich, his trade is profitable, therefore he must be hampered in every possible way. I have heard that there are many total abstainers employed in breweries ; supposing the trade forced its principles down the throats of its workmen in the earns way the various Temperance Federations force their theories, what a cry would be raised ! It is very sad to see fools drown themselves in drink ti‘ the ruin of their wives, and families, they know what is in store for them if they indulge beyond a reasonable limit. A man cannot be expected to eaution people against bu 3 ing his goods, especially when they are being forewarned by others. Depend upon it, excessive drink- ing will cover be put down by reviling the publican. It is a misuse of time. Nothing will be gained until the people are educated,or until either a more tasty beverage is brought out or the strength of the present Iquors is weakened,and plenty of entertainments (not too "goody- goody ") are provided. If Mr. Storey feels it worth his while to answer this letter,' should feel much obliged.— I am, yours truly,</p>
        <p>NEUTER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>Warrington, Oct. 23, 1893.</head>
        <p>Warrington, Oct. 23, 1893.</p>
        <p>TIIE COAL CRISIS AND COAL SWINDLE.</p>
        <p>Sir,,--flow much longer the patient endurance of a long-suffering public may last,remains to be seen. Here we have miners and masters who—to subserve their own interests—have thrown the trade of the district into a state of paralysis, and yet we have the men coining round begging from their victims with the most unblush- ing effrontery.</p>
        <p>I say that -While every man may cease work himself, ihe has no right to prevent others from doing the work he refuses. This is being done, and it makes the present situation one of siege, and the men enemies of their country. Now we pay heavy Imperial taxes for protec- tion from our enemies, be they internal or external. These men have been breaking the laws of the land and interfering with the liberty of the subject and should have been sternly repressed. I think Mr. S. Woods. M.P., to be very reprehensible in n,t telling the miners that they were in the wrong to prevent by word or deed ether men from doing the work they had refused,instead of saying the coal owners would be responsible for what happened if they allowed it. Why should the masters be held responsible for doing what is perfectly right, legal, and just ? Then on top of this we have the proposed coal syndic- ate, a gigantic swindle. By this means the consuming public are to be mulcted by artificial prices to stili further inflate mining profits. John Bull will be a greater ass than I think him if he permits this. It may perhaps occur to him that as the coal is his, he had bater take it over for his own benefit and permit no one to withhold or control the supply of an article almost as necessary as the air we breathe. It is clearly not for the " greatest good of the greatest number " that any combination of men should assume the privilege of Providence and cause a famine in the land. Did but Demos know his own strength,he would profit by this dire extremity and make his voice heard in Parliament to the sure prevention of the recurrence of a like misfortune.—Truly your,</p>
        <p>FEEns.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>" The Haws," Penketh. October 22, 1893.</head>
        <p>" The Haws," Penketh. October 22, 1893.</p>
        <p>THE COAL CRISIS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln the INl;ta-C-11-ester-'-' Guardian" of Friday, 20th inst., there appeared a letter from " a coal owner," in which, among other things' he gives a tabulated state- ment of the wages paid to his colliers during the seven months ending with July of this year, with the number of days worked in each month, and the average wage all round, and which I Was agreeably surprised to learn. was 7s. 7id. per day per man, for the seven months. In Baturday's " Guardian," " Colliery propriet)r " writes as follows : " Colliers in our pit, good and bad, earn on an average Bs. 8-id. per day per man ; trammers who are labourers to the colliers ss. 7d. per day per man." Der.aby Main average for June, 4 weeks 7s. fid. per day. At Lord Vernon's collieries at Poynton, the average per man for Mar, June and July, would be about ss. 91. per day. I-had reaCl of " starvation wages," and that colliers could only earn las. to El per week. There is a wide discrepancy between the statements, which is the nearest to correct ? " Coal owner " shews that he has men who could by a id. touch 12s. a day. Suppose we fisy of the halfpenny as Mr. Mantilini said, and call it 128., and what one man can do another may. In the face of statements such as these, is not this struggle a huge mistake? There are thousands of men out of work since this strike began, of whom no notice is taken. They are idle because their works are stopped for want of coal ; many of them are suffering privation and want—in fact starving in silence. They have no tin boxes or big drum, hand-cart or lorry to collect money and provisions with. The poor collier gets the sympathy and we g A the suffering; not a fair exchange, we think. And when we recollect that most of us—l refer to the aggregate of the unemployed outside the ranks of the miners—have not been getting more than hail the celliers' wage,and, except in Warrington, where the unemployed are liberally treated, I know of no other place where any organization exists to give the non- collier a bite of bread or a mouthful of soup. We are passive and wreak no vengeance en anyone, use no roburite, wreck no houses or furniture, throw no stones, lire no buildings or colliery property, or plan and carry out any act 4 of incendiarism and only regret that our citizenship should be stained with such acts as we have read of lately—the outcome of tyranny,cruelty,and ignor- anee.—l am, sir, yours respectfully, CYMRO.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>"HOME RULE AND TFIE PROTESTANT MINORITY."</head>
        <p>"HOME RULE AND TFIE PROTESTANT MINORITY."</p>
        <p>Sir,—lt is being continually asserted by Conservative and Unionist writers and speakers, that if Ilonie Rule is cor.ceded to the Irish people, they will oppress the Prot( stant minority resident in Ireland. As this is a que,tion much debated at the present time I trust you will, with your usual courtesy,allow me a little space in your valuable paper to disprove this as- sertion. First, let us consider how Catholic States in Europe treat their Protestant subjects—and here let it be observed that religious liberty guarantees to-every man the right to worship God according to the dicta' C 3 of his conscience, without thereby incurring any civil penalties or disabilities whatever. Now, there is not one Catholic Government of Europe which persecutes for conscience, sake; on the other hand,there is scarcely one Protestant Government which does not persecute in one 1 form or other. Belgium,Austria, Bohemia,and Hungary are Catholic, and they allow equal political rights to Protestants with Catholics. France is Catholic,and France not only does not persecute,but she protects the Protest ant religion,and pays its ministers even more than she allows to the Catholic clergy—which is but equitable, as the former have their wives and families to support! Italy is Catholic, and Protestants have places of worship in the Eternal City itself. So far is toleration carried,that but a few years since, a clergyman of the Established Church of England deli- vered lectures in Rome against Popery, and was an- swered by Cardinal, then Dr. Wiseman. Poland, poor,- 'hie( ding, and crushed Poland, was Catholic to its core, and Poland was seldom if ever sullied with persecution. Ireland was ever Catholic, and Ireland never persecuted, though she had it in her power to do so three different times. Finally, it was the Catholic Lord Balthnora and the Catholic colonists of Maryland, who in 1648 first proclaimed as a ett cd law the great principle of univer- sal toleration,while the Puritans were persecuting brother Protestants in New England,and the Episcopalians were doing the same thing in Virginia—see Bancroft' s " His- tory of the United States," Vol. L Maryland. It has been remarked that the children of martyrs are never persecutors ; and if ever a country suffered for principle that country is Ireland. Our Protestant breth- ren have nothing to fear from Irish Catholics, who ever have held that all should be equal, irrespective of creed. —I am, yours, arc., Warrington, 20th October, 1893.</p>
        <p>J. C. DALTON.</p>
        <p>TUE COAL STRIKE.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Since the beginning of the colliers' lock out, it has been my lot to listen to a great deal of argument on the subject of the causes and effects of the mis-called strike. It is not a strike, it is a lock-out. Some people are painfully ignorant of the real position they occupy in the social scale. I know a jobbing builder in a neigh- bouring town, who, like one of his neighbours, a fish dealer, considers himself a capitalist, and thinks he has a right to defend the cause of the coalowners. Master Jerrybuilt spends most of his time in carrying out al- terations in public house property, owned by two parti- cular Tory brewers, and that has a tendency to making him of the colour of true blue. Master Cestermonevr's religious belief also holds him tight to the blue along with the fact that ho can buy three t arrels of apples and a kit of fish, which makes him reckon himself a capita- list. Now, my two champions made a bold attack on me for having the presumption of advocating the cause of the colliers. They said that if it were not for Samuel Woods, Glover, Pickard, and others, the colliers would be at work ; and that Samuel Woods, M.P., Mr.Pickard, M.P., Mr. Glover, J.P., are paicPai,itators, who reap the benefit of the lock out.</p>
        <p>These colliers' leaders, said Jerrybuilt, are well paid, and naturally wish the strike may never end ; the colliers ought to be working,and not inflict privation and misery on people belonging to other trades,through compulsory idleness. Mr. Costermonger chimed in with the remark that if he had his way lie would put Glover, Wooris,and ' Pickard in a bathing van, take them into deep water, and drown them, to end all the strife. I had great difficulty in getting a hearing, as Mr. Costermonger was speaking as if he had been shouting herrings "three a penny," and he took a threatening attitude when I told him so. At last I got my word in edgeways, and this is what I said : "Mr. Samuel Woods is a gentleman of great ability, a good organiser, a true friend to the working men, he is paid to represent and to work for. Ile devotes all his time, all his energy, and, I may say his life to the colliers' cause, and the masters kaow it so ' wall that when Richard Evans and Co.'s managing direc- tors wanted to have an interview with a deputation of the colliers,they expressed a wish that Mr. Woods should not be with the men at the interview. But the men have ! such confidence iti Mr. Woods that they refused to meet the directors of Me,srs. Evans's unless Mr. Woods was also present. But the colliers are united, and their leaders are true men. Now, I inay tell you, Mr. Costermonger, and you, Mr. Jerrybuilt, for your own edification and for anybody's edification, that ever since the commencement of the lock out not one official of the colliers' Federation has received a penny piece frem the funds or any other source ; that they are giving* their time and their labours free—entirely free—and that Mr. Samuel Woods is and has been giving £lO per week to the lock out funds out of his own pocket. There is Men another word to he said, ani..lifs is it : Whenever there is to be a reduction in the expenses of coal or iron, or railway companys it is always the work- ingmen who are to be docked, and never a word i 3 said about reducing the salaries of chairmen, directors, or engineers. Now, Mr. Jerrybuilt and you, Mr. Coster- monger, let me show you that you are really thorough bred nodes, short sighted, ignorant, thick-headed, and good for nothing as bueiness men, or else you would know at all events on which side your bread is buttered. Listen to rue while I try to open your eyes to your own true interest as far as the £s. d. is concerned. Shop- keepers and farmers, as also all retail business men of whatever calling are tied hand and fist to the workers in in our coal pits and in our factories, ironworks, chemi- cal works, soap works, or any other industry. Take the colliers in the first instance. The royalties paid to landowners, as also the charges for wayleaves, amounts when added to the interest on capital, salaries to direct- ors, and high officials, to a sum equal to five to one com- pound with the collier's wages. Most of that money is spent abroad on the continent, in Venice, in Rome, Monte Carlo, Nice, Paris—everywhere in fact except in England. The colliers must spend their money at home and therefore, you have each a share of it, whereas you get nothing of the money spent abroad by the wealthy Masses. Should the colliers' wages be reduced by 25 per cent. the takings of publicans, grocers, butch- ers, tailors, cloggers, shoemakers, watchmakers, furni- ture dealers, and every other class of shopkeepers, will be reduced by 25 per cent., and that is a fact which admits of no contradiction, whereas the 25 per cent. added to the profits and salaries of the wealthy classes which will only enable them to squander more English money on the foreigners abroad, arid not one penny of it shall you get. 'rake my advice and help the colliers all sou can for your own sweet sake." I have left Mr. Jerrybuilt and Mr. (lostermonger, and on my way home bought the Liverpool " Courier" df October the 16h, 1893, in which I find a copy of a letter written to the " Times" by coal owners, the purport of which runs thus :—" There is something behind the strike left unnoticed. If the colliers beat the masters they will force Scotland, Durham and Northumberland ta unite the Federation,and by amalgamating with other trades unions possess the voting power of the country, return labour members, and the time is not far distant when they may be able to form a Labour Ministry." Seemingly the colliers' adversaries can see very much farther than my friends "Jerry " and " Coster." They dread that theworking classes should unite and conquer their oppressors, and therefore they, the wealthy, try to break the back of the colliers' Union to pre went the dread- ful event of England for Englishmen—England governed by a Labour ministry instead of being ruled by fossil- lized old Whigs and more antiquated old Tories—the people of England ruling England's destinies, instead of trusting the same to the descendants of Norman conquer- ors in the shape of the House of Lords. While I am writing these words, some- one has pushed a tract under our door and nay little girl has just handed it to me. It is entitled the Coal war and Local lodustries," and signed by a nom de plume " Judex," but might more appropriately have been signed **Judas." In this later I " Judas " is trying to show that the colliers are really going to bring about evil times for England and especially for Warrington and abjures his readers to withhold any help from the colliers, as if the colliers should win, all your trade will go to Belgium and Germany. Master Judas, I have seen and heard ' such trash before, but it is very mean of you t) try and stop workingmen from helping the colliers with such base arguments. In vain will you try to persuade John Bull that he must come down to the level of Continental wages and modes of living. England, my dear Judas, leads the way and does not intend to retract one step she has taken forwaid. England, my dear Judas, is the people, the fustian cutters, the file cutters, the leather tanner and weaver, the puddiers, the colliers—these are England, my dear Judas, all that motley crowd in mole- skin trousers, with black faces, these are England, and don't you forget it. Take them away and !hat is there left—squires, parsons, lawyers, agitators, bankers, brokers, ship-owners, landed proprietors—all those would be lost and starved but for the people. And the people have been governed up to date by the privileged ones without having really a word in it at all,except now and then,as in 1832 and at the repeal of the Corn Laws,avlien I the people got " riled," and the " upper ten" thought it wise to give way for a while. But really the people have been kept too busy at the plough, the anvil, the loom, the furnace, and the steam hammer, to have any time left for thinking. They left that part of the busi- ness to the parson, the squire, the banker, the lord of the manor, and the company promoters. Now, the people will insist on working less and doing their own thinking at leisure after their daily labour is ended. In- stead of John Bull being put back to the low level of the continental shares, John, like a good big brother, and a stout-hearted fellow that he is, will hold the hand of brotherhood to his Continental brother and bring him up to his own level. If the workers of England can get the eight hours' day legalised, and maintain a decent day's wages at that, in concert with Continental leaders the leaders of England will help the workers of Europe to obtain the same terms from their capitalists—the Federa- tion of Workers will be universal. The colliers of Eng-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>Tim EXAMINER, SATURD AV. OCTOBER 28, 1893.</head>
        <p>Tim EXAMINER, SATURD AV. OCTOBER 28, 1893.</p>
        <p>land when they have won their hard fought battle will see that the colliers of Charleroi, Mons Landrecies, I Matibeuge, and other foreign pins, do get wages equi- valent to the wages in England,even should they have to I support them by a special levy of is. per day on their I own earnings for a twelve months or fwo years strike. I That could be done easily enough as there are ten times, ' and more than ten times, more colliers in England than in the whole of Europe, and it will pay our cllliers better to do it than be brought down to the lower level as ad- vocated by our kind coal-owners. No, Judas, we will not retrograde but " Onward" is our motto, even if all the family of Iscariot' s were to he employed printing and distributing tracts to try and mislead the people of War- rington ; and I trust the tradesmen and working-men who can afford it will never withhold their mite from the , colliers on Judas's advice.—Yours ALFRED BROUCHEMIT E. . Cosmopolitan Hotel, Friars Green, Warrington, Oct. , 24, 1823.</p>
        <p>euphonious cognomen of the Zingari, and was I captained by Gr.Edwards.This club was a little ",theeky," ; and had a superabundance of "edge." The ExoelAor, however, meant to take this out of the players with a fancy name. So they challenged them to two matOws, E. W. being now a meniter of bath clubs, played with a different team each time. But each time the Excelsior won. So the Zingari' said, "Join us," and they did.: This was in 1880—or thereabouts—and Mr. Warren was then unanimously chosen as captain, lie having distin- guished himself, along with Mr. T. Pemberton, at half back. For the next three years Warren held the cap- taincy, and it was during those three years that the team acquired the name of the Warrington Club, toge- ther with first-class fixtures and good gates. Indeed, it was now that the game blossomed hi the town, and a large part of the credit of this is due to Mr. Warren.</p>
        <p>Next we come to 1885 6, the most eventful years in the history of foot ball in Wariington. W.E. was again the leading " General," the club having been brought up, after an amalgamation with the Wanderers, to a " form" which has never yet been surpassed. In the first part of the period alluded to, the West Lancashire v. Cumberland match was played at Warrington, and won by Lnncashire, who were captained by Warren—a great honour, I trow for Warrington. The next match played by Warrington was against Oldham, and Warren received serious injury which prevented him playing for the Lancashire cup, the competitions in which started the next week. Ibis was very much regretted, and though the cup was won without the actual assistance of Warren, the team was loth to allow him to be separ- ated from the attendant honour.</p>
        <p>He was made captain again in 1887-8, but after that busird himself with the now increasing secretarial work at which he has since laboured assiduously and well. There is no one who can claim to have dons more than Warren, for football in Warrington. In addition to all this he has won dozens of first class prizes as a runner, been one of the chief promoters of the gymnasium in the town and has been before the piblic of Warrington for mole than 15 years as all round athlete. He is as capable in organizing and discussing the theories of sport, ai at earring them out.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>STATE OF CRIME IN WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>STATE OF CRIME IN WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>CHIEF CONSTABLE'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>The report of Mr. S. Runt (Chief Constable), to the Watch Committee, on the police establishment, and of the state of clime in Warrington, for the year ended 29th teptember, 1893, has just been issued.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>THR RECENT SHOP BURGLARIPS.</head>
        <p>THR RECENT SHOP BURGLARIPS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hunt says he desires to Call attention to the breaking and entering shops that have no shutters, but which contain valuable goods displayed in the windows. Two of these, situate in Church-st., are pawnbrokers' shops (Greave's and Jackson's), and alongside, or close by them, are narrow openings leading to the fields at the back of the premises. These openings give thieves a splendid opportunity of making a dash at the windows with stones or brick-ends, breaking the panes, and then helping themselves to any valuables within reach, and getting clear away before it could be possible for the police or any persons sleeping on the premises getting a view of them. This has 'occurred at Jackson's, Church- street ; Houghton's, Mersey-street; and lastly, at Ur. Boothroyd's Music Shop,Sankey-street,which is situated in quite as favourable a locality for thieves asnre the three previously mentioned. The depredators make their arrangements beforehand, and when they find the streets free from the police, and from ordinary passers by, a crash of glass is heard, and immediately after the tramp of the persons, who have taken all they could see or reach, running away. As the winter approaches Mr. Hunt says he is afraid we shall have more of these sort of robberies unless owners of such shops take better pre- cautions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>NON•INDICTABLE OFFENCES.</head>
        <p>NON•INDICTABLE OFFENCES.</p>
        <p>Drunkenness : Pcrsons proceeded against, 603 ; con- vitted,s3o ; discharged,73; publicans proceeded against, 4 ; convicted, 3 ; beerhouse keepers proceeded against,l, whose case was dismissed. Assaults on : Proceeded against, 32 ; convicted, 27; dischatged,s. Assaults (ag- gravated) :,-Proceeded against, 11 ; all being convicted. a,saults (common) : Procee led against, 143; convicted, 64 ; discharged, 84. Cruelty to animals : Proceeded against, 52 ; convicted, 43 ; discharged 9. Cruelty to children : Proceeded against, 10 ; convicted, 6 ; dis- charged, 4. Elementary education : Proceeded against, 370 ; convicted, 322 ; discharged, 48.</p>
        <p>MISCELLAN EOITS. ,! The total offences disposed of summarily during the I year Is: Indictable 109, non-indictable 1,965, and quasi. criminal 219, in all 2,143. Six convicts on license have reported to the police. 4 have left for other towns, 1 : license expired, and 1 still resides in the borough. Nine supervisers have also reported, 7 have left for other towns, 1 died, and 1 was sentenced te 12 months for fraud. 'Ph,: number of chitiren detained in Reformatory and Industrial Schools is 43, for whose part maintenance the sum of £B7 83. 6d. has been paid by parents duriog the year, Seventy pedlars' and 6 chimney sweepers' certificates have beer: granted during the year. amount- ing to £l6 153. Seventy three persons have been brought in on suspicion, but allowed to go for want of evidence, and 70 other persons have been apprehended by or received from the police of other districts. Six persons have been summoned for keeping dogs without licences, 5 being convicted and 1 dismissed. Of sudden, violent, and unnatural deaths 120 were reported to the coroner through the police, inquest&amp; being held on 51 cases, 65 referred to the registrar, and 4 certified by the doctors. The verdicts were: 24 accidental, 10 natural causes, 6 open, 5 suicide during temporary insanity, 1 small-pox, 1 syncope 1 bronchitis, 1 marasnius, and 1 poisoning (accidenal;. Nine fires took place in the borough, damage being done probably to the amount of £4,500, mostly covered by insurance. Toe care 15 can ww,rs, and 71 cab and 'bus drivers, 10 marine store dealers, 6 gunpowder dealers, and 34 small fireworks dealers. Lost children to the number cit 65 were found and restored to their parents, 56 horses, cattle and sheep found straying were taken to the pinfold until owned. One hundred and ninety-five shops, warehouses, and houses were found insecure by the police on night duty, the owners not having fastened all the doors ald win- dows. The population by census of 1891 is 52,743; ratable value, £168,894 ss. ; acreage, 2,004 ; inhabited houses, 9,631. Gross total cost of police £4,900 18r. fld. ; the value of the police superannuation fund being £8,677 Bs. lid., with a charge of about 193. 6.1. per week upon it. The sum of £2B 183. for services of police, including £1 103. from the coroner for use of Parade Room, was paid in due course b the B &gt;rough Treasurer, and placed by him to the credit of the police superannua- tion fund. The borough contains 87 licensed victuallers, 45 beerhouse keepers, 60 off-license holders,and 5 refresh- ment houses with wine licenses; total, 206.</p>
        <p>POLICE RETURNS. Strength of force on September 29th, 1893, 54 all told, there being no vacancies ; an augmentation of 2 has taken place since September 29th, 1892. The variations are: 1 resigned voluntarily on religious scruples, and 1 dis• missed for drunkenness, therefore the force has been well sustained during the year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>ST. JOHN AMBULANCE CLASS.</head>
        <p>THE ST. JOHN AMBULANCE CLASS.</p>
        <p>Number holding certificates: 1 head constable, 4 in- spectors, 6 sergeants, and 30 constables, total 41. "First aid" has been given in 20 cases. Leave of absence granted to all ranks 655 clays, sick leave 410 days, and hurt upon duty 17 days. The police force was inspected by Sir Herbert Croft, Hart., on March Bth last, and I have reason to believe he was satisfied with the result, but condemned the building.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>SCHOOL ATTENDANCE IN WA It ItINOTON.</head>
        <p>SCHOOL ATTENDANCE IN WA It ItINOTON.</p>
        <p>The School Attendance Committee of the War- rington Town Council has just issued its annual re- port for the year ended Oct. 31st., 1892. It states) that the sixteenth annual rep.ort of the school at- tendance will not be available for comparison with previous years as regards the number of children presented for examination, in consequence of several schools in the town not having been examined by Her Majesty's Inspectors, owing to the epidemic of smallpox which prevailed in the borough at the time when the examinations ought to have taken place. The report of the chief school attendance inspector was to the effect that the average attendance for the year end- ing September, 1892, was 8,198 ; in 1891 the average attendanc4 was 7,704, an increase for 1892 of 494. This was an increase he did not expect considering the havoc made by the epidemic, but the cheap school fees caused more children to attend, and particularly inlants. At the close of the school year the number of children on the tolls was 10,426, but of this number 1,343 were un- der the age of five years. The average am:lir:lance for the year, 8,198, against the number no the rolls Or elud- ing the five year infants), va., 10,420, yielded a per- centage of 78. The number of children attending private schools, including the Blue School, Grammar, and Work- Ito4se Schools, is 667, t ut nearly all the Workhouse chil- dren were at l'adgate. The full attendance at school of all ages, 3 to 14 at both public and private schools was 8,865. The Committee further reported that if the examinations had taken place the increase would have been 615. The . prosecutions for offences against the Education Act for the year 11192 were 366. In 1891 the number was 483, a decrease of 117. Five boys were sent to the " Clio,"and seven to St, Anne's Industrial School, Liverpool. 'Fife Board of Guardians had allowed school fees during the year td the amount of £42 13s fild.,the previous year the amount was £l5O 163 6d.,the decrease being £lOB 2s 91. The districts requiring additional school accommodation were in Battersby-lane, Lovely-lane, and Winwick-road. The only schools in these parts were the Hamilton-st., St. Barnabas', St. Anne's, and Silver-street, all over- crowded. The Committees meetings in 1892 numbered 22, and the parents of over 600 children were summoned to appear belore them. A special examination for Labour Certificates was held at the request of the Committee by one of Her Majesty's Inspectors, on the 4th March, and the following was the result of such examination : 88 passed, 50 failed, total 138. The 88 children who passed the examination were thus enabled to go either to partial or total employment. The want of better accommodation generally wasieenly felt in certain congested parts of the Borough, and greater efforts would certainly have to be made before long to provide far the growing demands of those districts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>PEN KETII. .</head>
        <p>PEN KETII. .</p>
        <p>TEA MEETINO.—On Tuesday niaht the St. Mary's Church Tetnperance Association gave a tea party to the members in the infants' school room. A good number sat down to a ( apital tea under the charge of Mrs. Aitken, Misses Tinsley, Miss Holten, Miss Byrnes. After tea the room was cleared and games and other amuse- ments were indulged in and a most enjoyable evening spent. Public meetings are to be held monthly, and these will be of an entertaining cbaticter.</p>
        <p>SECOND CitOPS.—hi consequence of Vie early fruit season there are several pople in this village who are having a second crop. At one place there are some very fine, well developed strawberries, and Mr. J. Lloyd has a pear tree from which he has just gathered some excep- tionally fine fruit,.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND</p>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Phew ! happened again ; everybody deceived once more. It ca.n saft-ly be said that at least 6,000 out of the tremendous gate of 8,030, which assembled on the Causeway field, on Saturday,fully and iricerely believed that Warrington were going to do the trick with Oldham. Instead of w hich they go and cover their opponants all over with glory instead of themselves, and allow those "horny handed sons of toil" from " Owdam "to knock thsm into the most complete and unregenerate of"Cocktd hats," that ever disfigured the heads of the wearers dur- ing the whole of the season.</p>
        <p>Perhaps it was unwise of the Warrington enthusiasts to place such implicit trust in the winning proclivities of the home tt am, because en far this season they have been about as reliable as the English climate. In addi- tion to this, it was not wise to expect them to make a " shew " against such players as Oldham included in their ranks. Why, such men as M'Cutcheon and Hurst are a " little too far above the ordinary level." And it was soon seen that the opponents were by far our superiors in their backs. There was another thing, however, whieh militated against the home men, and that was the esiorced absence of Joim Bate and Evans, both of whom were otherwise engaged in playing off the effects of injuries received in the match of Whites v. Stripes at Manchester. There was also another matter which told apin,t our home team—in the eyes hf the visitors—and Vila was the fact that they kept their opponents waiting on the ground 15 minutes whilst they went-a-hunting for players. Why was this. Surely the players were not beginning to funk. However, when the team (I'd tut n out I was glad to see that it included Barnes of by-gone fame, and H. Ashton, as captain, who donned " the gaudy colours " for the first time this season.</p>
        <p>Well, now about the game. It was interesting, it was exciting—yea, it was instructive enough for the most fastidious. But the instruction was all imparted to Warrington. When play remained among the forwards our home men were not a whit too slow, but alas ! when the ball found its way among the backs of the visitors then there were exhibited such performances of dex- terity and " science " which relegated the actions of our backs into a dim and obscure background. The game can be said to have been completely won by the visit:Jr&amp; backs, assisted not a little by the poor form of our oppo- site men. Boscow seemed to have lost all his old activity in kicking and tackling, and his play though a little " orpli " all the season, was particularly so on this occa- sion. So much does "Surefoot " feel this—what I hope is a temporary indisposition—that lie has asked to be re- lieved of the onerous responsibilities of the position, and I am told that his place will be filled by Heesom to-day. The latter together with Potts worked hard at three- quarters, as did also Carey, who however seemed rather "flurried," and uncertain. Perhaps he was effected with the excitement so fatal to many successful young players.</p>
        <p>Smack Bate and Lally both won showers of applause for their various activities, and they were good examples of the prevailing individual brilliance—but they also demonstrated the general absence of proper and mutu- ally understood combination. This will have to be ended if the team intendi to hold its own with even its ordinary fixtures. Mare will have to be a change somewhere. The qaestion is where ?</p>
        <p>On Saturday, Salford give Us a visit. They hold about the same positicn as Warrington in the competi- tion, so the game should be interesting. No ! I won't prophesy. These are the results of the competition up to Saturday Phi. Won. Lost. Drn GT. G.T. Pts. For. Ag-t. Wigan 5 . 4 . 0.. 1 . 3 5 ... 0 0 ... 9 Swinton 5 . 3 . 0 . 2 . 5 6 ... 1 0 ... 8 Oldham 5 . 3 . 1 . 1 . 109 ... 2 1 ... Tyldesley Salford Warrington Broughton Hangers 5 ... 2 ... 3 ... 0 ... 5 1 ... 4 6 ... 4 ltoeh,la e Hornets Barrow .4 ... 3</p>
        <p>irOught.= 5 0 . 5 . 0 1 1 -.15 10 ... 0</p>
        <p>The Warrington Rangers first team journeyed to Man- chester on Saturday afternoon to meet the first team of Owen's College,. and it must be admitted that they achieved a notable victory, when they beat the colleg- ians by one gall and a try to one goal. It was not a very severe beating for the homesters, but enough for the Rangers to add another " w" on their fixture card. The second team of Owen's College played the Rangers secmd on their ground in Lovely-lane, but unfortun- ately only brought eleven men, and had to find sub- stitutes. They were defeated to the tune of 1 goal five tries to nil.</p>
        <p>The St. Mary's first team went across the town to the Parish Church ground,and were determined not to have another defeat recorded against them,which would have been the third in succession. Well they weren't defeaed, but somebody else was, and that was their cpponents, the Parish Church team. The St. Mary's men were by far the better team, and ran out victors by two goals two tries to one goal registered by their opponents.</p>
        <p>The second of the trial matches preliminary to the commencement of (Le Lancashire inter county fixtures was played on Wednesday at Whalley Range. Two teams —Probables and Possibles—had been chosen, but the failure of Valentine, M'Cutcheon, and Hurst to put in an appearance at three quarter back for the Probables, and of Miles at three-quartersand Kent,Stocldart, and Simp- son forwards for the Possibles, necessitated important changes in the sides. This, however, gave a number of the reserves an opportunity to show their ability, and was not altogether to be regretted. The conclu:ion of the game brought the following scores :—Possiblos, one goal and two tries (11 points) ; Probables, nil. The following were the teams :—Proba.bles : T. Coop (Leigh, captain), full back; F. Howarth (Crompton), P. Tree- weeke (loose ,Barrow), A. Barrett (Salford), and R. Holmes (Morecatnbe), three-quarter bucks; James T. Bate (Warrington) and W. Parlane (Manchester Rangers) half backs ; W. Hnsworth (Wigan), 11. Case (Swinton), J. B. Andrew (Oldham), T. King (Salford), T. Melledew (Rochdale Hornets), J. Jolley (Warringt4n), R. Pierc,!. (Liverpool), and A. Ashworth (Rochdale Hornets), for- wards. Possibles : W. Winterbottom (Swinton), full back; S. Lees (Oldham), J. IVinstanley (Wigan), F. Auderton (I,cgli), and T. Sudlow (St. Helens i, three- quarter hacks; J. Drummond (Widnes) and W. Halliwell (Wigan), half backs ; W. Dixon (Askain), T. Whitt tker (Wallnien), J. Simpson (Rochdale Hornets), T. Butter- wort:l (Rochdale Hornets), G. H. Murray (Swinton, cap- tain), W. Nevins (Warrington), J. Shepherd (Tyldesley), W. Wilson (St. Helens), and J. C. Goold (Liverpool uld lloys),Jorwards. Referee : Mr. J. H. Smith, Widnes. Touch-line judges : Messrs. F. Grover and J. Mills, Swinton ; and Mr. J. Robertson, Broughton Rangers.</p>
        <p>The same evening the following selections were made for the Lancashire and Cheshire championship Match to be played at Malley Range on November 4 :—T. Coop (Leigh), full back ; W. WCutcheon (Oldham), J. Valen- tine (Swinton, captain), A. Barr(t.; (Salford), and. J Hurst (Oldham), three-quarter backs ; James 'l'. Bate (Warrington) and W. Parlane (Manchester Rangers), half baeks ; G. H. Murray (S wintnn), J. C. Ooohi (Liver- pool Old Boys), J. Shepherd (Tyldeslev), J. Simpson (Rochdale Hornet 3), J. B. Andrew (Oldham), H. Case (Swinton), IV. Unsworth (Wigan), and W. Nevins (War- rington), forwards. 11-serves : I'. Foulkes (St. Helens), full back; F. Miles (Salford), S. Lees • (Oldham), T. Anderton (Leigh), and It. Holmes (Morecombe), three- quarter backs ; J. Drummond (Widnes) and W. Halliq ell (Wigan), half hacks ; T. King (Salford), A. Ashworth (Rochdale Hornets), and T. IVhittaker (Walkden), forwards.</p>
        <p>" ANTECEDMITS."</p>
        <p>This week I review, as far as lam able, the career of one who has for the last decade been, perhaps, the most central figure in connection with local football. This is Mr. J.E.Warren, a name inseparable from the annals of the Warrington football club. True, he does not fii:Ure now in the "fray," for he has laid aside his jersey and pants, and his football shoes are along with other trophies, stored away in the receptacle for antiquities. But tho' he has retired from immediate view from "battle field," he is now one of the most active wirepullers—not of the game in Warrington, but in the limn. He has passed through a successful career of active field work, and now has the honour of being the selected representative on the Lancashire Committee, for the West qf tile county. , Of his services to the game generally in this official posi- tion. his attentive interest in all matters brought before the Committee have been a sufficient testimony. That he has been apprecated by the committee itself is proved by the weight that is always placed upon his suggestions in the" conclave," and also by the fact that he has bean made a member of that important body, the Referees Committee. 'Fhat his selection for this work has been beneficisl to ‘Varrington is very evident from the manner in which the " form " of our good players is brought into consideration, and the fact that during the past two years mere Warrington men have been decorated with county honours than ever before.</p>
        <p>Then Mr. Warren has for many years nuvr discharged the laborious duties of secretary to the club, besides aiding it with his advice, which has necessarily the quality of being valuable from the very fact that it the outcome of a long connection with football. Be- sides being thoroughly fitted for the discharge of the clerical and representative walk, the subject of this little iketch has been the very epitome of versatility in the gains generally. But let us begin at the beginning —or as near ASCVC can. Well, in the year 1877—or, mas be I had better say, thereabouts—E. W. joined the Pa dg,aie Football club, organised by. Mr. A Bennett. The home field was at Bruche Farm, the " home quarters" in a barn, whilst the White Hart was ussd as the town quarters.—Thia club—the old Excelsior—was,strange to say, very prolilia in yielding good players, for I find that in addition to E. W. it included the following , players of the Lancashire cup fame :—W. Dillon, Povey, Rigby, T. Pemberton, Fair. Turner, icc. At thisl time the Warrington club swaggered under the 1</p>
        <p>-</p>
        <p>DISTRESS IN WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>There is very little change in the condition of things in Warrington, in connection with the coal dispute. With the exception of the Whitecross Wire Company, the works of the town are in pretty much the same condition as they were last week. Owing to the deduction of a penny in the shilling from the wages of the etnployes of this firm, they have had almost " full time" in the wire depart- ment, but the forge is unuffected by this arrange- ment, only one mill working during the present week. As there are signs that the end of the un- fot tunate dispute is almost in sight, a more hopeful tone has begun to prevade the town. The ordinary price of coal this week is is. 4d. per cwt. In the meantime the Corporation are giving considerable assistance to the unemployed. Mr. It. Norman nuisance inspector has about 50 men employed each week at 3s. a day. They are employed in cleansing streets, courts, passages, and disinfecting and flushing drains. Mr. Ch arch, the sanitary works inspector employs about 20 men each week in simi- lar work. WHITECROSS WORKS AND THE DISTRESS.</p>
        <p>A proposal was made at a meeting of the Whitecross employes on Friday afternoon week, to contribute a penny in the shilling, to defray the extra expense of the coal, und thus enable the firm to find more work for their employes. It was decided to have a ballot that aft2rnoon and night. The ballot, however, resulted in a defeat of the proposal by a large majority. On Monday, at the instigation of the masters, another meeting was held to re-consider the matter, when the former decision was reversed, and the proposal was adopted. The reduction commences this week, and will be in force for three weeks. CONCERT IN AID OF THE DISTRESS.</p>
        <p>A most successful and well attended concert, the proceeds of which are to devoted to the Warrington Distress Fund, was held in Bold-st. Wesleyan School, on Tuesday evening. Miss Bessie Dutton occupied the chair. The artistes who gave their services were —Misses Pick- ton, Gornall, 13, Dutton, E. Bennett, and L. Mclver ; Messrs. T. Boothroyd, F. Fieldhouse, W. E. Ashton, F. Sandal!, J. Emson, and F. Hickman, who accompanied. Miss B. Dutton in a few preliminary remarks, said they had helrd a great deal about "The Three Little Maids of Legh-st.," and she was pleased to say that four little maids had arranged that concert, two sets of sisters, she believed. Miss Dutton then referred to the distress in the town, and urged her hearers not simply to come to a concert, Out feel that they must deny themselves of something for the benefit of those who were suffering in their midst. Mr. T. 13,iothrovd excellently rendered two violin solos, and was rewarded with an encore. "The Carnival," and " The Venetian Boat Song," were well sung by Miss Gornall. Miss Bessie Dutton is an admir- able reader, and her two contributions highly pleased the audience. Mr. F. Fieldhouse's two songs were " Herrings are in the Bay " (Molly), and "In &amp;mitered Vale" (old German melody). Miss Pickton received a well deserved encore for her song ":;hildren asleep " (F. Moir), and Mr. J. Emson was awarded a similar honour for his singing of " I'm not, I'm not," and complied with "The last time." Mr. W. E. Ashton qmsontributed a re- citation " The execution of Montrose," and Mr. F. ban- dall gave a performance on fairy bells. A sung entitled " Dolly " was rendered by Miss L. Mclver, and Miss E. Bennett gave a pianoforte solo " Palaccl Op. 72 (Weber). The amount handed to the treasurer of the Distress Fund as a result of the concert was £7. It may be mentioned that the concort was arranged by four little girls, viz : Miss Edith Mclver, Miss Lena Mclver, Miss Ethel Ashton, and Miss Ettie Ashton.</p>
        <p>THE RELIEF COMMITTEE AND Tlllll COAL DISPUTE.</p>
        <p>The following is a copy of a resolution passed at a meeting of the Executive (R-lief) Committee on Tuesday morning, and which was forwarded by the Chair- man (Mr. Monks, J.P.) to the Local Government Board, the Board of Trade, several prominent Govern- ment officials, the member for the Borough, and the county members :—" Resolved that this meeting,formed for the purpose of relieving the distress caused by i-ither the entire or partial stoppage of the various works in Warrington, begs to memorialize the Government to intervene and take steps to speedily settle the present dispute in the c-al trad,i ; and at tie same time enters its earnest protest against the most unsa'isfactory method now employed of settling trade differences, whereby in the present instance 5,000 persons in War- rington alone have been plunged into a state. of semi- starvation and intense suffering, through no fault of their own ; this committee further urges upon the Government to suggest such measures as will establish some more reasonable and just method of settling wage disputes between capital and labour."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>SANKEY BRIDGES.</head>
        <p>SANKEY BRIDGES.</p>
        <p>The numbers who have had free teas during the past week at the Sankey Bridges Wesleyan Schoolroom are as follows: Saturday 220 . men, Tuesday 4,60 children, Wednesday, 150. women, and Thursday 430 children. There was no concert on Siturday night owing to busi- ness iu connection with the Sunday school requiring to be trabsacted. Several subscriptions have come in dur- ing the week and also hampers of potatoes from Messrs. J. Bennett of Bewsey, and Purslow of Appleton. It is intended to continuo the Saturday night concerts during the winter months. RELIEF COMMITTEE'S WEEKLY MEETING. INCREASED SUBSCRIPTIONS NEEDED. SERIOUS CONDITION OF THE TOWN.</p>
        <p>The weekly meeting of the relief committee was held yesterday afternoon in the Mayor's Parlour of the Town Hall, the Mayor occupying the chair. Mr. F. Monks in giving the weekly statement said that first of all he would read a latter from the B iarcl of Trade in reply to the resolution passed at the last meet- ing cif the committee and forwarded to-that department. It was as follows r--Sir,--I have the horour to acknow- ledge the receipt of your letter of the 25th October, addressed to Mr. Mundella and enclosing the copy of a resolution passed by a general meeting of the Warring- ton Relief Committee.—l am yours obediently, F. W. BLOOMFIELD." It was simply au acknowledgment of the resolution. With regard to the distribution of food he ! was sorry to have to report a considerable increase, thus showing an increase in the destitution in the town. During tho week there had been 25,080 pints of soup given against 23,198 last week,aniucrease of 2,000 pints. There had also been 20,9081bs of bread distributed this week against 25,193 last week, an increase of 4,800. In addition there had teen 1,279 half pounds of sugar a.nd ozs. of tea against 1,250 last week. The cent for rclief this week had been £lB3 us. 110.,against £lBll 19s. 4d. for the previous week, being the consideraltle increase of £22. The total subscriptions received up to date had been £1,193 23. 51, and the total expenditure £1,024 18. Bd., leaving a balance in hand of £1685. ffl. The subscriptions during the week had been £ll5 Bs. id. besides the weekly subscriptions of £34 103., the total amount received being £149 Be. id. or £3O less than what was expended. It would be of interest to give the total subscriptions received each week of the present mouth. They were October 7, £179 175., October 14, (after an earnest appeal had been made) £227 33.10 d., Oct. 21, £lO6 25., Oct. 28, £149 Be. Id., making atotal of £BB2 10a. 11d. fertho month. Considering that it was uncertain how long the strike was going to last yet, and considering that the subscriptions were not coming in very quickly, the executive committee did not feel able ' to recommend that tea and sugar should be given out ! this week, and therefore they recommended that only bread be given on Saturday afternoon. 'This was thought necessary as the funds would not last more than another week. If the public would respond to the committee's appeal Sr as to justify a renewal of the tea and sugar,the committee would only be tOo glad to do so. In view of the falling off of the subscriptions for the last two weeks the committee had also in consideration the atetion of the Florence Mill people, and they had passed a reso- lution which he would ask the sccretsey, Mr. Wilsot to</p>
        <p>read. It was to recommend them to do as the Coek- hedge and other employees had done, viz., to contribute something towards the cost of fuel. It would pAy the relief committee to defray the entire cost of the cal for th^se mills rather than to have the employees to relieve. The tickets to the employes would accordingly be stopped on Saturday. He could only concluda by urgently appealing to his fellow townsmen to continue their sub- scriptions as long as it would be necessary. The suffer- lag in the town was very acute and he was quite certain must be extremely painful to those who came in contact with it from day to day. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Staaley Wilson then read the resolution with respect to Florence Mills.</p>
        <p>The Mayor said that a large anr4mt of . money had been received, but there were some people who had given nothing yet,and he did not know how to approach them. They, refttsed to contribute anything. The Cor- poration had done what they could in the matter and they had applied to the Local Government Board for money to carry on the work. He was getting tired of the mention of the distress as there were firms who employed thousands of men, and made that the excuse for not giving, and yet large numbers of their workmen were being relieved by the Committee. He had appealed to them himself, but without result. The stopping of the tickets to the Florence Mill people would cause a reduction of 400 lbs. of bread per day. Rev. Owen Watkins ta'd that they were face to face with the question whether the large and wealthy town of Warrington—and they knew that it was wealthy— was going to cease altogether in giving relief to those who were out of employment through no fault of their own. He acknowledged very gratefully and the Com- mittee also acknowledged the very generous response that had been made by many of the townspe)ple to the appeals tint had been made by the executive. It could not be too distinctly understood that unless there was a large increase of the subscriptions which would justifiy , the Committee to continue the rate of relief, they would have to recommend to the meeting bast ad of giving a pint of soup and a pound of bread to each adult—and some people were living on nothing else. (Hear, hear.) i—they would have to recommend half a pint of soup and half a pound of bread. In a Christian county like this, that was a serious matter, and he was sure that no one in that room would contemplate with any feeling but one of deep regret the prospect of such an alternative. He wondered whether there could be no means to increase the funds. He suggested that the ladies do something similar to what the ladies of London do for the hospitals on Hospital Saturday. It was appalling that they should now be face to face with the fact that in Warrington there were 8,000 persons receiving relief from day to day and that they were within measurable distance of that relief ceasing altogether, The Mayor said that the ladies were doing a noble work (hear, hear) and it was a great tie to them to be at work every day. Mr. Monks said he did not know what else they could do. Perhaps Mr. Watkins' appeal would do some good. The Rev. W. H. Drumm-did suggested that circulars be sent round asking for weekly subscriptions and stating the terrible distress that prevailed in the town. . The Rev. Owen Watkins said that on Sunday he was going to appeal to the members of his church for weekly subscriptions and he would mad them in a lump sum to the Town Hall. (Rear, hear.) Mr. Monks said that there was a considerable number of wealthy men who were altogether indifferent to the suffering in the town. If their Muses were burnt or something of that sort done, that might perhaps waken them up, and if the present state of things continued that might happen. He hoped however that nothing ap- proaching that would take place, but he trusted that the wealthy people would contribute something towards the funds.</p>
        <p>A number of questions were asked about Whitecross Works being opened again and Mr. D. Fd nandw explained that it was only the wire department which was work- ing under the reduction of a penny in the shilling and not the forge. One mill however had been working dur- ipg the present week. The Rev. J. Yonge thought a deputation might wait upon soine of the men who refused to contribute, and be offered to be one. He did not like the idea of stopping the tea and sugar at the week end,and moved that they be supplied this week as usual. He also thoth.ht that an idea was abroad that there was a lot of irnposii.in practised upon the committee. and that might cause some people to be backward in giving. There was very little imposition, however. The Rev W. H. Drummond seconded, and suggested that gentlemen in the room should guarantee that there be no loss incurred by this step. The Rev. E. C. E. Carleton supported the resolu- tion.</p>
        <p>, Mr. Fairclough thought that the executive committee should have a tree hand in the matter. Mr. Monks said that it was for the meeting to choose, and on the resolutioa being put, it was carried unani- mously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>WARRINGTON CHURCH SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CHURCH SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>THE FIRST .4NNIVERSARY.</p>
        <p>The first anniversary festival in connection with the Warrington Church society, was held yester- day. There was Holy Communion at the Parish Church, at 8 o'clock a.m., and evensong and ser- mon in the evening, the preacher . being Chancellor Espins-D.D. The annual meeting of the society was held after the evening service, in the National Schools, Church-street, for the purpose of electing officers for the ensuing year, enrolling, new mem- bers, and other business. Mr. Herbert Woods, the secretary, in his report said it would be recollected that prior to the actual forma- tion of the-society a number of meetings of churchmen, and later of a provisiotial eommittee chosen from those who attended those meetings were held, and the objects of the satiety, alter much discussion and careful thought were laid down. A meeting was held on the 20th June, 1892, when the objects and rules of the society were read and adopted, officers were appointed, and the society came into existence. The Rev. Canon West, of. • Choriton-on-Medlock de- livered an interesting address on the intended work of the society and referred at length to the duties of churchmen as regards disendowment, de- naoninational education, and temperance. During the Parliamentary elections various questions, decided upon by the Committee, were addressed to the local candi- dates, the answers to which bad been known to them, and were published in the local papers. The worship- ful, the chancellor of the diocese, the Rev. E. Espin, ,D.D., addressed a meeting on 4th November 1892, and urged on the members the necessity of thoroughly in- structing themselves in the history and principles of the church. The Rev.. C. C.•Elcum very kindly delivered two interesting and instructive lectures. The first was on let December 1592 on "Glastonbury Abbey" and the second on 12th January 1893 on "The Reformation in the latter part of Henry VIII's reign." The Sotsety was was much indebted to H. Houghton for providing and manipulating the lantern at both of the lectures. A meeting of churchmen was held on tho 16ch March, 1892 to protest against the Welsh Suspensory Bill when an able address was given by Mr. 13yrom Reed. One out- come of the meeting was a petition to the House of Com- mons against the hill, The petition bore 1326 signa- tures, and was presented Le the House of Commons by Mr. Robert Pierpotnt, the member for Warrington, on the 6th June. The society consisted of 77 members, of whom four had left the neighbourhood, one had resigned and, he regretted to add, one (Mr.F.W.Walker, the late assistant Borough Surveyor) had died. The president of the aociety,Rev.F.W. Willis, occupied the chair.</p>
        <p>„, THE NEW r r,NGINE. The new steam fire engine specially constructed to the order of the Warrington Town Council is ex- pected to arrive by the end of this month. Messrs. Merryweather and Soots, of Greenwich, the makers of the engine, we are informed, will send one of their patent hydiaulic sewer hustlers for trial. This ingenious contrivance can be worked either from the water supply of the town, or, if in any district the pressure on the mains should be insufficient, one of the steam tire engines can lend material as- sistance. A great feature of this system of hydrau- lic flushing is that ,only a minimum quantity of water is used owing to the washing jets being dis- charged under great pressure. A LANDLADY AND HER LODGER&amp;</p>
        <p>Sarah Cronoigan, 49 liewsey-st., claimed at the Warrington County Court on Thursday from Sylvanus Taylor, a member of Messrs. Calvert and Helmsley's Repertoire Co., 245. for breach of an agreement with respect to the letting of rooms, and 38. lid., the value of a leg of mutton. Air. A. Browne appeared for plaintiff, and Mr. P. Davies for the defendant. The plaintiff's case was to the effect that the defendant wrote from the Isle of Alan engaging rooms at the plaintiff's house, and stating that he would arrive in Warrington at three o'clock on October 2nd. Ile also asked Mrs. Cronotan to purchase a leg of mutton. In another letter lie said that he might arrive at twelve o'clock. On the day in question, at nine o'clock in the morn- ing, Mr. and Mrs. Taylor came to the house and Mrs. Cronoigan and another person were putting the room in order. The plaintiff did not expect the defen !ant until twelve o'clock. He complained that the rooms were dirty, and refused to pay for the leg of mutton.—Mrs. Cronoigan in her evidence said that she would have had the rooms ready by twelve o'clock, and denied that the rooms were dirty..-In cross-examination she admitted that she did not wish anyone to remain in the house unless they were comfortable They then left the house. —The defendant and witnesses called said that the rooms were in a dirty condition.—His Honour held that the condition of the rooms did not justify the defendant in leaving so suddenly, as he supposed they could have been put in order before 12 o'clock, the time the defendant was expected to arrive. The remarks of Mrs. Cronoigan however relieved him from his conttact. He gave judgment for the defend:int,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTII</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON. During the week, which was the 41st of current year, the following births and death.) registered :—Births : Warrington, 35 : Larct'' 3; total,3B. Deaths: Warrington.26 •, Latchfot total 27. The excess of births over deaths r; Thejleath-rate from all causes was equal to 1.0, against • 249 the average of the previous weeks, and against 30.8 the corresponding Nv last year. The death-rate from the seven zymotic diseases was equal to 1.5 per 1.000, 711 the average rate of the four previous wee] against 8.6 the corresponding week of The ages of mortality were :-0 to 1t05,9 ; 5 to 15. 1;15 to 25, 2 ; 250 60. :1 0; total, 26-1 27. The deaths included-40 measles; ltrona scarlet fever; 1 from cancer ; tubercular disease; 2 from lung disease brain disease; 2 from phthisis. The following' of infectious disease have been reported ing the week :—Cholera 1 ; scarlet fe'' enteric fever, 2. Meteorologl mein, perature was 671 Fah, and therefore 17 7 deg., than,the preceding week. The maximum tei ture 67 F., was recorded on the 17th ins; the minimum temperature 35 F., on tr Inst. The rainfall measured •10in. The direction was S.W.. the reading of the anet denoting 735 miles.—Joils 11. GonNALL, Officer of Health.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-28_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>THE SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>THE OPENING OF THE CANAL. At a meeting of the Manchester City Ci Wednesday, a letter was read from Sir John - Intimating that the Ship Canal works were bell ceeded with even more rapidly than was exile( that there would be about four weeks available .d purpose of testing all the locks, bridges, ga'o sn appliances, so as to have them in as perfect order' sible for January 1, if that day should be Exec' Opening.</p>
        <p>THE CAN&amp;L OPEN TO RUNCORN. At last the Manchester Ship Caual has beer' Pl', from Ea.stham to Runorn. Th 9 great waterwlY pleted between those two points. The first vessel' by this route on Saturday. This was the Ardc toes burden laden with china clay from Poole+ By using the canal instead of the river the . . .</p>
        <p>_ completed the journey in two diys less time th3n have been the case by the latter route. On MO! steamer Eastham belonging lo Messrs. Fisher, and Co. of Newcastle, arrived at Runcorn from The master, Captain Higham, Nought his ship Ship Canal without the help of a pilot, and stu put her alongside the lay-by ht Runcorn Doe steamer had a general cargo of merchandise 50 tons of tea, together with 200 or 300 tons 01 : weight" cargo._ The Eistham left for London on day afternoon with a general cargo, which inch tons of Manchester bale goods. The vessel w 53 tiously discharged and loaded at the lay-by. ' EAstham belongs the distinction of being the first Canal vessel dealt with at Runcorn. This London vice of steamships should prove of advantage to R9' tradesmen.</p>
        <p>ALLEGATIONS AGAINST AN INSITRA' COMPANTS OFFICIALS.</p>
        <p>The "Daily Chronicle' say;: We regret to ha' announce that irregularities of a very SPfjOUS C",V have just come to light in the insurance world AT. created a most painful impression. &amp;vete! Al', well known insurance coaipany holding high Dot have been implicated in losses which are varicos". mated to Fun from £15,000 to £40,000. Tue lett" is, we understand, exaggerated.</p>
        <p>- • The officials employed in municipal atialil tion are not usually credited with very Pr°g, ,', ideas, especially in Londm. It seems. , • that the spread of trade unionism is begio"l reach even so apparently unlikely a region', movement arising from within is being 89! pushed forward for the formation of an otW,cl or union of municipal officials with the uttual union objects of improving their material Pt and forming a friendly society or superanul fund for the benefit of the less fortunate Ine!fi, This new departure is perhaps the most aigT, proof of the extent to which the new princiP,Le bodied in the establishment of the County ve making their way even into the heart chaotic local adrnini4tration of London.</p>
        <p>One who has rt eently returned from It otne,; seen the Pope, repotta him to be in extrtmclY b 35 During the summer months he has become gri• weaker, and is unable to endure the slightest P exertion. The pork which Lo MIL was l, accustomed to do h plf is now executed at the from the iii ruction given by %In Pope. T</p>
