        <p>eltrtion citairtssts. MUNICIPAL ELECTION, 1893. TO THE BURGESSES OF TOWN HALL WAR D. LADIES AND GSWTLEMEN, My term pieties as one of your representatives in the Town Council having expired, I have decided, in re- sponse to the invitation of a large number of ratepayers, to offer myself for re-election. During the nine years I have been in the Town Council I have endeavoured to serve you to the best of my ability. I have always, both by my voice and vote, opposed what I be- /lever/ lobe extravagant and unnecessary expenditure, and will not hesitate to do so in the future. At the same time. I htve strongly supported every move- ment whleh in my judgment was con ducive to the best Interests of the ratepayers. I cordially supportel the freeing of our Public Library, the erectim of the New Baths, and you will doubtless remember the very active part I took In advocating and completing the purchase of tie Water Works, which has proved to be a great advantage to the Borough c, Warrington. I therefore appeal to you for your support on Nov ember Ist, believing that I still retain your confidence and approt al. lam. Ladies and Gentlemen, Your obedient Servant, 701 IN HENDERSON FLEMING. Springffeld-at., October 17th, 1893.</p>
        <p>MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS, 181)3. TOWN HALL WARD. TO THE BURGESSES OF THE WARD. LADIES AND GENTLEMEN, At the des:re of your Ward Committee, ani with the unanimous approval aud support of the Council of the Conservative Association, I have much pleasure in offering sou my services as one of your representatives in the Town Council. I am a resident in the Ward, your interests and mine are Identical, and I shall at all times be at hand and ready to hear the grievances of any of the burgesses, and so far as it lies in my power shah endeavour to have such grievances remedied. The distress which n ow unfortunately exists in the town will be an additional incentive to me to do all I can to secure due economy in all theaff airs of the borough. and it will ever be my earnest desire to promote the improvement of the town and its trade, so that Lab our and Wages may be created for the Working-classes and business f. r the Shopkeepers. I am moderate in my view s of things In g.neral, and I trust I shall never be led to attempt to do an injustice to any sect ion of the ratepayers in order to gratify my own caprice and crotchets, or the fads of any class of the community. I appeal to the burgesses of the Ward generally for their votes and active support on the polling clay. I am. Ladies and Gentlemen, Yours faithfully, JOSEPH Sin LLCOCK. Winwick Street. Warrington. October Ilth. 1393.</p>
        <p>AIUNICIPAL ELECTION, 189.31.. -- TO THE BURGESSES OF BEWSEY WARD.. LADIES AND GENTLEMEN, After urgent solicitation. I have consented to ask a renewal of your confidence. Born in Warrington, where my family has lived for generations. I have a thorough knowledge of the town, and I have spared no pains to become acquainted with its re- quirements. For six years now, I have thoughtfully and, diligently per- formed my duties in the Council. I have paid special at- tention t , the Fine.nces, and kept a watchful eye upon all Corporate Expenditure. I represent no private interest, however, large, as my Opponent tells you he does, but In all ways I have endeavoured to secure the true Welfare of the Ratepayers. My Record of Attendances, and of IVerk done, is the evi- dence of fitness upon which I rely to induce you to return me again RS your Representative. Trusting I may be favoured with your Support, I am, Yours faithfully, J. SHAW GREEN, Froghall Lane, 18th October, 1893.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS. 1893. BEWSRY WARD. THE BURGESSES OF THE WARD. LADIES AND GENTLEMEN.— Having been requested by an influential body of ratepayers in your ward to allow mrelf to be nominated as a candidate tn contest the seat becoming vacant on let November, I now offer my services and will be glad of your votes and support on that ( =Flom I am, MI you all know, a ratepayer in the borough, and I also represent the largebt firm of ratepayers in Warrington. I think these facts will ben sufficient guarawee to your interests as ratepayers. Any improvements required in the ward which may be brought under my notice will have my special care and attention. Soliciting your vote and favour, Believe me Ladies and Gentlemen, Your Obedient Servant, JOHN BAIRD.</p>
        <p>OROUG Il OF WARRINGTON. MUNICIPAL ELEC FION. 1893. TO THE ELECTORS OF ST. JOHN'S WARD. LADIES AND GENTLEMEN.. Having been nominated by a large and influential body of the Liberal party—(and at the special request of numerous Conservatlves)—as the Candidate for the vacant seat in the Town Council, I have consented to offer myself for election. have lived ai a boy and man amongst you for over 30 PAM and am a prorerty owner and a large ratepayer in the ward. My business Is in your midst, thsrefare understand- ing the wants of its people, I am more likely to attend to them, than one who has no personal connection with the ward. lam In favour of all Corporation Contracts being given to Local Manufacturers and Tradesmen, and so encouraging our own Industries. It will be my earnest endeavour to get the ratepayers a better gas at a less cost, as I am of opinion that the quality and price nre not chat we ought toexpect. Lam In favour of using all appliances and means to bring about a more healthy sanitary condition of our town, so as to prevent a repetition of the epidemic we had in the early part of the year. I shall endeavour to keep down the expenditure of the Borough 69 as to lessen the rates and enable landlords to re-- duce rents, and If you do me the honour of electing me as your representative, I shall do all I can for the general interests of the town. I am, Ladies and Gentlemen. Your obedient Servant, WILLIAM FURNESS. llaydobk-street and Lythgoes Lane, Warrington, October 13th. 1893.</p>
