        <p>MUNICIPAL ELECTION, 1893. TO THE BURGESSES OF ORFORD WARD. LADIPS AND GENTLEMEN, I regret that in consequence of failing he ilth your esteemed representative, Mr. James Walker, is unable to offer himself for re-election. I am desirous of serving the burgesses of Orford Ward,and I rejoice that upon this occasion I have promises of support from both political parties, as I de Madly object to the intro- duction of politics in municipal affairs. I have lived nearly the whole of my life in Warrington, and have taken MI active.part in public affair.. I have previou.ly occupied a seat In 'lto Town Council for nine years, and am fully acquainted with ti e work which, if lam elected. I shall be required to undort ,ke. I believe that healthy recreation is conducive to happiness, end I will endeavour to promote the acquirement of open spaces for playgrounds in crowded areas. I am also oropMion that small free lending libraries and newsrooms, in several p les of the town, could be established with great benefit to te localities, and without impairing the efficiency of the present Library and Museum. In conclusion, if you dons e the honour of electing mesa your representative, I shall appreciate your confidence, and will endeavour to discharee the duties to your satisfaction. I am, Ladie. and Gentlenyn, Your obedient Servant, WILLIAM FIREPOINT. Latchford, October 17th, 1893.</p>
