        <p>MUNICIPAL ELECTION, 1893. TO THE BURGESSES OF THE HOWLEY WARD, LADIES AND GENTLEMEN. The term of my services R 3 a Gonne!nor in the How- ley Ward (to which position you returned me by a large majority three years ago) will expire on the 31st inst. and I have been asked by burgesses of both political part lea, to allow myself to be again nominated for the ward, and after con- sideration I have consented to again offer my services to you as your representative in the Council. During the term ot my offlee I have endeavoured to fulfil my duty to th best of my ability in furthering and support- ing all claims for the advancement of the town and for the reduction of the rates. I have cons stently o. posed the increase of salsries of the officials of the Corporatien when I was of the opinion that they ve. re already well enough paid for their services, and on the Other hand I have never hesi- tated to support an increase of the salaries where I thought the officials descry edit for the onerous duties they had to per- form. Three years ago! promised to get remedied the dangerous condition of Howley Brook and owing to my persistently raisingt be:question in the Council and elsewhere, the danger has been entirely removed. I do not know that it is necessary to mention any other of the subjects that I have endeavoured to carry to a successful issue in the Committee or in the Council as you have had ample opportunity of judging of my merits from the position which I again seek to occupy. If you do me the honour of again returning me I shall not regard any matter that com Is before me from a party point of view, as I do not agree with partypolitics being introduced Into Municipal matters, but I shall endeavour to do my duty in the future as I have done in the past. I am, Ladies and Gentlemen. Your obedient Servant, ART/IE7R BROWNE. I Cloth Hall Yard, Warrington, 1 19th October, 1893.</p>
