        <p>ALWAYS SEEKING. T,lie children who have a special aptitude for some Inanch of knowledge and who are determined to develop it, are thosa who seem to notice what estapea the gazo of ordinary men and women. James, Ferguson, the Scotch astronomer, was very anxious, even its a litle boy, to understand the mechanism of watches. His father re- fused to allow him to " play with" lila wateh, and se James waited until a stranger called at the house one day to ask his way. " Will you be good enough to tell me what time it is ?" asked the boy. The gentleman told him. " Would you be willingthat I should 10-dc at your watch continued Ferguson. "Certainly,' re- plied the gentleman. The- boy took Vie watch eagerly. After he bad examined it for a moment, his next ques- tion came : " What makes that box go round ?" " A steel spring," replied the owner. " How can a steel spring in a box turn it round -so as to wind up all the chain ?" The gentleman explained the procecs. " don't see through it yet," answered young Ferguson. " Well, now," said the traveller, who had become deeply interested in the boy, " take a long, thin piece of whalebone, hold or.° end of it fast between your thumb and forefinger, and wind it round your , linger. It will then attempt to unwind itself, and if you fix the other end of it tont() inside of a sin ill hoop, and leave it to itself, it will turn the hoop round and round, and wind up a thread tied to the outside. "I see it ! I sea it ! exclaimed Ferguson,enthusiastically. " Thank you very, very much." It was not long before he had made a. wooden watch, which lie enclosed in a case about the size of a tea cop. The boy was afterwards I set at werk to watch sheep at night. H&amp;c he ha:: I nothing to watch but the stars, sad his future eminence in astronomy was, lera way, founded upon his boyish I out-of-door observation. Blain Pascal, who wrote a 1 remarkable treatise on the laws of sound,was constantly observing the familiar occurrence about him, even as a boy. When he was only 10 years old he sat at the din- ner table one day striking his plate with his knife, and then listening to the sound. '' What are you doing wit that plate, lilaise ?" asked his sister. " Sep " he re replied. " When I strike Vie plate with 'my knife it rings. Hark I " Again lie called forth the sound. " When I grasp it with my bald, so," he continued, the sound ceases. I wonder why it is ? " Thus had both boys begun to study, each according to his own bent, with no urging from Ider people. PUBLIC NOTICB.—Not one kat I(UNDIIIMS of people of both sexes with wrecked and debilitated constitutions are yearly restored to sound and robutt health again by Messrs. Wilkinson. 3 and 4, Fitzallan Square, Sheffield. Write to thorn for advicefree when suffering tram weakness, loss of flesh. appetite, disturbed rest, pimples, blotches, indlges• tion, pallid skin, nervousness, and general debt Um. Post tive cure for all Blood and Skin Diseases, acquired or consti- tutional. Sixty year:' experience,. One address ;as above) for over 30 years. The '• A 1 Mixture," 4s. 93. the case. The Magic Mirror In envelope, four stamps Sufferers Weep points d with !Metric Belts should write to mat onee.</p>
